const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"An apartment in the Duke's palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke, Escalus, Lords and Attendants.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Escalus."],"id":2,"m":"Escalus."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["My lord."],"id":3,"m":"My lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Of government the properties to unfold","Would seem in me t' affect speech and discourse,","Since I am put to know that your own science","Exceeds, in that, the lists of all advice","My strength can give you. Then no more remains","But that, to your sufficiency, as your worth is able,","And let them work. The nature of our people,","Our city's institutions, and the terms","For common justice, you're as pregnant in","As art and practice hath enriched any","That we remember. There is our commission,","From which we would not have you warp.—Call hither,","I say, bid come before us, Angelo."],"id":4,"m":"To lecture you on the principles of government would only make me look as if I loved the sound of my own voice, since I know your own knowledge of it goes beyond any advice I could give. So all that is left is to add authority to your ability, and let the two work together. You know our people's character, our city's institutions and the workings of public justice as well as anyone I can remember, in both theory and practice. Here is your commission; do not stray from it. (To an Attendant) Go and tell Angelo to come here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant.","id":5},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["What figure of us think you he will bear?","For you must know we have with special soul","Elected him our absence to supply;","Lent him our terror, drest him with our love,","And given his deputation all the organs","Of our own power. What think you of it?"],"id":6,"m":"How do you think he will carry my role? You must know that I have deliberately chosen him to fill my place while I am away. I have lent him my power to punish, dressed him in my love, and given his deputyship every instrument of my own authority. What do you think of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["If any in Vienna be of worth","To undergo such ample grace and honour,","It is Lord Angelo."],"id":7,"m":"If anyone in Vienna is worthy of such great honour and favour, it is Lord Angelo."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo.","id":8},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Look where he comes."],"id":9,"m":"Look, here he comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Always obedient to your Grace's will,","I come to know your pleasure."],"id":10,"m":"Always ready to obey your Grace, I have come to hear what you want."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Angelo,","There is a kind of character in thy life","That to th' observer doth thy history","Fully unfold. Thyself and thy belongings","Are not thine own so proper as to waste","Thyself upon thy virtues, they on thee.","Heaven doth with us as we with torches do,","Not light them for themselves; for if our virtues","Did not go forth of us, 'twere all alike","As if we had them not. Spirits are not finely touched","But to fine issues; nor nature never lends","The smallest scruple of her excellence","But, like a thrifty goddess, she determines","Herself the glory of a creditor,","Both thanks and use. But I do bend my speech","To one that can my part in him advertise.","Hold, therefore, Angelo.","In our remove be thou at full ourself.","Mortality and mercy in Vienna","Live in thy tongue and heart. Old Escalus,","Though first in question, is thy secondary.","Take thy commission."],"id":11,"m":"Angelo, there is something in the way you live that tells anyone watching your whole story. You and your gifts do not belong to you so completely that you may spend yourself on your virtues, or they on you. Heaven treats us the way we treat torches: we do not light them for their own sake. If our virtues did not shine out from us, we might as well not have them. Fine spirits are given for fine purposes, and nature never lends even the smallest piece of her excellence without claiming, like a careful lender, both thanks and interest. But I am speaking to a man who could teach me my own part. So take this, Angelo. While I am away, be me in full. Death and mercy in Vienna live in your tongue and your heart. Old Escalus, though he was asked first, is your second in command. Take your commission."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Now, good my lord,","Let there be some more test made of my metal,","Before so noble and so great a figure","Be stamped upon it."],"id":12,"m":"But my lord, test my metal a little more before such a great and noble stamp is pressed onto it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["No more evasion.","We have with a leavened and prepared choice","Proceeded to you; therefore take your honours.","Our haste from hence is of so quick condition","That it prefers itself, and leaves unquestioned","Matters of needful value. We shall write to you,","As time and our concernings shall importune,","How it goes with us; and do look to know","What doth befall you here. So, fare you well.","To th' hopeful execution do I leave you","Of your commissions."],"id":13,"m":"No more excuses. I chose you after careful thought, so accept the honour. I have to leave so quickly that more important matters must wait. I will write to you when time and my affairs allow, telling you how things go with me, and I expect to hear what happens here. So, goodbye. I leave you to carry out your commissions, and I hope you do it well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Yet give leave, my lord,","That we may bring you something on the way."],"id":14,"m":"At least let us go with you part of the way, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["My haste may not admit it;","Nor need you, on mine honour, have to do","With any scruple. Your scope is as mine own,","So to enforce or qualify the laws","As to your soul seems good. Give me your hand;","I'll privily away. I love the people,","But do not like to stage me to their eyes.","Though it do well, I do not relish well","Their loud applause and Aves vehement;","Nor do I think the man of safe discretion","That does affect it. Once more, fare you well."],"id":15,"m":"I am in too much of a hurry for that, and on my honour you need have no doubts. Your power is as great as mine, to enforce the laws strictly or to soften them as your conscience thinks right. Give me your hand. I will slip away quietly. I love the people, but I do not like to parade myself in front of them. It may be well meant, but I take no pleasure in their loud cheering and shouting; and I do not think a man with any judgment seeks it. Once again, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["The heavens give safety to your purposes!"],"id":16,"m":"May heaven keep you safe in what you do!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Lead forth and bring you back in happiness."],"id":17,"m":"May it lead you out and bring you back happily."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I thank you. Fare you well."],"id":18,"m":"Thank you. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":19},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I shall desire you, sir, to give me leave","To have free speech with you; and it concerns me","To look into the bottom of my place.","A power I have, but of what strength and nature","I am not yet instructed."],"id":20,"m":"Sir, I would like to talk frankly with you. I need to understand exactly what my position is. I have been given power, but nobody has told me how far it reaches or what kind it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["'Tis so with me. Let us withdraw together,","And we may soon our satisfaction have","Touching that point."],"id":21,"m":"It is the same with me. Let us go somewhere private and soon settle the question."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I'll wait upon your honour."],"id":22,"m":"I am at your service."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":23},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"A street.","id":24},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucio and two other Gentlemen.","id":25},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["If the Duke, with the other dukes, come not to composition with the","King of Hungary, why then all the dukes fall upon the King."],"id":26,"m":"If the Duke and the other dukes do not come to terms with the King of Hungary, then all the dukes will attack the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Heaven grant us its peace, but not the King of Hungary's!"],"id":27,"m":"God give us peace, but not the King of Hungary's peace!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Amen."],"id":28,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Thou conclud'st like the sanctimonious pirate that went to sea with the","ten commandments, but scraped one out of the table."],"id":29,"m":"You end that like the holy-looking pirate who went to sea with the Ten Commandments but scratched one off the tablet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["\"Thou shalt not steal\"?"],"id":30,"m":"\"Thou shalt not steal\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Ay, that he razed."],"id":31,"m":"Yes, that's the one he rubbed out."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Why, 'twas a commandment to command the captain and all the rest from","their functions! They put forth to steal. There's not a soldier of us","all that, in the thanksgiving before meat, do relish the petition well","that prays for peace."],"id":32,"m":"Of course: that commandment would have put the captain and all his crew out of work. They set out to steal. There's not one soldier among us who enjoys the bit of grace before dinner that prays for peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I never heard any soldier dislike it."],"id":33,"m":"I never heard a soldier who disliked it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I believe thee; for I think thou never wast where grace was said."],"id":34,"m":"I believe you, because I don't think you've ever been anywhere grace was said."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["No? A dozen times at least."],"id":35,"m":"No? At least a dozen times."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["What? In metre?"],"id":36,"m":"What, in verse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["In any proportion or in any language."],"id":37,"m":"In any form and in any language."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I think, or in any religion."],"id":38,"m":"And in any religion, I suppose."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Ay, why not? Grace is grace, despite of all controversy; as, for","example, thou thyself art a wicked villain, despite of all grace."],"id":39,"m":"Yes, why not? Grace is grace whatever the arguments, just as you, for example, are a wicked villain whatever grace you have."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Well, there went but a pair of shears between us."],"id":40,"m":"Well, we're cut from the same cloth."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I grant, as there may between the lists and the velvet. Thou art the","list."],"id":41,"m":"Agreed, just as the plain edging and the velvet are cut from the same piece. You're the edging."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["And thou the velvet. Thou art good velvet; thou'rt a three-piled piece,","I warrant thee. I had as lief be a list of an English kersey as be","piled, as thou art piled, for a French velvet. Do I speak feelingly","now?"],"id":42,"m":"And you're the velvet. Good velvet too, thick triple pile, I'm sure. I'd rather be the plain edge of an English woollen than be bald like you, rubbed bare by French velvet. Have I touched a sore spot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I think thou dost, and indeed, with most painful feeling of thy speech.","I will, out of thine own confession, learn to begin thy health; but,","whilst I live, forget to drink after thee."],"id":43,"m":"I think you have, and you speak like a man in real pain. Since you've confessed it, I'll drink your health first, but as long as I live I'll never drink from the same cup after you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I think I have done myself wrong, have I not?"],"id":44,"m":"I think I've done myself harm there, haven't I?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes, that thou hast, whether thou art tainted or free."],"id":45,"m":"Yes, you have, whether you're infected or not."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Overdone, a Bawd.","id":46},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Behold, behold, where Madam Mitigation comes! I have purchased as many","diseases under her roof as come to—"],"id":47,"m":"Look, look, here comes Madam Mitigation! I've caught as many diseases under her roof as would add up to..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["To what, I pray?"],"id":48,"m":"To what, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Judge."],"id":49,"m":"Guess."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["To three thousand dolours a year."],"id":50,"m":"To three thousand dollars' worth of pain a year."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Ay, and more."],"id":51,"m":"Yes, and more."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["A French crown more."],"id":52,"m":"A French crown more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Thou art always figuring diseases in me, but thou art full of error; I","am sound."],"id":53,"m":"You're always imagining diseases in me, but you're wrong: I'm sound."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Nay, not, as one would say, healthy, but so sound as things that are","hollow. Thy bones are hollow. Impiety has made a feast of thee."],"id":54,"m":"No, not sound as in healthy, but sound like something hollow when you knock it. Your bones are hollow. Sin has feasted on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["How now, which of your hips has the most profound sciatica?"],"id":55,"m":"Well now, which of your hips has the worst sciatica?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Well, well! There's one yonder arrested and carried to prison was worth","five thousand of you all."],"id":56,"m":"Well, well. There's a man down there who has been arrested and taken to prison who was worth five thousand of you all."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Who's that, I pray thee?"],"id":57,"m":"Who is that, tell me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Marry, sir, that's Claudio, Signior Claudio."],"id":58,"m":"Why, sir, it's Claudio, Signior Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Claudio to prison? 'Tis not so."],"id":59,"m":"Claudio in prison? It can't be true."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Nay, but I know 'tis so. I saw him arrested, saw him carried away; and,","which is more, within these three days his head to be chopped off."],"id":60,"m":"No, I know it's true. I saw him arrested and taken away. What's more, in three days' time his head is to be chopped off."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["But, after all this fooling, I would not have it so. Art thou sure of","this?"],"id":61,"m":"All joking aside, I would hate that to happen. Are you sure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["I am too sure of it. And it is for getting Madam Julietta with child."],"id":62,"m":"I'm only too sure. And it's for getting Madam Julietta pregnant."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Believe me, this may be. He promised to meet me two hours since, and he","was ever precise in promise-keeping."],"id":63,"m":"Believe me, this could be true. He promised to meet me two hours ago, and he always keeps his appointments exactly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Besides, you know, it draws something near to the speech we had to such","a purpose."],"id":64,"m":"Besides, you know, it fits with what we were saying earlier."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["But most of all agreeing with the proclamation."],"id":65,"m":"And most of all it fits the new proclamation."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Away! Let's go learn the truth of it."],"id":66,"m":"Come on! Let's go and find out the truth."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucio and Gentlemen.","id":67},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Thus, what with the war, what with the sweat, what with the gallows,","and what with poverty, I am custom-shrunk."],"id":68,"m":"So what with the war, the sweating sickness, the gallows and poverty, I'm losing all my customers."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pompey.","id":69},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","cont":1,"lines":["How now? What's the news with you?"],"id":70,"m":"Well? What's your news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Yonder man is carried to prison."],"id":71,"m":"That man over there is being taken to prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Well, what has he done?"],"id":72,"m":"Well, what has he done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["A woman."],"id":73,"m":"A woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["But what's his offence?"],"id":74,"m":"But what is his crime?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Groping for trouts in a peculiar river."],"id":75,"m":"Tickling for trout in a private river."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["What? Is there a maid with child by him?"],"id":76,"m":"What? Has he made a girl pregnant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["No, but there's a woman with maid by him. You have not heard of the","proclamation, have you?"],"id":77,"m":"No, but he's made a woman out of a girl. Haven't you heard about the proclamation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["What proclamation, man?"],"id":78,"m":"What proclamation, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["All houses in the suburbs of Vienna must be plucked down."],"id":79,"m":"All the brothels in the suburbs of Vienna are to be torn down."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["And what shall become of those in the city?"],"id":80,"m":"And what will happen to the ones in the city?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["They shall stand for seed. They had gone down too, but that a wise","burgher put in for them."],"id":81,"m":"They'll be left standing, for breeding. They'd have come down too, but a wise citizen put in a word for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["But shall all our houses of resort in the suburbs be pulled down?"],"id":82,"m":"But are all our houses of business in the suburbs to be pulled down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["To the ground, mistress."],"id":83,"m":"Right to the ground, mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Why, here's a change indeed in the commonwealth! What shall become of","me?"],"id":84,"m":"Well, here's a change in the state of things! What will become of me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Come, fear not you. Good counsellors lack no clients. Though you change","your place, you need not change your trade. I'll be your tapster still.","Courage, there will be pity taken on you. You that have worn your eyes","almost out in the service, you will be considered."],"id":85,"m":"Come on, don't worry. A good lawyer never lacks clients. You may change your address, but you needn't change your trade. I'll still be your barman. Cheer up, people will take pity on you. You've almost worn out your eyes in the service; you'll be looked after."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost, Claudio, Juliet and Officers.","id":86},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["What's to do here, Thomas Tapster? Let's withdraw."],"id":87,"m":"What's going on here, Thomas the barman? Let's get out of the way."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Here comes Signior Claudio, led by the Provost to prison. And there's","Madam Juliet."],"id":88,"m":"Here comes Signior Claudio, being taken to prison by the Provost. And there's Madam Juliet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bawd and Pompey.","id":89},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Fellow, why dost thou show me thus to the world?","Bear me to prison, where I am committed."],"id":90,"m":"Why do you parade me in front of everyone like this, fellow? Take me to the prison I've been sent to."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I do it not in evil disposition,","But from Lord Angelo by special charge."],"id":91,"m":"I don't do it out of spite, but on Lord Angelo's particular orders."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Thus can the demi-god Authority","Make us pay down for our offence by weight.","The words of heaven; on whom it will, it will;","On whom it will not, so; yet still 'tis just."],"id":92,"m":"So the half-god Authority can make us pay for our crimes in full measure. It is like the word of heaven: it strikes whoever it chooses and spares whoever it chooses, and still it is always just."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucio and two Gentlemen.","id":93},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Why, how now, Claudio? Whence comes this restraint?"],"id":94,"m":"Claudio, what's this? Why are you under arrest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["From too much liberty, my Lucio, liberty.","As surfeit is the father of much fast,","So every scope by the immoderate use","Turns to restraint. Our natures do pursue,","Like rats that ravin down their proper bane,","A thirsty evil; and when we drink, we die."],"id":95,"m":"From too much freedom, my Lucio, freedom. Just as overeating leads to fasting, so every liberty, used without limit, turns into restraint. Our natures chase after a thirst for evil the way rats greedily swallow the poison meant for them; and when we drink, we die."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["If I could speak so wisely under an arrest, I would send for certain of","my creditors; and yet, to say the truth, I had as lief have the foppery","of freedom as the morality of imprisonment. What's thy offence,","Claudio?"],"id":96,"m":"If I could talk that wisely under arrest, I would send for some of my creditors. But to tell the truth, I'd rather have the foolishness of freedom than the wisdom of prison. What have you done, Claudio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["What but to speak of would offend again."],"id":97,"m":"Something I would offend again just by talking about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["What, is't murder?"],"id":98,"m":"What, is it murder?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["No."],"id":99,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Lechery?"],"id":100,"m":"Lechery?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Call it so."],"id":101,"m":"Call it that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Away, sir; you must go."],"id":102,"m":"Come along, sir; you have to go."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["One word, good friend.—Lucio, a word with you."],"id":103,"m":"Just one word, good friend. Lucio, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["A hundred, if they'll do you any good. Is lechery so looked after?"],"id":104,"m":"A hundred, if they'll help you. Is lechery so closely watched now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Thus stands it with me: upon a true contract","I got possession of Julietta's bed.","You know the lady; she is fast my wife,","Save that we do the denunciation lack","Of outward order. This we came not to","Only for propagation of a dower","Remaining in the coffer of her friends,","From whom we thought it meet to hide our love","Till time had made them for us. But it chances","The stealth of our most mutual entertainment","With character too gross is writ on Juliet."],"id":105,"m":"This is my situation. Under a binding contract of marriage, I slept with Julietta. You know the lady. She is truly my wife, except that we have not made the public announcement in church. We held back from that only so that her dowry, which is still held by her relatives, would come to us; we thought it best to hide our love from them until time had won them over to us. But as it happens, the secret of our love for each other is now written too plainly on Juliet's body."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["With child, perhaps?"],"id":106,"m":"She's pregnant, perhaps?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Unhappily, even so.","And the new deputy now for the Duke—","Whether it be the fault and glimpse of newness,","Or whether that the body public be","A horse whereon the governor doth ride,","Who, newly in the seat, that it may know","He can command, lets it straight feel the spur;","Whether the tyranny be in his place,","Or in his eminence that fills it up,","I stagger in—but this new governor","Awakes me all the enrolled penalties","Which have, like unscoured armour, hung by th' wall","So long that nineteen zodiacs have gone round,","And none of them been worn; and for a name","Now puts the drowsy and neglected act","Freshly on me. 'Tis surely for a name."],"id":107,"m":"Unfortunately, yes. And the Duke's new deputy, whether it is the mistake and glare of being new, or whether he thinks the people are a horse for the ruler to ride, and that a new rider must use the spur at once to show he is in charge, whether the tyranny lies in the office or in the man who holds it, I can't tell; but this new governor has woken up every penalty in the statute book that has hung unused on the wall like rusty armour for nineteen years. To make a name for himself he has dug out this sleepy, forgotten law and applied it fresh to me. It must be to make a name."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I warrant it is. And thy head stands so tickle on thy shoulders that a","milkmaid, if she be in love, may sigh it off. Send after the Duke, and","appeal to him."],"id":108,"m":"I'm sure it is. And your head sits so loosely on your shoulders that a lovesick milkmaid could sigh it off. Send a message to the Duke and appeal to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I have done so, but he's not to be found.","I prithee, Lucio, do me this kind service:","This day my sister should the cloister enter,","And there receive her approbation.","Acquaint her with the danger of my state;","Implore her, in my voice, that she make friends","To the strict deputy; bid herself assay him.","I have great hope in that. For in her youth","There is a prone and speechless dialect","Such as moves men; beside, she hath prosperous art","When she will play with reason and discourse,","And well she can persuade."],"id":109,"m":"I have, but he can't be found. Please, Lucio, do me this favour. Today my sister is due to enter the convent and begin her trial as a novice. Tell her what danger I'm in. Beg her, from me, to make friends with the strict deputy and try to win him over herself. I have great hopes of that. In her youth there is a silent, willing language that moves men; and besides, she has a real gift when she uses argument and reason, and she can be very persuasive."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I pray she may, as well for the encouragement of the like, which else","would stand under grievous imposition, as for the enjoying of thy life,","who I would be sorry should be thus foolishly lost at a game of","tick-tack. I'll to her."],"id":110,"m":"I hope she can, both to encourage others like you, who would otherwise be in serious danger, and so you can keep your life. I would hate to see it thrown away so stupidly over a game of tick-tack. I'll go to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I thank you, good friend Lucio."],"id":111,"m":"Thank you, good friend Lucio."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Within two hours."],"id":112,"m":"Within two hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Come, officer, away."],"id":113,"m":"Come, officer, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":114},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"A monastery.","id":115},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke and Friar Thomas.","id":116},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["No, holy father, throw away that thought;","Believe not that the dribbling dart of love","Can pierce a complete bosom. Why I desire thee","To give me secret harbour hath a purpose","More grave and wrinkled than the aims and ends","Of burning youth."],"id":117,"m":"No, holy father, put that idea out of your head. Don't think love's feeble little arrow can pierce a heart as armoured as mine. My reason for asking you to hide me secretly is far more serious and mature than the aims of hot-blooded young men."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR THOMAS","lines":["May your Grace speak of it?"],"id":118,"m":"May your Grace tell me what it is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["My holy sir, none better knows than you","How I have ever loved the life removed,","And held in idle price to haunt assemblies","Where youth, and cost, a witless bravery keeps.","I have delivered to Lord Angelo,","A man of stricture and firm abstinence,","My absolute power and place here in Vienna,","And he supposes me travelled to Poland;","For so I have strewed it in the common ear,","And so it is received. Now, pious sir,","You will demand of me why I do this?"],"id":119,"m":"My holy sir, nobody knows better than you how I have always loved a quiet, private life, and thought little of going where young people gather and spend money on empty show. I have handed my complete power and position here in Vienna to Lord Angelo, a man of strict discipline and firm self-denial, and he thinks I have gone to Poland. That is the story I have spread among the people, and they believe it. Now, good sir, you will want to know why I am doing this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR THOMAS","lines":["Gladly, my lord."],"id":120,"m":"I would, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["We have strict statutes and most biting laws,","The needful bits and curbs to headstrong weeds,","Which for this fourteen years we have let slip,","Even like an o'ergrown lion in a cave","That goes not out to prey. Now, as fond fathers,","Having bound up the threat'ning twigs of birch,","Only to stick it in their children's sight","For terror, not to use, in time the rod","Becomes more mocked than feared: so our decrees,","Dead to infliction, to themselves are dead,","And liberty plucks justice by the nose,","The baby beats the nurse, and quite athwart","Goes all decorum."],"id":121,"m":"We have strict statutes and harsh laws, the necessary bits and bridles for wild horses, which for fourteen years we have let lapse, like an overgrown lion in a cave that never goes out to hunt. Foolish fathers tie up a threatening bundle of birch twigs and only display it to frighten their children, never to use it, so that in time the rod is mocked instead of feared. In the same way our laws, never enforced, are as good as dead. Freedom pulls justice by the nose, the baby hits the nurse, and all good order is turned upside down."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR THOMAS","lines":["It rested in your Grace","To unloose this tied-up justice when you pleased;","And it in you more dreadful would have seemed","Than in Lord Angelo."],"id":122,"m":"Your Grace had the power to set this tied-up justice loose whenever you wanted, and it would have seemed more frightening coming from you than from Lord Angelo."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I do fear, too dreadful.","Sith 'twas my fault to give the people scope,","'Twould be my tyranny to strike and gall them","For what I bid them do; for we bid this be done","When evil deeds have their permissive pass","And not the punishment. Therefore, indeed, my father,","I have on Angelo imposed the office;","Who may in th' ambush of my name strike home,","And yet my nature never in the fight","To do in slander. And to behold his sway,","I will, as 'twere a brother of your order,","Visit both prince and people. Therefore, I prithee,","Supply me with the habit, and instruct me","How I may formally in person bear","Like a true friar. Moe reasons for this action","At our more leisure shall I render you;","Only, this one: Lord Angelo is precise;","Stands at a guard with envy; scarce confesses","That his blood flows or that his appetite","Is more to bread than stone. Hence shall we see,","If power change purpose, what our seemers be."],"id":123,"m":"Too frightening, I fear. Since it was my fault that the people were given such freedom, it would be tyranny for me to punish and hurt them for what I let them do; for when we let wrongdoing pass unpunished, we as good as order it. That, father, is why I have given the office to Angelo. He can strike hard while hiding behind my name, and my own reputation will not be damaged in the fight. And to watch how he rules, I will visit both prince and people as if I were a friar of your order. So please, give me the habit and teach me how to carry myself like a real friar. I will give you more reasons for this when we have more time. Just this one: Lord Angelo is strict. He stands on guard against malice and will hardly admit that blood runs in his veins, or that he would rather eat bread than stone. So we shall see, if power changes a man's aims, what these people who only seem good are really like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":124},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"A nunnery.","id":125},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Isabella and Francisca, a Nun.","id":126},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["And have you nuns no farther privileges?"],"id":127,"m":"Do you nuns have no other privileges?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCISCA","lines":["Are not these large enough?"],"id":128,"m":"Aren't these generous enough?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Yes, truly; I speak not as desiring more,","But rather wishing a more strict restraint","Upon the sisterhood, the votarists of Saint Clare."],"id":129,"m":"Yes, really. I'm not asking for more; I'd rather wish for stricter rules for the sisterhood, the devoted servants of Saint Clare."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["[Within] Ho! Peace be in this place!"],"id":130,"m":"(Calling from outside) Hello! Peace be in this place!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Who's that which calls?"],"id":131,"m":"Who is that calling?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCISCA","lines":["It is a man's voice. Gentle Isabella,","Turn you the key, and know his business of him;","You may, I may not; you are yet unsworn.","When you have vowed, you must not speak with men","But in the presence of the prioress;","Then, if you speak, you must not show your face;","Or if you show your face, you must not speak.","He calls again. I pray you answer him."],"id":132,"m":"It's a man's voice. Gentle Isabella, unlock the door and find out what he wants. You may; I may not, because you have not taken your vows yet. Once you have, you may not talk to men except in the presence of the prioress, and then if you speak you must not show your face, or if you show your face you must not speak. He's calling again. Please answer him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Francisca.","id":133},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Peace and prosperity! Who is't that calls?"],"id":134,"m":"Peace and prosperity! Who is calling?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucio.","id":135},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Hail, virgin, if you be, as those cheek-roses","Proclaim you are no less. Can you so stead me","As bring me to the sight of Isabella,","A novice of this place, and the fair sister","To her unhappy brother Claudio?"],"id":136,"m":"Hello, maiden, if that is what you are, as those rosy cheeks say you are. Can you help me to see Isabella, a novice here and the beautiful sister of her unhappy brother Claudio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Why \"her unhappy brother\"? let me ask,","The rather for I now must make you know","I am that Isabella, and his sister."],"id":137,"m":"Why \"her unhappy brother\", may I ask? I ask because I must tell you that I am that Isabella, and his sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Gentle and fair, your brother kindly greets you.","Not to be weary with you, he's in prison."],"id":138,"m":"Gentle and beautiful lady, your brother sends you his love. To keep it short, he is in prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Woe me! For what?"],"id":139,"m":"Oh no! What for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["For that which, if myself might be his judge,","He should receive his punishment in thanks:","He hath got his friend with child."],"id":140,"m":"For something that, if I were his judge, would earn him thanks instead of punishment. He has made his girlfriend pregnant."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Sir, make me not your story."],"id":141,"m":"Sir, don't make fun of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["'Tis true.","I would not, though 'tis my familiar sin","With maids to seem the lapwing, and to jest,","Tongue far from heart, play with all virgins so.","I hold you as a thing enskied and sainted","By your renouncement an immortal spirit,","And to be talked with in sincerity,","As with a saint."],"id":142,"m":"It's true. It is my usual fault to play the lapwing with girls, teasing them with words that are far from my heart, but I would never treat all maidens like that. I think of you as someone set in heaven and made a saint, an immortal spirit because you have given up the world, and to be spoken to honestly, as one would speak to a saint."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["You do blaspheme the good in mocking me."],"id":143,"m":"You insult goodness by mocking me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Do not believe it. Fewness and truth, 'tis thus:","Your brother and his lover have embraced;","As those that feed grow full, as blossoming time","That from the seedness the bare fallow brings","To teeming foison, even so her plenteous womb","Expresseth his full tilth and husbandry."],"id":144,"m":"Don't think that. In short, and truly: your brother and his lover have slept together. Just as people who eat grow full, and as springtime turns the bare ploughed field into a rich harvest, so her fruitful womb shows how well he has tilled and farmed."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Someone with child by him? My cousin Juliet?"],"id":145,"m":"Someone is pregnant by him? My cousin Juliet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Is she your cousin?"],"id":146,"m":"Is she your cousin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Adoptedly, as school-maids change their names","By vain though apt affection."],"id":147,"m":"Only by choice, the way schoolgirls give each other new names out of silly but natural affection."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["She it is."],"id":148,"m":"Yes, it's her."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, let him marry her!"],"id":149,"m":"Oh, let him marry her!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["This is the point.","The Duke is very strangely gone from hence;","Bore many gentlemen, myself being one,","In hand, and hope of action; but we do learn,","By those that know the very nerves of state,","His givings-out were of an infinite distance","From his true-meant design. Upon his place,","And with full line of his authority,","Governs Lord Angelo; a man whose blood","Is very snow-broth; one who never feels","The wanton stings and motions of the sense;","But doth rebate and blunt his natural edge","With profits of the mind, study and fast.","He, to give fear to use and liberty,","Which have for long run by the hideous law","As mice by lions, hath picked out an act,","Under whose heavy sense your brother's life","Falls into forfeit. He arrests him on it,","And follows close the rigour of the statute","To make him an example. All hope is gone,","Unless you have the grace by your fair prayer","To soften Angelo. And that's my pith of business","'Twixt you and your poor brother."],"id":150,"m":"That's the point. The Duke has left here very mysteriously. He led many gentlemen, me among them, to expect military action, but we hear from people who know the inner workings of the state that what he said was miles away from what he really intended. Lord Angelo now rules in his place, with all his authority. He is a man whose blood is melted snow, who never feels the playful stings and impulses of the senses, but dulls and blunts his natural desires with improving the mind, study and fasting. To frighten people out of the habit of freedom, which has long run past the terrible law like mice past lions, he has picked out a statute under whose harsh terms your brother's life is forfeit. He has arrested him under it and is following the strict letter of the law to make an example of him. There is no hope left, unless you can soften Angelo with your sweet pleading. And that is the heart of my business between you and your poor brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Doth he so","Seek his life?"],"id":151,"m":"Does he really want his life?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Has censured him already;","And, as I hear, the Provost hath a warrant","For's execution."],"id":152,"m":"He has sentenced him already, and I hear the Provost has a warrant for his execution."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Alas, what poor ability's in me","To do him good?"],"id":153,"m":"Oh, what little power I have to help him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Assay the power you have."],"id":154,"m":"Try what power you have."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["My power? Alas, I doubt."],"id":155,"m":"My power? I'm afraid I doubt it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Our doubts are traitors,","And make us lose the good we oft might win","By fearing to attempt. Go to Lord Angelo,","And let him learn to know, when maidens sue,","Men give like gods; but when they weep and kneel,","All their petitions are as freely theirs","As they themselves would owe them."],"id":156,"m":"Our doubts are traitors. They make us lose the good we might often win by making us afraid to try. Go to Lord Angelo and let him learn that when young women plead, men give like gods; and when they weep and kneel, they get whatever they ask for as if it were already theirs."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I'll see what I can do."],"id":157,"m":"I'll see what I can do."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["But speedily."],"id":158,"m":"But hurry."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I will about it straight;","No longer staying but to give the Mother","Notice of my affair. I humbly thank you.","Commend me to my brother. Soon at night","I'll send him certain word of my success."],"id":159,"m":"I'll go at once, stopping only to tell the Mother Superior what I'm doing. Thank you very much. Give my love to my brother. Early this evening I'll send him word of how I get on."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I take my leave of you."],"id":160,"m":"I'll say goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Good sir, adieu."],"id":161,"m":"Goodbye, good sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":162},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"A hall in Angelo's house.","id":163},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo, Escalus, Servants, and a Justice.","id":164},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["We must not make a scarecrow of the law,","Setting it up to fear the birds of prey,","And let it keep one shape till custom make it","Their perch, and not their terror."],"id":165,"m":"We must not turn the law into a scarecrow, put up to frighten the birds of prey, and leave it standing the same until they get used to it and use it as a perch instead of fearing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Ay, but yet","Let us be keen, and rather cut a little","Than fall and bruise to death. Alas, this gentleman,","Whom I would save, had a most noble father.","Let but your honour know,","Whom I believe to be most strait in virtue,","That, in the working of your own affections,","Had time cohered with place, or place with wishing,","Or that the resolute acting of your blood","Could have attained th' effect of your own purpose,","Whether you had not sometime in your life","Erred in this point which now you censure him,","And pulled the law upon you."],"id":166,"m":"Yes, but still, let us be sharp and cut a little rather than knock a man down and crush him to death. This gentleman, whom I would like to save, had a very noble father. Just think, your honour, though I believe you are perfectly upright: if in the heat of your own feelings time had fitted with place, or place with desire, or if your passion had been able to get what it wanted, consider whether you might not once in your life have done the very thing you are condemning him for, and brought the law down on yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["'Tis one thing to be tempted, Escalus,","Another thing to fall. I not deny","The jury passing on the prisoner's life","May in the sworn twelve have a thief or two","Guiltier than him they try. What's open made to justice,","That justice seizes. What knows the laws","That thieves do pass on thieves? 'Tis very pregnant,","The jewel that we find, we stoop and take 't,","Because we see it; but what we do not see,","We tread upon, and never think of it.","You may not so extenuate his offence","For I have had such faults; but rather tell me,","When I that censure him do so offend,","Let mine own judgement pattern out my death,","And nothing come in partial. Sir, he must die."],"id":167,"m":"Being tempted is one thing, Escalus; falling is another. I do not deny that a jury of twelve men deciding a prisoner's life may include a thief or two who are guiltier than the man they are trying. Justice seizes what is brought before it. What does the law know about thieves sentencing thieves? It is obvious: if we see a jewel, we bend down and pick it up because we see it; but what we don't see, we tread on without a thought. You cannot excuse his crime because I have had such faults. Instead, tell me this: if I who sentence him ever commit the same offence, let my own judgment set the pattern for my death, and let nothing soften it. Sir, he must die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost.","id":168},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Be it as your wisdom will."],"id":169,"m":"As your wisdom wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Where is the Provost?"],"id":170,"m":"Where is the Provost?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Here, if it like your honour."],"id":171,"m":"Here, if it please your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["See that Claudio","Be executed by nine tomorrow morning.","Bring him his confessor, let him be prepared,","For that's the utmost of his pilgrimage."],"id":172,"m":"Make sure Claudio is executed by nine o'clock tomorrow morning. Bring him his confessor and let him be prepared, because that is the end of his journey."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Provost.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Well, heaven forgive him; and forgive us all.","Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall.","Some run from brakes of vice, and answer none,","And some condemned for a fault alone."],"id":174,"m":"Well, heaven forgive him, and forgive us all. Some rise through sin, and some fall through virtue. Some escape from thickets of vice and never answer for it, and some are condemned for a single fault."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Elbow and Officers with Froth and Pompey.","id":175},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Come, bring them away. If these be good people in a commonweal that do","nothing but use their abuses in common houses, I know no law. Bring","them away."],"id":176,"m":"Come on, bring them along. If these are good citizens of the commonwealth, who do nothing but carry on their abuses in common brothels, then I know nothing about the law. Bring them along."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["How now, sir, what's your name? And what's the matter?"],"id":177,"m":"Well, sir, what's your name? And what's the problem?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["If it please your honour, I am the poor Duke's constable, and my name","is Elbow. I do lean upon justice, sir, and do bring in here before your","good honour two notorious benefactors."],"id":178,"m":"If it please your honour, I am the poor Duke's constable, and my name is Elbow. I lean on justice, sir, and I bring here before your honour two notorious benefactors (he means malefactors, criminals)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Benefactors? Well, what benefactors are they? Are they not malefactors?"],"id":179,"m":"Benefactors? Well, what benefactors are they? Don't you mean malefactors?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["If it please your honour, I know not well what they are, but precise","villains they are, that I am sure of, and void of all profanation in","the world that good Christians ought to have."],"id":180,"m":"If it please your honour, I don't really know what they are, but they're downright villains, I'm sure of that, and empty of all the profanity (he means piety) that good Christians ought to have."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["This comes off well. Here's a wise officer."],"id":181,"m":"This is going well. What a wise officer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Go to. What quality are they of? Elbow is your name? Why dost thou not","speak, Elbow?"],"id":182,"m":"Come on. What sort of people are they? Your name is Elbow? Why don't you speak, Elbow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["He cannot, sir. He's out at elbow."],"id":183,"m":"He can't, sir. He's out at elbow (worn out, at a loss)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["What are you, sir?"],"id":184,"m":"And what are you, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["He, sir? A tapster, sir; parcel bawd; one that serves a bad woman;","whose house, sir, was, as they say, plucked down in the suburbs; and","now she professes a hot-house, which, I think is a very ill house too."],"id":185,"m":"Him, sir? A barman, sir, and part pimp. He works for a bad woman whose house, sir, was, as they say, pulled down in the suburbs; and now she claims to run a bath-house, which I think is a very bad house too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["How know you that?"],"id":186,"m":"How do you know that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["My wife, sir, whom I detest before heaven and your honour—"],"id":187,"m":"My wife, sir, whom I detest (he means protest) before heaven and your honour..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["How? Thy wife?"],"id":188,"m":"What? Your wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Ay, sir, whom I thank heaven is an honest woman—"],"id":189,"m":"Yes, sir, and I thank heaven she is an honest woman..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Dost thou detest her therefore?"],"id":190,"m":"So you detest her for that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["I say, sir, I will detest myself also, as well as she, that this house,","if it be not a bawd's house, it is pity of her life, for it is a","naughty house."],"id":191,"m":"I say, sir, I will detest myself as well as her that this house, if it isn't a brothel, it's a shame she's alive, because it's a wicked house."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["How dost thou know that, constable?"],"id":192,"m":"How do you know that, constable?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Marry, sir, by my wife, who, if she had been a woman cardinally given,","might have been accused in fornication, adultery, and all uncleanliness","there."],"id":193,"m":"Why, sir, through my wife, who, if she had been a woman cardinally (he means carnally) inclined, might have been accused of fornication, adultery and every kind of filth there."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["By the woman's means?"],"id":194,"m":"Through the woman's doing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Ay, sir, by Mistress Overdone's means; but as she spit in his face, so","she defied him."],"id":195,"m":"Yes, sir, through Mistress Overdone's doing. But since she spat in his face, she defied him."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, if it please your honour, this is not so."],"id":196,"m":"Sir, if it please your honour, that's not true."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Prove it before these varlets here, thou honourable man, prove it."],"id":197,"m":"Prove it in front of these villains here, you honourable man, prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["[To Angelo] Do you hear how he misplaces?"],"id":198,"m":"(To Angelo) Do you hear how he gets his words in the wrong place?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, she came in great with child; and longing, saving your honour's","reverence, for stewed prunes; sir, we had but two in the house, which","at that very distant time stood, as it were, in a fruit dish, a dish of","some threepence; your honours have seen such dishes; they are not china","dishes, but very good dishes—"],"id":199,"m":"Sir, she came in heavily pregnant, and longing, begging your honour's pardon, for stewed prunes. Sir, we had only two in the house, which at that very distant time stood, as it were, in a fruit dish, a dish worth about threepence. Your honours have seen such dishes; they aren't china dishes, but very good dishes..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Go to, go to. No matter for the dish, sir."],"id":200,"m":"Come on, come on. Never mind the dish, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["No, indeed, sir, not of a pin; you are therein in the right. But to the","point. As I say, this Mistress Elbow, being, as I say, with child, and","being great-bellied, and longing, as I said, for prunes; and having but","two in the dish, as I said, Master Froth here, this very man, having","eaten the rest, as I said, and, as I say, paying for them very","honestly; for, as you know, Master Froth, I could not give you","threepence again—"],"id":201,"m":"No, indeed, sir, it doesn't matter a pin; you're quite right there. But to the point. As I say, this Mistress Elbow being, as I say, pregnant, and big-bellied, and longing, as I said, for prunes, and there being only two in the dish, as I said, Master Froth here, this very man, having eaten the rest, as I said, and, as I say, paying for them very honestly, for, as you know, Master Froth, I couldn't give you threepence change..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["No, indeed."],"id":202,"m":"No, indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Very well. You being then, if you be remembered, cracking the stones of","the foresaid prunes—"],"id":203,"m":"Very well. You were then, if you remember, cracking the stones of the said prunes..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["Ay, so I did indeed."],"id":204,"m":"Yes, so I was."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Why, very well. I telling you then, if you be remembered, that such a","one and such a one were past cure of the thing you wot of, unless they","kept very good diet, as I told you—"],"id":205,"m":"Why, very well. I was telling you then, if you remember, that so-and-so and so-and-so were past curing of the thing you know about, unless they kept to a very careful diet, as I told you..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["All this is true."],"id":206,"m":"All this is true."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Why, very well then—"],"id":207,"m":"Why, very well then..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Come, you are a tedious fool. To the purpose. What was done to Elbow's","wife that he hath cause to complain of? Come me to what was done to","her."],"id":208,"m":"Come on, you're a tiresome fool. Get to the point. What was done to Elbow's wife that he has reason to complain about? Get me to what was done to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, your honour cannot come to that yet."],"id":209,"m":"Sir, your honour can't come to that yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["No, sir, nor I mean it not."],"id":210,"m":"No, sir, and I don't intend to."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, but you shall come to it, by your honour's leave. And I beseech","you, look into Master Froth here, sir, a man of fourscore pound a year;","whose father died at Hallowmas—was't not at Hallowmas, Master Froth?"],"id":211,"m":"But you shall come to it, sir, with your honour's permission. And I beg you, take a look at Master Froth here, sir, a man with eighty pounds a year, whose father died at Hallowmas. Wasn't it at Hallowmas, Master Froth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["All-hallond Eve."],"id":212,"m":"On Halloween."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Why, very well. I hope here be truths. He, sir, sitting, as I say, in a","lower chair, sir—'twas in the Bunch of Grapes, where, indeed, you have","a delight to sit, have you not?"],"id":213,"m":"Why, very well. I hope these are truths. He, sir, sitting, as I say, in a low chair, sir, it was in the Bunch of Grapes room, where you do like to sit, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["I have so, because it is an open room, and good for winter."],"id":214,"m":"I do, because it's an open room and good in winter."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Why, very well then. I hope here be truths."],"id":215,"m":"Why, very well then. I hope these are truths."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["This will last out a night in Russia","When nights are longest there. I'll take my leave,","And leave you to the hearing of the cause;","Hoping you'll find good cause to whip them all."],"id":216,"m":"This will last as long as a night in Russia when the nights there are longest. I'll leave and let you hear the case, hoping you'll find good reason to whip them all."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I think no less. Good morrow to your lordship."],"id":217,"m":"I expect so. Good day, your lordship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Angelo.","id":218},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","cont":1,"lines":["Now, sir, come on. What was done to Elbow's wife, once more?"],"id":219,"m":"Now, sir, go on. What was done to Elbow's wife, once more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Once, sir? There was nothing done to her once."],"id":220,"m":"Once, sir? Nothing was done to her once."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["I beseech you, sir, ask him what this man did to my wife."],"id":221,"m":"I beg you, sir, ask him what this man did to my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I beseech your honour, ask me."],"id":222,"m":"I beg your honour, ask me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Well, sir, what did this gentleman to her?"],"id":223,"m":"Well, sir, what did this gentleman do to her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I beseech you, sir, look in this gentleman's face. Good Master Froth,","look upon his honour; 'tis for a good purpose.—Doth your honour mark","his face?"],"id":224,"m":"I beg you, sir, look at this gentleman's face. Good Master Froth, look at his honour; it's for a good reason. Does your honour notice his face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Ay, sir, very well."],"id":225,"m":"Yes, sir, very well."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Nay, I beseech you, mark it well."],"id":226,"m":"No, I beg you, look at it carefully."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Well, I do so."],"id":227,"m":"Well, I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Doth your honour see any harm in his face?"],"id":228,"m":"Does your honour see any harm in his face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Why, no."],"id":229,"m":"Why, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I'll be supposed upon a book, his face is the worst thing about him.","Good, then, if his face be the worst thing about him, how could Master","Froth do the constable's wife any harm? I would know that of your","honour."],"id":230,"m":"I'll swear on the Bible his face is the worst thing about him. Well then, if his face is the worst thing about him, how could Master Froth do the constable's wife any harm? I'd like your honour to tell me that."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["He's in the right. Constable. What say you to it?"],"id":231,"m":"He has a point. Constable, what do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["First, an it like you, the house is a respected house; next, this is a","respected fellow; and his mistress is a respected woman."],"id":232,"m":"First, if you please, the house is a respected (he means suspected) house; next, this is a respected fellow; and his mistress is a respected woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["By this hand, sir, his wife is a more respected person than any of us","all."],"id":233,"m":"I swear, sir, his wife is a more respected person than any of us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Varlet, thou liest; thou liest, wicked varlet! The time is yet to come","that she was ever respected with man, woman, or child."],"id":234,"m":"You villain, you lie! You lie, you wicked villain! The day has never come when she was respected with any man, woman or child."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, she was respected with him before he married with her."],"id":235,"m":"Sir, she was respected with him before he married her."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Which is the wiser here, Justice or Iniquity? Is this true?"],"id":236,"m":"Which is the wiser here, Justice or Iniquity? Is this true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["O thou caitiff! O thou varlet! O thou wicked Hannibal! I respected with","her before I was married to her? If ever I was respected with her, or","she with me, let not your worship think me the poor Duke's officer.","Prove this, thou wicked Hannibal, or I'll have mine action of battery","on thee."],"id":237,"m":"Oh, you wretch! Oh, you villain! Oh, you wicked Hannibal (he means cannibal)! I respected with her before I married her? If I was ever respected with her, or she with me, don't let your worship think I'm the poor Duke's officer. Prove it, you wicked Hannibal, or I'll sue you for battery."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["If he took you a box o' th' ear, you might have your action of slander","too."],"id":238,"m":"If he gave you a box on the ear, you could sue him for slander as well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Marry, I thank your good worship for it. What is't your worship's","pleasure I shall do with this wicked caitiff?"],"id":239,"m":"Why, thank you for that, your good worship. What would your worship like me to do with this wicked wretch?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Truly, officer, because he hath some offences in him that thou wouldst","discover if thou couldst, let him continue in his courses till thou","know'st what they are."],"id":240,"m":"Honestly, officer, since he has some crimes in him that you would uncover if you could, let him carry on as he is until you find out what they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Marry, I thank your worship for it.—Thou seest, thou wicked varlet,","now, what's come upon thee. Thou art to continue now, thou varlet, thou","art to continue."],"id":241,"m":"Why, thank you, your worship. (To Pompey) Now you see what has come down on you, you wicked villain. You are to continue now, you villain, you are to continue."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["[To Froth] Where were you born, friend?"],"id":242,"m":"(To Froth) Where were you born, friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["Here in Vienna, sir."],"id":243,"m":"Here in Vienna, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Are you of fourscore pounds a year?"],"id":244,"m":"Do you have eighty pounds a year?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["Yes, an't please you, sir."],"id":245,"m":"Yes, if you please, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["So. [To Pompey] What trade are you of, sir?"],"id":246,"m":"I see. (To Pompey) And what's your trade, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["A tapster, a poor widow's tapster."],"id":247,"m":"A barman, a poor widow's barman."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Your mistress' name?"],"id":248,"m":"Your mistress's name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Mistress Overdone."],"id":249,"m":"Mistress Overdone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Hath she had any more than one husband?"],"id":250,"m":"Has she had more than one husband?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Nine, sir; Overdone by the last."],"id":251,"m":"Nine, sir. She was Overdone by the last."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Nine?—Come hither to me, Master Froth. Master Froth, I would not have","you acquainted with tapsters; they will draw you, Master Froth, and you","will hang them. Get you gone, and let me hear no more of you."],"id":252,"m":"Nine? Come over here, Master Froth. Master Froth, I'd rather you didn't mix with barmen. They will draw you in like beer, Master Froth, and you'll have them hanged. Off you go, and let me hear no more about you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FROTH","lines":["I thank your worship. For mine own part, I never come into any room in","a taphouse but I am drawn in."],"id":253,"m":"Thank you, your worship. For my part, I never go into a room in a tavern without being drawn in."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Well, no more of it, Master Froth. Farewell."],"id":254,"m":"Well, that's enough, Master Froth. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Froth.","id":255},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","cont":1,"lines":["Come you hither to me, Master tapster. What's your name, Master","tapster?"],"id":256,"m":"Come over here, Master Barman. What's your name, Master Barman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Pompey."],"id":257,"m":"Pompey."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["What else?"],"id":258,"m":"What else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Bum, sir."],"id":259,"m":"Bum, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Troth, and your bum is the greatest thing about you; so that, in the","beastliest sense, you are Pompey the great. Pompey, you are partly a","bawd, Pompey, howsoever you colour it in being a tapster, are you not?","Come, tell me true, it shall be the better for you."],"id":260,"m":"Honestly, your bum is the biggest thing about you, so in the crudest sense you are Pompey the Great. Pompey, you're partly a pimp, however you disguise it by being a barman, aren't you? Come on, tell me the truth; it will go better for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Truly, sir, I am a poor fellow that would live."],"id":261,"m":"Honestly, sir, I'm a poor fellow trying to make a living."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["How would you live, Pompey? By being a bawd? What do you think of the","trade, Pompey? Is it a lawful trade?"],"id":262,"m":"How do you make a living, Pompey? By being a pimp? What do you think of the trade, Pompey? Is it a lawful trade?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["If the law would allow it, sir."],"id":263,"m":"It would be, sir, if the law allowed it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["But the law will not allow it, Pompey; nor it shall not be allowed in","Vienna."],"id":264,"m":"But the law won't allow it, Pompey, and it will not be allowed in Vienna."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Does your worship mean to geld and splay all the youth of the city?"],"id":265,"m":"Does your worship plan to castrate and spay all the young people in the city?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["No, Pompey."],"id":266,"m":"No, Pompey."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Truly, sir, in my poor opinion, they will to't then. If your worship","will take order for the drabs and the knaves, you need not to fear the","bawds."],"id":267,"m":"Then honestly, sir, in my humble opinion, they'll go on doing it. If your worship deals with the whores and the rogues, you won't need to worry about the pimps."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["There is pretty orders beginning, I can tell you. It is but heading and","hanging."],"id":268,"m":"I can tell you, some fine orders are being made. It's nothing but beheading and hanging."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["If you head and hang all that offend that way but for ten year","together, you'll be glad to give out a commission for more heads. If","this law hold in Vienna ten year, I'll rent the fairest house in it","after threepence a bay. If you live to see this come to pass, say","Pompey told you so."],"id":269,"m":"If you behead and hang everyone who offends that way for ten years running, you'll be glad to send out an order for more heads. If this law lasts ten years in Vienna, I'll rent the finest house in it for threepence a room. If you live to see it, say Pompey told you so."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Thank you, good Pompey; and, in requital of your prophecy, hark you: I","advise you, let me not find you before me again upon any complaint","whatsoever; no, not for dwelling where you do. If I do, Pompey, I shall","beat you to your tent, and prove a shrewd Caesar to you. In plain","dealing, Pompey, I shall have you whipped. So for this time, Pompey,","fare you well."],"id":270,"m":"Thank you, good Pompey. And in return for your prophecy, listen: I advise you not to let me find you before me again on any complaint at all, not even for living where you live. If I do, Pompey, I'll beat you back to your tent and be a harsh Caesar to you. To put it plainly, Pompey, I'll have you whipped. So for now, Pompey, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I thank your worship for your good counsel. [Aside] But I shall","follow it as the flesh and fortune shall better determine.","Whip me? No, no; let carman whip his jade;","The valiant heart's not whipped out of his trade."],"id":271,"m":"Thank you, your worship, for your good advice. (Aside) But I'll follow it as my body and my luck decide. Whip me? No, no. Let the carter whip his old nag; a brave heart can't be whipped out of his trade."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":272},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Come hither to me, Master Elbow. Come hither, Master Constable. How","long have you been in this place of constable?"],"id":273,"m":"Come here, Master Elbow. Come here, Master Constable. How long have you been a constable?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Seven year and a half, sir."],"id":274,"m":"Seven and a half years, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I thought, by the readiness in the office, you had continued in it","sometime. You say seven years together?"],"id":275,"m":"I thought, from how expert you are at the job, that you'd been doing it for some time. Seven years in a row, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["And a half, sir."],"id":276,"m":"And a half, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Alas, it hath been great pains to you. They do you wrong to put you so","oft upon't. Are there not men in your ward sufficient to serve it?"],"id":277,"m":"Oh dear, that must have been a great burden for you. It's unfair to make you do it so often. Aren't there men in your district able to serve?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Faith, sir, few of any wit in such matters. As they are chosen, they","are glad to choose me for them; I do it for some piece of money, and go","through with all."],"id":278,"m":"Honestly, sir, few with any brains for such matters. When they are chosen, they're glad to choose me in their place. I do it for a bit of money and handle it all."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Look you bring me in the names of some six or seven, the most","sufficient of your parish."],"id":279,"m":"Make sure you bring me the names of six or seven of the most capable men in your parish."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["To your worship's house, sir?"],"id":280,"m":"To your worship's house, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["To my house. Fare you well."],"id":281,"m":"To my house. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Elbow.","id":282},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","cont":1,"lines":["What's o'clock, think you?"],"id":283,"m":"What time do you think it is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JUSTICE","lines":["Eleven, sir."],"id":284,"m":"Eleven, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I pray you home to dinner with me."],"id":285,"m":"Please come home and have lunch with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"JUSTICE","lines":["I humbly thank you."],"id":286,"m":"Thank you very much."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["It grieves me for the death of Claudio,","But there's no remedy."],"id":287,"m":"Claudio's death makes me sad, but there's nothing to be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"JUSTICE","lines":["Lord Angelo is severe."],"id":288,"m":"Lord Angelo is harsh."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["It is but needful.","Mercy is not itself that oft looks so;","Pardon is still the nurse of second woe.","But yet, Poor Claudio! There's no remedy.","Come, sir."],"id":289,"m":"It's only necessary. Mercy is not really mercy when it is shown too often; pardon always breeds a second wrong. And yet, poor Claudio! There's nothing to be done. Come, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":290},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Another room in Angelo's house.","id":291},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost and a Servant.","id":292},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He's hearing of a cause. He will come straight.","I'll tell him of you."],"id":293,"m":"He's hearing a case. He'll come in a moment. I'll tell him you're here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Pray you do."],"id":294,"m":"Please do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":295},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["I'll know","His pleasure, may be he will relent. Alas,","He hath but as offended in a dream;","All sects, all ages, smack of this vice, and he","To die for 't!"],"id":296,"m":"I'll find out what he wants; maybe he will relent. Oh, he has only sinned as if in a dream. People of every kind and every age have a taste of this vice, and he has to die for it!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo.","id":297},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Now, what's the matter, Provost?"],"id":298,"m":"Well, what is it, Provost?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Is it your will Claudio shall die tomorrow?"],"id":299,"m":"Is it your wish that Claudio should die tomorrow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Did not I tell thee yea? Hadst thou not order?","Why dost thou ask again?"],"id":300,"m":"Didn't I tell you yes? Didn't you have your orders? Why are you asking again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Lest I might be too rash.","Under your good correction, I have seen","When, after execution, judgement hath","Repented o'er his doom."],"id":301,"m":"In case I might be acting too hastily. With respect, I have seen judges, after an execution, regret their sentence."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Go to; let that be mine.","Do you your office, or give up your place,","And you shall well be spared."],"id":302,"m":"Enough; leave that to me. Do your job, or resign, and you won't be missed."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I crave your honour's pardon.","What shall be done, sir, with the groaning Juliet?","She's very near her hour."],"id":303,"m":"I beg your honour's pardon. What is to be done, sir, with Juliet? She is groaning in labour; her time is very near."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Dispose of her","To some more fitter place; and that with speed."],"id":304,"m":"Move her to some more suitable place, and quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Servant.","id":305},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Here is the sister of the man condemned","Desires access to you."],"id":306,"m":"The sister of the condemned man is here and asks to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Hath he a sister?"],"id":307,"m":"He has a sister?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Ay, my good lord, a very virtuous maid,","And to be shortly of a sisterhood,","If not already."],"id":308,"m":"Yes, my good lord, a very virtuous young woman who is soon to join a sisterhood, if she hasn't already."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Well, let her be admitted."],"id":309,"m":"Well, let her in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":310},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","cont":1,"lines":["See you the fornicatress be removed;","Let her have needful but not lavish means;","There shall be order for it."],"id":311,"m":"See that the fornicator is moved. Give her what she needs, but nothing extravagant. Orders will be given for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucio and Isabella.","id":312},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["[Offering to retire] Save your honour!"],"id":313,"m":"(Starting to leave) God save your honour!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Stay a little while. [To Isabella] You are welcome. What's your","will?"],"id":314,"m":"Stay a little while. (To Isabella) You are welcome. What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I am a woeful suitor to your honour,","Please but your honour hear me."],"id":315,"m":"I come to your honour with a sorrowful request, if your honour will only hear me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Well, what's your suit?"],"id":316,"m":"Well, what is your request?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["There is a vice that most I do abhor,","And most desire should meet the blow of justice;","For which I would not plead, but that I must;","For which I must not plead, but that I am","At war 'twixt will and will not."],"id":317,"m":"There is a vice that I hate above all others, and which I most want to see struck by justice. I would not plead for it, except that I must; I must not plead for it, except that I am torn between wanting to and not wanting to."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Well, the matter?"],"id":318,"m":"Well, what is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I have a brother is condemned to die;","I do beseech you, let it be his fault,","And not my brother."],"id":319,"m":"I have a brother who is condemned to die. I beg you, let it be his crime that dies, and not my brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Heaven give thee moving graces."],"id":320,"m":"Heaven give you the grace to move him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Condemn the fault, and not the actor of it?","Why, every fault's condemned ere it be done.","Mine were the very cipher of a function","To find the faults whose fine stands in record,","And let go by the actor."],"id":321,"m":"Condemn the crime and not the man who did it? Why, every crime is condemned before it is committed. My job would be worth nothing if all I did was record the punishments for crimes and let the criminals go free."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O just but severe law!","I had a brother, then. Heaven keep your honour!"],"id":322,"m":"Oh, just but harsh law! Then I had a brother. Heaven keep your honour!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Going.","id":323},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["[To Isabella] Give't not o'er so. To him again, entreat him,","Kneel down before him, hang upon his gown;","You are too cold. If you should need a pin,","You could not with more tame a tongue desire it.","To him, I say."],"id":324,"m":"(To Isabella) Don't give up like that. Go back to him, beg him, kneel down in front of him, cling to his robe. You're too cold. If you were asking for a pin, you couldn't ask more tamely. Go to him, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Must he needs die?"],"id":325,"m":"Must he die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Maiden, no remedy."],"id":326,"m":"Young lady, nothing can be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Yes, I do think that you might pardon him,","And neither heaven nor man grieve at the mercy."],"id":327,"m":"Yes, I think you could pardon him, and neither heaven nor any man would be sorry for the mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I will not do't."],"id":328,"m":"I won't do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["But can you if you would?"],"id":329,"m":"But could you, if you wanted to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Look, what I will not, that I cannot do."],"id":330,"m":"Listen: what I will not do, I cannot do."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["But might you do't, and do the world no wrong,","If so your heart were touched with that remorse","As mine is to him?"],"id":331,"m":"But could you do it without doing the world any wrong, if your heart were touched with the same pity that mine feels for him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["He's sentenced, 'tis too late."],"id":332,"m":"He has been sentenced; it's too late."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["[To Isabella] You are too cold."],"id":333,"m":"(To Isabella) You're too cold."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Too late? Why, no. I that do speak a word","May call it back again. Well, believe this:","No ceremony that to great ones longs,","Not the king's crown, nor the deputed sword,","The marshal's truncheon, nor the judge's robe,","Become them with one half so good a grace","As mercy does.","If he had been as you, and you as he,","You would have slipped like him, but he like you","Would not have been so stern."],"id":334,"m":"Too late? Why, no. I can speak a word and take it back again. So believe this: none of the trappings that belong to the great, not the king's crown, nor the sword of his deputy, nor the marshal's baton, nor the judge's robe, suits them half so gracefully as mercy does. If he had been in your place, and you in his, you would have slipped as he did; but he, in your place, would not have been so harsh."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Pray you be gone."],"id":335,"m":"Please go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I would to heaven I had your potency,","And you were Isabel! Should it then be thus?","No; I would tell what 'twere to be a judge","And what a prisoner."],"id":336,"m":"I wish to heaven I had your power, and you were Isabel! Would it go like this then? No. I would show you what it means to be a judge, and what it means to be a prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["[Aside] Ay, touch him; there's the vein."],"id":337,"m":"(Aside) Yes, get at him; that's the spot."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Your brother is a forfeit of the law,","And you but waste your words."],"id":338,"m":"Your brother's life is forfeit to the law, and you are only wasting your words."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Alas, alas!","Why, all the souls that were were forfeit once,","And He that might the vantage best have took","Found out the remedy. How would you be","If He, which is the top of judgement, should","But judge you as you are? O, think on that,","And mercy then will breathe within your lips,","Like man new made."],"id":339,"m":"Oh, oh! Why, every soul that ever lived was once forfeit, and He who had every right to take advantage of that found a way to save them instead. What would become of you if He, the highest judge of all, judged you as you are? Oh, think about that, and then mercy will breathe from your lips, as if you were a man made new."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Be you content, fair maid.","It is the law, not I, condemns your brother.","Were he my kinsman, brother, or my son,","It should be thus with him. He must die tomorrow."],"id":340,"m":"Calm yourself, lovely young woman. It is the law that condemns your brother, not me. If he were my relative, my brother or my son, it would be the same for him. He must die tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Tomorrow? O, that's sudden! Spare him, spare him!","He's not prepared for death. Even for our kitchens","We kill the fowl of season. Shall we serve heaven","With less respect than we do minister","To our gross selves? Good, good my lord, bethink you.","Who is it that hath died for this offence?","There's many have committed it."],"id":341,"m":"Tomorrow? Oh, that's so sudden! Spare him, spare him! He isn't ready to die. Even for our kitchens we only kill birds when they are in season. Shall we serve heaven with less care than we feed our own coarse bodies? My good, good lord, think again. Who has ever died for this crime? Plenty have committed it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Ay, well said."],"id":342,"m":"Yes, well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["The law hath not been dead, though it hath slept.","Those many had not dared to do that evil","If the first that did th' edict infringe","Had answered for his deed. Now 'tis awake,","Takes note of what is done, and, like a prophet,","Looks in a glass that shows what future evils,","Either now, or by remissness new conceived,","And so in progress to be hatched and born,","Are now to have no successive degrees,","But, where they live, to end."],"id":343,"m":"The law has not been dead, though it has been asleep. All those people would not have dared to do that wrong if the first person who broke the law had been made to answer for it. Now it is awake. It notices what is done and, like a prophet, looks into a crystal ball that shows future crimes, whether already planned or newly conceived through carelessness and so on their way to being hatched and born, and it means them to have no further stages, but to end where they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Yet show some pity."],"id":344,"m":"Still, show some pity."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I show it most of all when I show justice;","For then I pity those I do not know,","Which a dismissed offence would after gall,","And do him right that, answering one foul wrong,","Lives not to act another. Be satisfied;","Your brother dies tomorrow; be content."],"id":345,"m":"I show it most of all when I show justice. Then I pity people I don't know, who would later be hurt by a crime that went unpunished; and I do right by the man who pays for one foul crime and doesn't live to commit another. Be satisfied. Your brother dies tomorrow. Accept it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["So you must be the first that gives this sentence,","And he that suffers. O, it is excellent","To have a giant's strength; but it is tyrannous","To use it like a giant."],"id":346,"m":"So you must be the first to pass this sentence, and he the first to suffer it. Oh, it is wonderful to have a giant's strength, but it is tyranny to use it like a giant."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["That's well said."],"id":347,"m":"That's well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Could great men thunder","As Jove himself does, Jove would ne'er be quiet,","For every pelting petty officer","Would use his heaven for thunder.","Nothing but thunder. Merciful Heaven,","Thou rather with thy sharp and sulphurous bolt","Splits the unwedgeable and gnarled oak,","Than the soft myrtle. But man, proud man,","Dressed in a little brief authority,","Most ignorant of what he's most assured,","His glassy essence, like an angry ape","Plays such fantastic tricks before high heaven","As makes the angels weep; who, with our spleens,","Would all themselves laugh mortal."],"id":348,"m":"If great men could thunder like Jove himself, Jove would never have any peace, because every paltry little official would use his sky to thunder in. Nothing but thunder. Merciful heaven, you would rather split the gnarled oak that no wedge can split with your sharp, sulphurous lightning than the soft myrtle. But man, proud man, dressed up in a little brief authority, most ignorant of what he is most sure of, his own fragile soul, plays such crazy tricks before high heaven, like an angry ape, that he makes the angels weep; and if they had our tempers, they would laugh themselves to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["O, to him, to him, wench! He will relent;","He's coming. I perceive 't."],"id":349,"m":"Oh, at him, at him, girl! He will give in. He's coming round, I can see it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Pray heaven she win him."],"id":350,"m":"Heaven grant she wins him over."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["We cannot weigh our brother with ourself.","Great men may jest with saints; 'tis wit in them,","But in the less, foul profanation."],"id":351,"m":"We cannot judge our brother by the same scale as ourselves. Great men may joke about saints; in them it is wit, but in lesser men it is foul blasphemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Thou'rt i' th' right, girl; more o' that."],"id":352,"m":"You're right, girl. More of that."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["That in the captain's but a choleric word","Which in the soldier is flat blasphemy."],"id":353,"m":"What is only a hot-tempered word from the captain is downright blasphemy from the soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Art advised o' that? More on't."],"id":354,"m":"Do you know that? More of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Why do you put these sayings upon me?"],"id":355,"m":"Why do you apply these sayings to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Because authority, though it err like others,","Hath yet a kind of medicine in itself","That skins the vice o' th' top. Go to your bosom,","Knock there, and ask your heart what it doth know","That's like my brother's fault. If it confess","A natural guiltiness such as is his,","Let it not sound a thought upon your tongue","Against my brother's life."],"id":356,"m":"Because authority, though it makes mistakes like anyone else, has a kind of medicine of its own that grows skin over the sore on top. Look into your own breast. Knock there and ask your heart if it knows anything like my brother's fault. If it admits a natural guilt like his, don't let it speak a single thought on your tongue against my brother's life."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["She speaks, and 'tis such sense","That my sense breeds with it. [Going]","Fare you well."],"id":357,"m":"She speaks such sense that my senses are stirred by it. (Starting to leave) Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Gentle my lord, turn back."],"id":358,"m":"My kind lord, turn back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I will bethink me. Come again tomorrow."],"id":359,"m":"I will think it over. Come back tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Hark how I'll bribe you. Good my lord, turn back."],"id":360,"m":"Listen to how I will bribe you. My good lord, turn back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["How? Bribe me?"],"id":361,"m":"What? Bribe me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Ay, with such gifts that heaven shall share with you."],"id":362,"m":"Yes, with gifts that heaven itself will share with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["You had marred all else."],"id":363,"m":"(To Isabella) Otherwise you'd have ruined everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Not with fond shekels of the tested gold,","Or stones, whose rates are either rich or poor","As fancy values them, but with true prayers,","That shall be up at heaven and enter there","Ere sunrise, prayers from preserved souls,","From fasting maids, whose minds are dedicate","To nothing temporal."],"id":364,"m":"Not with silly coins of tested gold, or with gems whose value is high or low depending on fashion, but with true prayers that will rise to heaven and enter it before sunrise: prayers from souls kept pure, from fasting young women whose minds are devoted to nothing of this world."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Well; come to me tomorrow."],"id":365,"m":"Well, come to me tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["[Aside to Isabella] Go to, 'tis well; away."],"id":366,"m":"(Aside to Isabella) Good, that's done it. Let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Heaven keep your honour safe."],"id":367,"m":"Heaven keep your honour safe."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["[Aside] Amen.","For I am that way going to temptation,","Where prayers cross."],"id":368,"m":"(Aside) Amen. For I am heading towards temptation, where prayers cross each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["At what hour tomorrow","Shall I attend your lordship?"],"id":369,"m":"At what time tomorrow shall I come to your lordship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["At any time 'fore noon."],"id":370,"m":"Any time before noon."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Save your honour."],"id":371,"m":"God save your honour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Isabella, Lucio and Provost.","id":372},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["From thee, even from thy virtue!","What's this? What's this? Is this her fault or mine?","The tempter or the tempted, who sins most, ha?","Not she; nor doth she tempt; but it is I","That, lying by the violet in the sun,","Do as the carrion does, not as the flower,","Corrupt with virtuous season. Can it be","That modesty may more betray our sense","Than woman's lightness? Having waste ground enough,","Shall we desire to raze the sanctuary","And pitch our evils there? O, fie, fie, fie!","What dost thou, or what art thou, Angelo?","Dost thou desire her foully for those things","That make her good? O, let her brother live.","Thieves for their robbery have authority","When judges steal themselves. What, do I love her,","That I desire to hear her speak again","And feast upon her eyes? What is't I dream on?","O cunning enemy, that, to catch a saint,","With saints dost bait thy hook! Most dangerous","Is that temptation that doth goad us on","To sin in loving virtue. Never could the strumpet","With all her double vigour, art, and nature,","Once stir my temper, but this virtuous maid","Subdues me quite. Ever till now","When men were fond, I smiled and wondered how."],"id":373,"m":"Safe from you, even from your virtue! What's this? What's this? Is it her fault or mine? Who sins more, the tempter or the tempted? Not her. She doesn't tempt me. It is I who, lying beside the violet in the sunshine, behave like the rotting carcass and not like the flower, and go bad in the same good season that makes it bloom. Can it be that modesty arouses our senses more than a woman's looseness? When there is plenty of waste ground, do we want to knock down the holy place and set up our sins there? Oh, shame, shame, shame! What are you doing, Angelo, and what are you? Do you desire her foully for the very things that make her good? Oh, let her brother live. Thieves have a licence to rob when judges steal themselves. What, do I love her, that I want to hear her speak again and feast my eyes on hers? What am I dreaming about? Oh cunning devil, who baits his hook with a saint to catch a saint! The most dangerous temptation is the one that drives us to sin through loving virtue. The whore, with all her double power of art and nature, could never once stir my passions, but this virtuous young woman conquers me completely. Until now, when men were lovesick, I smiled and wondered how they could be."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":374},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A room in a prison.","id":375},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke disguised as a Friar, and Provost.","id":376},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Hail to you, Provost, so I think you are."],"id":377,"m":"Greetings to you, Provost; I think that's who you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I am the Provost. What's your will, good friar?"],"id":378,"m":"I am the Provost. What do you want, good friar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Bound by my charity and my blessed order,","I come to visit the afflicted spirits","Here in the prison. Do me the common right","To let me see them, and to make me know","The nature of their crimes, that I may minister","To them accordingly."],"id":379,"m":"Bound by charity and by my holy order, I have come to visit the troubled souls here in the prison. Grant me the usual right of seeing them, and tell me what their crimes are, so that I can minister to each one properly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I would do more than that, if more were needful."],"id":380,"m":"I would do more than that, if more were needed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Juliet.","id":381},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["Look, here comes one, a gentlewoman of mine,","Who, falling in the flaws of her own youth,","Hath blistered her report. She is with child,","And he that got it, sentenced: a young man","More fit to do another such offence","Than die for this."],"id":382,"m":"Look, here comes one, a young lady in my charge, who fell through the weaknesses of her youth and has scarred her good name. She is pregnant, and the man who fathered the child is sentenced: a young man more fit to commit the same offence again than to die for this one."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["When must he die?"],"id":383,"m":"When must he die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["As I do think, tomorrow.","[To Juliet] I have provided for you; stay a while","And you shall be conducted."],"id":384,"m":"Tomorrow, I believe. (To Juliet) I have made arrangements for you. Wait a while and you will be taken there."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Repent you, fair one, of the sin you carry?"],"id":385,"m":"Do you repent, lovely one, of the sin you are carrying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["I do; and bear the shame most patiently."],"id":386,"m":"I do, and I bear the shame very patiently."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I'll teach you how you shall arraign your conscience,","And try your penitence, if it be sound","Or hollowly put on."],"id":387,"m":"I will teach you how to put your conscience on trial and test your repentance, whether it is sound or just hollow show."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["I'll gladly learn."],"id":388,"m":"I will gladly learn."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Love you the man that wronged you?"],"id":389,"m":"Do you love the man who wronged you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["Yes, as I love the woman that wronged him."],"id":390,"m":"Yes, just as I love the woman who wronged him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["So then it seems your most offenceful act","Was mutually committed?"],"id":391,"m":"So it seems your sinful act was committed by both of you together?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["Mutually."],"id":392,"m":"By both of us."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Then was your sin of heavier kind than his."],"id":393,"m":"Then your sin was heavier than his."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["I do confess it, and repent it, father."],"id":394,"m":"I confess it, and I repent it, father."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["'Tis meet so, daughter; but lest you do repent","As that the sin hath brought you to this shame,","Which sorrow is always toward ourselves, not heaven,","Showing we would not spare heaven as we love it,","But as we stand in fear—"],"id":395,"m":"That is right, daughter. But in case you are repenting only because the sin has brought you to this shame, which is the kind of sorrow that is always for ourselves and not for heaven, and shows that we keep from offending heaven not because we love it but because we fear it..."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["I do repent me as it is an evil,","And take the shame with joy."],"id":396,"m":"I repent because it is a sin, and I accept the shame with joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["There rest.","Your partner, as I hear, must die tomorrow,","And I am going with instruction to him.","Grace go with you! Benedicite!"],"id":397,"m":"Keep to that. Your partner, I hear, must die tomorrow, and I am going to give him spiritual guidance. Grace go with you! God bless you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":398},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIET","lines":["Must die tomorrow? O, injurious love","That respites me a life, whose very comfort","Is still a dying horror!"],"id":399,"m":"Must die tomorrow? Oh, cruel love, that spares my life when the only comfort of that life is a constant, dying horror!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["'Tis pity of him."],"id":400,"m":"It's a pity about him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":401},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"A room in Angelo's house.","id":402},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo.","id":403},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["When I would pray and think, I think and pray","To several subjects. Heaven hath my empty words,","Whilst my invention, hearing not my tongue,","Anchors on Isabel. Heaven in my mouth,","As if I did but only chew his name,","And in my heart the strong and swelling evil","Of my conception. The state whereon I studied","Is, like a good thing being often read,","Grown sere and tedious; yea, my gravity,","Wherein—let no man hear me—I take pride,","Could I with boot change for an idle plume","Which the air beats for vain. O place, O form,","How often dost thou with thy case, thy habit,","Wrench awe from fools, and tie the wiser souls","To thy false seeming! Blood, thou art blood.","Let's write good angel on the devil's horn.","'Tis not the devil's crest."],"id":404,"m":"When I try to pray and think, my thoughts and prayers go to different places. Heaven gets my empty words, while my imagination, not listening to my tongue, is anchored on Isabel. Heaven is in my mouth, as if I were only chewing on its name, and in my heart is the strong and swelling evil of my desire. The government I used to study has grown dry and boring, like a good book read too often. Yes, even my seriousness, which, let no one hear me say it, I take pride in, I would gladly trade for a silly feather blown about by the wind. Oh rank, oh outward show, how often your mere outside and costume frighten fools into awe, and trap even wiser souls into believing your false appearance! Flesh, you are only flesh. Write \"good angel\" on the devil's horn, and it is no longer the devil's badge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knock within.","id":405},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","cont":1,"lines":["How now, who's there?"],"id":406,"m":"Well now, who's there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Servant.","id":407},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["One Isabel, a sister, desires access to you."],"id":408,"m":"A woman called Isabel, a nun, wants to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Teach her the way."],"id":409,"m":"Show her the way in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":410},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","cont":1,"lines":["O heavens,","Why does my blood thus muster to my heart,","Making both it unable for itself","And dispossessing all my other parts","Of necessary fitness?","So play the foolish throngs with one that swoons,","Come all to help him, and so stop the air","By which he should revive. And even so","The general subject to a well-wished king","Quit their own part, and in obsequious fondness","Crowd to his presence, where their untaught love","Must needs appear offence."],"id":411,"m":"Oh heavens, why is my blood rushing to my heart like this, making it unable to work and robbing every other part of me of the strength it needs? This is how foolish crowds behave around someone who faints: they all come to help him and so block the air that would revive him. And in the same way the subjects of a well-loved king leave their own work and in fawning devotion crowd around him, where their clumsy love can only seem an offence."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Isabella.","id":412},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","cont":1,"lines":["How now, fair maid?"],"id":413,"m":"Well, lovely young woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I am come to know your pleasure."],"id":414,"m":"I have come to hear what you have decided."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["That you might know it, would much better please me","Than to demand what 'tis. Your brother cannot live."],"id":415,"m":"I would be much better pleased if you knew it without having to ask. Your brother cannot live."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Even so. Heaven keep your honour."],"id":416,"m":"I see. Heaven keep your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Yet may he live a while. And, it may be,","As long as you or I. Yet he must die."],"id":417,"m":"And yet he may live a while; perhaps as long as you or I. And yet he must die."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Under your sentence?"],"id":418,"m":"Under your sentence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Yea."],"id":419,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["When, I beseech you? That in his reprieve,","Longer or shorter, he may be so fitted","That his soul sicken not."],"id":420,"m":"When, I beg you? So that during his reprieve, however long or short, he can be made ready, and his soul does not go sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Ha! Fie, these filthy vices! It were as good","To pardon him that hath from nature stolen","A man already made, as to remit","Their saucy sweetness that do coin heaven's image","In stamps that are forbid. 'Tis all as easy","Falsely to take away a life true made","As to put metal in restrained means","To make a false one."],"id":421,"m":"Ha! Shame on these filthy vices! It would be as right to pardon a man who has stolen from nature a person already made, by murder, as to excuse the insolent pleasure of those who stamp heaven's image on forbidden coins and make illegitimate children. It is just as easy wrongly to take away a lawfully made life as to pour metal into a banned mould to make a false one."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["'Tis set down so in heaven, but not in earth."],"id":422,"m":"That is how it is written in heaven, but not on earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Say you so? Then I shall pose you quickly.","Which had you rather, that the most just law","Now took your brother's life; or, to redeem him,","Give up your body to such sweet uncleanness","As she that he hath stained?"],"id":423,"m":"Do you say so? Then I will quickly put a question to you. Which would you rather: that the perfectly just law should now take your brother's life, or that, to buy him back, you should give your body up to the same sweet filth as the woman he has stained?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Sir, believe this:","I had rather give my body than my soul."],"id":424,"m":"Sir, believe this: I would rather give up my body than my soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I talk not of your soul. Our compelled sins","Stand more for number than for accompt."],"id":425,"m":"I am not talking about your soul. Sins we are forced into are counted, but not held against us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["How say you?"],"id":426,"m":"What do you mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Nay, I'll not warrant that, for I can speak","Against the thing I say. Answer to this:","I, now the voice of the recorded law,","Pronounce a sentence on your brother's life.","Might there not be a charity in sin","To save this brother's life?"],"id":427,"m":"No, I won't swear to that, because I could argue against what I've just said. Answer this. I, who am now the voice of the written law, pass sentence on your brother's life. Might there not be a kind of charity in a sin committed to save this brother's life?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Please you to do't,","I'll take it as a peril to my soul;","It is no sin at all, but charity."],"id":428,"m":"If you are willing to do it, I'll take the risk to my soul. It isn't a sin at all, but charity."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Pleased you to do't at peril of your soul,","Were equal poise of sin and charity."],"id":429,"m":"If you were willing to do it at the risk of your soul, the sin and the charity would balance out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["That I do beg his life, if it be sin,","Heaven let me bear it. You granting of my suit,","If that be sin, I'll make it my morn prayer","To have it added to the faults of mine,","And nothing of your answer."],"id":430,"m":"If it is a sin for me to beg for his life, let heaven make me carry it. If it is a sin for you to grant my request, I will pray every morning to have it added to my own faults, and none of it charged to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Nay, but hear me.","Your sense pursues not mine. Either you are ignorant,","Or seem so, crafty; and that's not good."],"id":431,"m":"No, listen to me. You are not following my meaning. Either you are ignorant, or you are cunningly pretending to be, and that is not good."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Let me be ignorant, and in nothing good,","But graciously to know I am no better."],"id":432,"m":"Let me be ignorant, and good in nothing, except in knowing, by grace, that I am no better."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Thus wisdom wishes to appear most bright","When it doth tax itself, as these black masks","Proclaim an enshield beauty ten times louder","Than beauty could, displayed. But mark me;","To be received plain, I'll speak more gross.","Your brother is to die."],"id":433,"m":"That is how wisdom likes to shine most brightly, when it accuses itself, just as these black masks announce the hidden beauty behind them ten times more loudly than the beauty could if it were on show. But listen. To be understood plainly, I'll speak more crudely. Your brother is to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["So."],"id":434,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["And his offence is so, as it appears,","Accountant to the law upon that pain."],"id":435,"m":"And his crime, as it appears, makes him answerable to the law for that penalty."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["True."],"id":436,"m":"True."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Admit no other way to save his life—","As I subscribe not that, nor any other,","But, in the loss of question, that you, his sister,","Finding yourself desired of such a person","Whose credit with the judge, or own great place,","Could fetch your brother from the manacles","Of the all-binding law; and that there were","No earthly mean to save him but that either","You must lay down the treasures of your body","To this supposed, or else to let him suffer,","What would you do?"],"id":437,"m":"Suppose there is no other way to save his life (not that I agree to that, or to anything else; I am only putting it as a case for argument): suppose that you, his sister, found yourself desired by a man whose influence with the judge, or whose own high position, could free your brother from the chains of the law that binds everyone; and suppose there were no earthly way to save him except that either you give up the treasures of your body to this imagined man, or else let your brother suffer. What would you do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["As much for my poor brother as myself.","That is, were I under the terms of death,","Th' impression of keen whips I'd wear as rubies,","And strip myself to death as to a bed","That longing have been sick for, ere I'd yield","My body up to shame."],"id":438,"m":"As much for my poor brother as for myself. That is, if I were under sentence of death, I would wear the marks of sharp whips like rubies, and strip for death as if for a bed I had been longing for, before I would give up my body to shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Then must your brother die."],"id":439,"m":"Then your brother must die."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["And 'twere the cheaper way.","Better it were a brother died at once","Than that a sister, by redeeming him,","Should die for ever."],"id":440,"m":"And that would be the cheaper way. It is better for a brother to die once than for a sister, by saving him, to die for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Were not you then as cruel as the sentence","That you have slandered so?"],"id":441,"m":"Wouldn't you then be as cruel as the sentence you have attacked so much?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Ignominy in ransom and free pardon","Are of two houses. Lawful mercy","Is nothing kin to foul redemption."],"id":442,"m":"A disgraceful ransom and a free pardon come from two different families. Lawful mercy is no relation of foul bargaining."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["You seemed of late to make the law a tyrant,","And rather proved the sliding of your brother","A merriment than a vice."],"id":443,"m":"A moment ago you seemed to make the law a tyrant, and you made your brother's slip sound more like a bit of fun than a vice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, pardon me, my lord. It oft falls out,","To have what we would have, we speak not what we mean.","I something do excuse the thing I hate","For his advantage that I dearly love."],"id":444,"m":"Oh, forgive me, my lord. It often happens that to get what we want, we don't say what we mean. I made some excuses for the thing I hate, for the sake of the brother I love dearly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["We are all frail."],"id":445,"m":"We are all weak."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Else let my brother die,","If not a feodary but only he","Owe and succeed by weakness."],"id":446,"m":"Then let my brother die, if he alone, and no one alongside him, owns and inherits this weakness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Nay, women are frail too."],"id":447,"m":"No, women are weak too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Ay, as the glasses where they view themselves,","Which are as easy broke as they make forms.","Women?—Help, heaven! Men their creation mar","In profiting by them. Nay, call us ten times frail;","For we are soft as our complexions are,","And credulous to false prints."],"id":448,"m":"Yes, like the mirrors they look at themselves in, which break as easily as they make images. Women! Heaven help us! Men spoil the creatures God made them by taking advantage of women. Yes, call us ten times weak, because we are as soft as our complexions, and easily take the stamp of false impressions."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I think it well.","And from this testimony of your own sex,","Since I suppose we are made to be no stronger","Than faults may shake our frames, let me be bold.","I do arrest your words. Be that you are,","That is, a woman. If you be more, you're none.","If you be one, as you are well expressed","By all external warrants, show it now","By putting on the destined livery."],"id":449,"m":"I quite agree. And on this evidence from your own sex, since I suppose we are made no stronger than our faults can shake us, let me be bold. I take you at your word. Be what you are, that is, a woman. If you are more, you are not one. If you are one, as every outward sign shows you are, prove it now by putting on the uniform you were born to wear."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I have no tongue but one. Gentle my lord,","Let me intreat you speak the former language."],"id":450,"m":"I have only one way of speaking. My kind lord, I beg you, talk the way you did before."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Plainly conceive, I love you."],"id":451,"m":"Understand it plainly: I love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["My brother did love Juliet,","And you tell me that he shall die for 't."],"id":452,"m":"My brother loved Juliet, and you tell me he must die for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["He shall not, Isabel, if you give me love."],"id":453,"m":"He will not, Isabel, if you give me your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I know your virtue hath a license in't,","Which seems a little fouler than it is,","To pluck on others."],"id":454,"m":"I know your virtue allows itself a little freedom, which sounds fouler than it is, to test other people."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Believe me, on mine honour,","My words express my purpose."],"id":455,"m":"Believe me, on my honour, my words mean what they say."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Ha! Little honour to be much believed,","And most pernicious purpose! Seeming, seeming!","I will proclaim thee, Angelo, look for't.","Sign me a present pardon for my brother","Or with an outstretched throat I'll tell the world aloud","What man thou art."],"id":456,"m":"Ha! Not much honour to be believed, and a most wicked purpose! Show, all show! I will expose you, Angelo, wait and see. Sign me an immediate pardon for my brother, or I will tell the whole world at the top of my voice what kind of man you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Who will believe thee, Isabel?","My unsoiled name, th' austereness of my life,","My vouch against you, and my place i' th' state","Will so your accusation overweigh","That you shall stifle in your own report,","And smell of calumny. I have begun,","And now I give my sensual race the rein.","Fit thy consent to my sharp appetite;","Lay by all nicety and prolixious blushes","That banish what they sue for. Redeem thy brother","By yielding up thy body to my will;","Or else he must not only die the death,","But thy unkindness shall his death draw out","To ling'ring sufferance. Answer me tomorrow,","Or, by the affection that now guides me most,","I'll prove a tyrant to him. As for you,","Say what you can, my false o'erweighs your true."],"id":457,"m":"Who will believe you, Isabel? My spotless name, the strictness of my life, my word against yours and my position in the state will so outweigh your accusation that you will choke on your own story, and it will stink of slander. I have started, and now I give my desire its head. Make your consent fit my sharp hunger. Put aside all your fussiness and long blushes that drive away what they really invite. Save your brother by giving your body to my will; or else he will not only die, but your unkindness will stretch his death out into lingering torture. Answer me tomorrow, or, by the passion that now rules me most, I will be a tyrant to him. As for you, say what you like: my lies outweigh your truth."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":458},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["To whom should I complain? Did I tell this,","Who would believe me? O perilous mouths,","That bear in them one and the self-same tongue","Either of condemnation or approof,","Bidding the law make curtsy to their will,","Hooking both right and wrong to th' appetite,","To follow as it draws! I'll to my brother.","Though he hath fall'n by prompture of the blood,","Yet hath he in him such a mind of honour","That, had he twenty heads to tender down","On twenty bloody blocks, he'd yield them up","Before his sister should her body stoop","To such abhorred pollution.","Then, Isabel, live chaste, and, brother, die.","More than our brother is our chastity.","I'll tell him yet of Angelo's request,","And fit his mind to death, for his soul's rest."],"id":459,"m":"Who can I complain to? If I told this, who would believe me? Oh, dangerous mouths, that keep one and the same tongue for condemning or approving, making the law bow to their will and hooking right and wrong alike onto their appetite, to follow wherever it pulls! I'll go to my brother. Though he fell through the urging of his blood, he has such a sense of honour that, if he had twenty heads to lay down on twenty bloody blocks, he would give them all up before his sister should lower her body to such hateful filth. Then, Isabel, live chaste, and, brother, die. Our chastity is worth more than our brother. Still, I'll tell him about Angelo's demand, and prepare his mind for death, so his soul can be at peace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":460},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"A room in the prison.","id":461},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke, Claudio and Provost.","id":462},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["So then you hope of pardon from Lord Angelo?"],"id":463,"m":"So you hope for a pardon from Lord Angelo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["The miserable have no other medicine","But only hope.","I have hope to live, and am prepared to die."],"id":464,"m":"The miserable have no other medicine but hope. I hope to live, and I am ready to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Be absolute for death. Either death or life","Shall thereby be the sweeter. Reason thus with life:","If I do lose thee, I do lose a thing","That none but fools would keep. A breath thou art,","Servile to all the skyey influences","That dost this habitation where thou keep'st","Hourly afflict. Merely, thou art death's fool;","For him thou labour'st by thy flight to shun,","And yet runn'st toward him still. Thou art not noble;","For all th' accommodations that thou bear'st","Are nursed by baseness. Thou'rt by no means valiant;","For thou dost fear the soft and tender fork","Of a poor worm. Thy best of rest is sleep,","And that thou oft provok'st, yet grossly fear'st","Thy death, which is no more. Thou art not thyself;","For thou exists on many a thousand grains","That issue out of dust. Happy thou art not;","For what thou hast not, still thou striv'st to get,","And what thou hast, forget'st. Thou art not certain;","For thy complexion shifts to strange effects","After the moon. If thou art rich, thou'rt poor;","For, like an ass whose back with ingots bows,","Thou bear'st thy heavy riches but a journey,","And death unloads thee. Friend hast thou none;","For thine own bowels which do call thee sire,","The mere effusion of thy proper loins,","Do curse the gout, serpigo, and the rheum","For ending thee no sooner. Thou hast nor youth nor age,","But as it were an after-dinner's sleep","Dreaming on both; for all thy blessed youth","Becomes as aged, and doth beg the alms","Of palsied eld; and when thou art old and rich,","Thou hast neither heat, affection, limb, nor beauty","To make thy riches pleasant. What's yet in this","That bears the name of life? Yet in this life","Lie hid more thousand deaths; yet death we fear,","That makes these odds all even."],"id":465,"m":"Be completely ready for death. Then whichever comes, death or life, will be sweeter. Argue with life like this: if I lose you, I lose something only fools would want to keep. You are a breath, a slave to every influence of the stars that hour by hour afflicts this body you live in. You are simply death's fool, because you struggle to escape him by running, and still run towards him. You are not noble, because all the comforts you enjoy are fed by base things. You are not brave at all, because you fear the soft, delicate forked tongue of a poor worm. Your best rest is sleep, and you often seek it, yet you are terrified of death, which is no more than sleep. You are not your own self, because you are made of thousands of grains that come from dust. You are not happy, because you keep striving to get what you haven't got, and forget what you have. You are not constant, because your temperament changes in strange ways with the moon. If you are rich, you are poor; like a donkey whose back bends under gold bars, you carry your heavy wealth for only one journey, and then death unloads you. You have no friends, because your own children, who call you father, the very offspring of your body, curse gout, skin disease and catarrh for not finishing you off sooner. You have neither youth nor age, but only a kind of after-dinner nap dreaming of both. All your blessed youth lives like an old man and begs from shaky old age; and when you are old and rich, you have no warmth, no feeling, no strength and no beauty left to enjoy your riches. What is there in this that deserves the name of life? Yet in this life a thousand more deaths lie hidden; and still we fear death, which evens all these differences out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I humbly thank you.","To sue to live, I find I seek to die,","And seeking death, find life. Let it come on."],"id":466,"m":"Thank you humbly. I find that in begging to live I am seeking death, and in seeking death I find life. Let it come."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["[Within] What ho! Peace here; grace and good company!"],"id":467,"m":"(Calling from outside) Hello! Peace be here, grace and good company!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Who's there? Come in. The wish deserves a welcome."],"id":468,"m":"Who's there? Come in. That greeting deserves a welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Dear sir, ere long I'll visit you again."],"id":469,"m":"Dear sir, I will visit you again soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Most holy sir, I thank you."],"id":470,"m":"Thank you, most holy sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Isabella.","id":471},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["My business is a word or two with Claudio."],"id":472,"m":"My business is a word or two with Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["And very welcome. Look, signior, here's your sister."],"id":473,"m":"And you're very welcome. Look, signior, here's your sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Provost, a word with you."],"id":474,"m":"Provost, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["As many as you please."],"id":475,"m":"As many as you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Bring me to hear them speak, where I may be concealed."],"id":476,"m":"Take me where I can hear them talk without being seen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Duke and Provost.","id":477},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Now, sister, what's the comfort?"],"id":478,"m":"Now, sister, what comfort do you bring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Why,","As all comforts are, most good, most good indeed.","Lord Angelo, having affairs to heaven,","Intends you for his swift ambassador,","Where you shall be an everlasting leiger.","Therefore your best appointment make with speed;","Tomorrow you set on."],"id":479,"m":"Why, the best of comforts, the very best. Lord Angelo, having business with heaven, means to send you as his express ambassador, to stay there as a permanent envoy. So make your preparations quickly; you set off tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Is there no remedy?"],"id":480,"m":"Is there no way out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["None, but such remedy as, to save a head,","To cleave a heart in twain."],"id":481,"m":"None, except one that would save a head by splitting a heart in two."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["But is there any?"],"id":482,"m":"But is there one?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Yes, brother, you may live.","There is a devilish mercy in the judge,","If you'll implore it, that will free your life,","But fetter you till death."],"id":483,"m":"Yes, brother, you could live. There is a devilish mercy in the judge which, if you beg for it, will set your life free but chain you until death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Perpetual durance?"],"id":484,"m":"Life imprisonment?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Ay, just; perpetual durance; a restraint,","Though all the world's vastidity you had,","To a determined scope."],"id":485,"m":"Yes, exactly, life imprisonment: a confinement to one fixed space, even if you had the whole wide world to roam."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["But in what nature?"],"id":486,"m":"But what kind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["In such a one as, you consenting to't,","Would bark your honour from that trunk you bear,","And leave you naked."],"id":487,"m":"A kind that, if you agreed to it, would strip your honour from you like bark from a tree, and leave you naked."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Let me know the point."],"id":488,"m":"Tell me what it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, I do fear thee, Claudio, and I quake,","Lest thou a feverous life shouldst entertain,","And six or seven winters more respect","Than a perpetual honour. Dar'st thou die?","The sense of death is most in apprehension;","And the poor beetle that we tread upon","In corporal sufferance finds a pang as great","As when a giant dies."],"id":489,"m":"Oh, I'm afraid for you, Claudio, and I tremble in case you want to hang on to a feverish life, and value six or seven more winters above everlasting honour. Do you dare to die? The fear of death lies mostly in imagining it, and the poor beetle we tread on feels a pain in its body as great as a giant's when he dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Why give you me this shame?","Think you I can a resolution fetch","From flowery tenderness? If I must die,","I will encounter darkness as a bride","And hug it in mine arms."],"id":490,"m":"Why do you shame me like this? Do you think I can get courage from flowery, soft words? If I must die, I will meet darkness like a bride and hug it in my arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["There spake my brother! There my father's grave","Did utter forth a voice. Yes, thou must die.","Thou art too noble to conserve a life","In base appliances. This outward-sainted deputy,","Whose settled visage and deliberate word","Nips youth i' th' head, and follies doth enew","As falcon doth the fowl, is yet a devil.","His filth within being cast, he would appear","A pond as deep as hell."],"id":491,"m":"That was my brother speaking! That was a voice from my father's grave. Yes, you must die. You are too noble to keep your life by low means. This deputy, a saint on the outside, whose fixed expression and careful words nip youth in the bud and drive follies into hiding as a falcon drives birds into the water, is still a devil. If the filth inside him were dredged out, he would look like a pond as deep as hell."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["The precise Angelo?"],"id":492,"m":"The strict Angelo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, 'tis the cunning livery of hell","The damned'st body to invest and cover","In precise guards! Dost thou think, Claudio,","If I would yield him my virginity","Thou mightst be freed?"],"id":493,"m":"Oh, it is hell's cunning uniform, to dress and cover the most damned body in strict trimmings! Do you think, Claudio, that if I gave him my virginity, you might be set free?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O heavens, it cannot be."],"id":494,"m":"Oh heavens, it can't be."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Yes, he would give it thee, from this rank offence,","So to offend him still. This night's the time","That I should do what I abhor to name,","Or else thou diest tomorrow."],"id":495,"m":"Yes, he would give you this foul crime as a licence, so you could go on committing it. Tonight is the time when I must do what I can't bear to name, or else you die tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Thou shalt not do't."],"id":496,"m":"You shall not do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, were it but my life,","I'd throw it down for your deliverance","As frankly as a pin."],"id":497,"m":"Oh, if it were only my life, I'd throw it away to set you free as readily as a pin."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Thanks, dear Isabel."],"id":498,"m":"Thank you, dear Isabel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Be ready, Claudio, for your death tomorrow."],"id":499,"m":"Be ready, Claudio, for your death tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Yes. Has he affections in him","That thus can make him bite the law by th' nose","When he would force it? Sure it is no sin;","Or of the deadly seven it is the least."],"id":500,"m":"Yes. Does he have passions in him that can make him tweak the law's nose when he means to enforce it? Surely it is no sin, or of the seven deadly sins it is the least."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Which is the least?"],"id":501,"m":"Which is the least?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If it were damnable, he being so wise,","Why would he for the momentary trick","Be perdurably fined? O Isabel!"],"id":502,"m":"If it were damnable, why would he, being so wise, risk everlasting punishment for a moment's pleasure? Oh, Isabel!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["What says my brother?"],"id":503,"m":"What does my brother say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Death is a fearful thing."],"id":504,"m":"Death is a terrifying thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["And shamed life a hateful."],"id":505,"m":"And a life of shame is a hateful one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Ay, but to die, and go we know not where;","To lie in cold obstruction, and to rot;","This sensible warm motion to become","A kneaded clod; and the delighted spirit","To bathe in fiery floods, or to reside","In thrilling regions of thick-ribbed ice;","To be imprisoned in the viewless winds","And blown with restless violence round about","The pendent world; or to be worse than worst","Of those that lawless and incertain thought","Imagine howling—'tis too horrible.","The weariest and most loathed worldly life","That age, ache, penury, and imprisonment","Can lay on nature is a paradise","To what we fear of death."],"id":506,"m":"Yes, but to die and go we don't know where; to lie cold and stiff and rot; for this warm, feeling, moving body to become a lump of clay; and for the spirit that once delighted to bathe in floods of fire, or live in freezing regions of thick walls of ice; to be imprisoned in the invisible winds and blown with restless violence round and round the hanging earth; or to be worse than the worst of those that wild, uncertain imagination pictures howling: it is too horrible. The most weary and hated life on earth that old age, pain, poverty and prison can lay on us is paradise compared with what we fear in death."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Alas, alas!"],"id":507,"m":"Oh, no, no!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Sweet sister, let me live.","What sin you do to save a brother's life,","Nature dispenses with the deed so far","That it becomes a virtue."],"id":508,"m":"Sweet sister, let me live. Whatever sin you commit to save a brother's life, nature excuses the act so far that it becomes a virtue."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, you beast!","O faithless coward! O dishonest wretch!","Wilt thou be made a man out of my vice?","Is't not a kind of incest to take life","From thine own sister's shame? What should I think?","Heaven shield my mother played my father fair,","For such a warped slip of wilderness","Ne'er issued from his blood. Take my defiance,","Die, perish! Might but my bending down","Reprieve thee from thy fate, it should proceed.","I'll pray a thousand prayers for thy death,","No word to save thee."],"id":509,"m":"Oh, you beast! Oh, faithless coward! Oh, dishonourable wretch! Will you be made a man out of my sin? Isn't it a kind of incest to take your life from your own sister's shame? What am I to think? Heaven grant my mother was faithful to my father, because such a twisted, wild offshoot never came from his blood. I reject you. Die, perish! If just my bending down could save you from your fate, I would let it go ahead. I'll pray a thousand prayers for your death and not one word to save you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Nay, hear me, Isabel."],"id":510,"m":"No, listen to me, Isabel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O fie, fie, fie!","Thy sin's not accidental, but a trade.","Mercy to thee would prove itself a bawd.","'Tis best that thou diest quickly."],"id":511,"m":"Oh, shame, shame, shame! Your sin is not an accident but a trade. Mercy to you would turn out to be a pimp. It's best that you die quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Going.","id":512},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O, hear me, Isabella."],"id":513,"m":"Oh, listen to me, Isabella."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke as a Friar.","id":514},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Vouchsafe a word, young sister, but one word."],"id":515,"m":"Allow me a word, young sister, just one word."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["What is your will?"],"id":516,"m":"What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Might you dispense with your leisure, I would by and by have some","speech with you. The satisfaction I would require is likewise your own","benefit."],"id":517,"m":"If you can spare the time, I would like to speak with you shortly. What I want from you will also be to your own benefit."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I have no superfluous leisure, my stay must be stolen out of other","affairs, but I will attend you a while."],"id":518,"m":"I have no time to spare; anything I stay for has to be stolen from other business. But I will wait for you a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["[To Claudio aside] Son, I have overheard what hath passed between","you and your sister. Angelo had never the purpose to corrupt her; only","he hath made an assay of her virtue, to practise his judgement with the","disposition of natures. She, having the truth of honour in her, hath","made him that gracious denial which he is most glad to receive. I am","confessor to Angelo, and I know this to be true; therefore prepare","yourself to death. Do not satisfy your resolution with hopes that are","fallible. Tomorrow you must die; go to your knees and make ready."],"id":519,"m":"(Aside to Claudio) My son, I have overheard what passed between you and your sister. Angelo never meant to corrupt her. He was only testing her virtue, to practise his judgment of human nature. She, having true honour in her, gave him the gracious refusal he was very glad to receive. I am Angelo's confessor, and I know this is true. So prepare yourself for death. Don't comfort your resolve with hopes that will fail you. Tomorrow you must die. Go down on your knees and get ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Let me ask my sister pardon. I am so out of love with life that I will","sue to be rid of it."],"id":520,"m":"Let me ask my sister's forgiveness. I am so out of love with life that I will beg to be rid of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Hold you there. Farewell."],"id":521,"m":"Keep to that. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Claudio.","id":522},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost.","id":523},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["Provost, a word with you."],"id":524,"m":"Provost, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["What's your will, father?"],"id":525,"m":"What do you want, father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["That, now you are come, you will be gone. Leave me a while with the","maid; my mind promises with my habit no loss shall touch her by my","company."],"id":526,"m":"That, now you have come, you will go. Leave me alone with the young woman for a while. My intentions, and my habit, promise that no harm will come to her in my company."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["In good time."],"id":527,"m":"Very well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Provost.","id":528},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The hand that hath made you fair hath made you good. The goodness that","is cheap in beauty makes beauty brief in goodness; but grace, being the","soul of your complexion, shall keep the body of it ever fair. The","assault that Angelo hath made to you, fortune hath conveyed to my","understanding; and, but that frailty hath examples for his falling, I","should wonder at Angelo. How will you do to content this substitute,","and to save your brother?"],"id":529,"m":"The hand that made you beautiful has made you good. Goodness that holds beauty cheap soon makes the beauty lose its goodness; but grace, being the soul of your nature, will keep your outward self always beautiful. Chance has let me know of the assault Angelo has made on you, and if weakness did not have so many examples of falling like his, I would be amazed at Angelo. What will you do to satisfy this deputy and save your brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I am now going to resolve him. I had rather my brother die by the law","than my son should be unlawfully born. But, O, how much is the good","Duke deceived in Angelo! If ever he return, and I can speak to him, I","will open my lips in vain, or discover his government."],"id":530,"m":"I am going now to give him my answer. I would rather my brother died lawfully than that a son of mine should be born unlawfully. But oh, how badly the good Duke has been deceived in Angelo! If he ever comes back and I can speak to him, I will either be wasting my breath or I will expose how Angelo has governed."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["That shall not be much amiss. Yet, as the matter now stands, he will","avoid your accusation: he made trial of you only. Therefore fasten your","ear on my advisings, to the love I have in doing good, a remedy","presents itself. I do make myself believe that you may most","uprighteously do a poor wronged lady a merited benefit; redeem your","brother from the angry law; do no stain to your own gracious person;","and much please the absent Duke, if peradventure he shall ever return","to have hearing of this business."],"id":531,"m":"That would not be a bad thing. But as things stand now, he will dodge your accusation by saying he was only testing you. So listen carefully to my advice. Out of my love of doing good, a remedy suggests itself. I truly believe you can, with complete integrity, do a poor, wronged lady a deserved favour, save your brother from the angry law, keep your own gracious self unstained, and greatly please the absent Duke, if by chance he ever returns to hear about this business."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Let me hear you speak farther. I have spirit to do anything that","appears not foul in the truth of my spirit."],"id":532,"m":"Tell me more. I have the courage to do anything that does not seem foul to my conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Virtue is bold, and goodness never fearful. Have you not heard speak of","Mariana, the sister of Frederick, the great soldier who miscarried at","sea?"],"id":533,"m":"Virtue is bold, and goodness never afraid. Have you heard of Mariana, the sister of Frederick, the great soldier who was lost at sea?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I have heard of the lady, and good words went with her name."],"id":534,"m":"I have heard of the lady, and people always spoke well of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["She should this Angelo have married, was affianced to her oath, and the","nuptial appointed. Between which time of the contract and limit of the","solemnity, her brother Frederick was wrecked at sea, having in that","perished vessel the dowry of his sister. But mark how heavily this","befell to the poor gentlewoman. There she lost a noble and renowned","brother, in his love toward her ever most kind and natural; with him,","the portion and sinew of her fortune, her marriage dowry; with both,","her combinate husband, this well-seeming Angelo."],"id":535,"m":"Angelo was supposed to marry her. He was formally engaged to her under oath, and the wedding day was fixed. Between the contract and the ceremony her brother Frederick was shipwrecked, and her dowry went down with him in that ship. Think how hard this was for the poor lady. She lost a noble, famous brother who had always loved her dearly; with him she lost her fortune, her dowry; and with both she lost her promised husband, this respectable-looking Angelo."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Can this be so? Did Angelo so leave her?"],"id":536,"m":"Can this be true? Did Angelo really leave her like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Left her in her tears, and dried not one of them with his comfort,","swallowed his vows whole, pretending in her discoveries of dishonour;","in few, bestowed her on her own lamentation, which she yet wears for","his sake; and he, a marble to her tears, is washed with them, but","relents not."],"id":537,"m":"He left her in tears and did not dry a single one of them with comfort. He swallowed his vows whole, claiming he had found she was dishonoured. In short, he left her to her grief, which she still wears for his sake; and he, like marble in the rain, is washed by her tears but never softens."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["What a merit were it in death to take this poor maid from the world!","What corruption in this life, that it will let this man live! But how","out of this can she avail?"],"id":538,"m":"How good it would be of death to take this poor woman out of the world! And how corrupt this life is, to let this man live! But how can she gain anything from this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It is a rupture that you may easily heal, and the cure of it not only","saves your brother, but keeps you from dishonour in doing it."],"id":539,"m":"It is a break you can easily mend, and mending it not only saves your brother but keeps you from dishonour in doing so."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Show me how, good father."],"id":540,"m":"Show me how, good father."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This forenamed maid hath yet in her the continuance of her first","affection. His unjust unkindness, that in all reason should have","quenched her love, hath, like an impediment in the current, made it","more violent and unruly. Go you to Angelo; answer his requiring with a","plausible obedience; agree with his demands to the point. Only refer","yourself to this advantage: first, that your stay with him may not be","long; that the time may have all shadow and silence in it; and the","place answer to convenience. This being granted in course, and now","follows all. We shall advise this wronged maid to stead up your","appointment, go in your place. If the encounter acknowledge itself","hereafter, it may compel him to her recompense; and here, by this, is","your brother saved, your honour untainted, the poor Mariana advantaged,","and the corrupt deputy scaled. The maid will I frame and make fit for","his attempt. If you think well to carry this as you may, the doubleness","of the benefit defends the deceit from reproof. What think you of it?"],"id":541,"m":"This woman I mentioned still feels the love she first felt. His unjust cruelty, which by rights should have killed her love, has instead, like a dam in a stream, made it stronger and wilder. Go to Angelo. Answer his demand with convincing obedience and agree to everything he asks. Only make these conditions: first, that you do not stay with him long; that the meeting happens in darkness and silence; and that the place is convenient. Once that is agreed, everything follows. We will advise this wronged woman to keep your appointment and go in your place. If the meeting comes out later, it may force him to make amends to her. And in this way your brother is saved, your honour kept clean, poor Mariana helped, and the corrupt deputy weighed and found wanting. I will prepare the woman and make her ready for his advances. If you are willing to carry this out, the double good it does will protect the trick from blame. What do you think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["The image of it gives me content already, and I trust it will grow to a","most prosperous perfection."],"id":542,"m":"Just picturing it makes me happy already, and I trust it will turn out very successfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It lies much in your holding up. Haste you speedily to Angelo; if for","this night he entreat you to his bed, give him promise of satisfaction.","I will presently to Saint Luke's; there at the moated grange resides","this dejected Mariana. At that place call upon me; and dispatch with","Angelo, that it may be quickly."],"id":543,"m":"Much depends on how well you play your part. Hurry to Angelo. If he asks you to come to his bed tonight, promise to satisfy him. I will go at once to Saint Luke's; there, at the moated farmhouse, this unhappy Mariana lives. Come and find me there, and settle things with Angelo quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I thank you for this comfort. Fare you well, good father."],"id":544,"m":"Thank you for this comfort. Goodbye, good father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Isabella.","id":545},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"The street before the prison.","id":546},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Elbow, Pompey and Officers.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Nay, if there be no remedy for it, but that you will needs buy and sell","men and women like beasts, we shall have all the world drink brown and","white bastard."],"id":548,"m":"No, if nothing can be done about it, and you insist on buying and selling men and women like animals, the whole world will be drinking brown and white bastard (a sweet wine)."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O heavens, what stuff is here?"],"id":549,"m":"Oh heavens, what nonsense is this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["'Twas never merry world since, of two usuries, the merriest was put","down, and the worser allowed by order of law a furred gown to keep him","warm; and furred with fox on lambskins too, to signify that craft,","being richer than innocency, stands for the facing."],"id":550,"m":"The world has never been merry since, of the two kinds of usury, the merriest was banned and the worse was allowed by law to wear a furred gown to keep it warm; and furred with fox on lambskin too, to show that cunning, being richer than innocence, makes the trimming."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Come your way, sir.—Bless you, good father friar."],"id":551,"m":"Come along, sir. God bless you, good father friar."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["And you, good brother father. What offence hath this man made you, sir?"],"id":552,"m":"And you, good brother father. What crime has this man committed, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Marry, sir, he hath offended the law; and, sir, we take him to be a","thief too, sir; for we have found upon him, sir, a strange picklock,","which we have sent to the deputy."],"id":553,"m":"Why, sir, he has broken the law. And, sir, we think he's a thief too, sir, because we found a strange lock-pick on him, sir, which we have sent to the deputy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Fie, sirrah, a bawd, a wicked bawd;","The evil that thou causest to be done,","That is thy means to live. Do thou but think","What 'tis to cram a maw or clothe a back","From such a filthy vice. Say to thyself,","From their abominable and beastly touches","I drink, I eat, array myself, and live.","Canst thou believe thy living is a life,","So stinkingly depending? Go mend, go mend."],"id":554,"m":"Shame on you, a pimp, a wicked pimp! The evil you cause to be done is how you make your living. Just think what it means to fill your belly or clothe your back from such a filthy vice. Say to yourself, \"From their disgusting, beastly gropings I drink, I eat, I dress and I live.\" Can you believe your living is a life when it depends on such stink? Go and reform, go and reform."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Indeed, it does stink in some sort, sir. But yet, sir, I would prove—"],"id":555,"m":"It does stink in a way, sir, I admit. But still, sir, I would prove..."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Nay, if the devil have given thee proofs for sin,","Thou wilt prove his. Take him to prison, officer.","Correction and instruction must both work","Ere this rude beast will profit."],"id":556,"m":"No, if the devil has given you arguments to defend sin, you will prove to be his. Take him to prison, officer. Punishment and teaching must both work on this rough beast before he gets any good from them."},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["He must before the deputy, sir; he has given him warning. The deputy","cannot abide a whoremaster. If he be a whoremonger and comes before","him, he were as good go a mile on his errand."],"id":557,"m":"He has to go before the deputy, sir; he has been warned. The deputy can't stand a whoremaster. If a whoremonger comes before him, he'd do better to go a mile out of his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["That we were all, as some would seem to be,","Free from our faults, as faults from seeming, free!"],"id":558,"m":"If only we were all what some people seem to be, free from faults; or that faults were free from disguising themselves!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["His neck will come to your waist—a cord, sir."],"id":559,"m":"His neck will be like your waist: tied with a cord, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucio.","id":560},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I spy comfort, I cry bail! Here's a gentleman, and a friend of mine."],"id":561,"m":"I see help! I call for bail! Here's a gentleman, and a friend of mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["How now, noble Pompey? What, at the wheels of Caesar? Art thou led in","triumph? What, is there none of Pygmalion's images, newly made woman,","to be had now, for putting the hand in the pocket and extracting it","clutched? What reply, ha? What say'st thou to this tune, matter, and","method? Is't not drowned i' th' last rain, ha? What say'st thou, trot?","Is the world as it was, man? Which is the way? Is it sad and few words?","Or how? The trick of it?"],"id":562,"m":"Well now, noble Pompey? What, tied to Caesar's chariot wheels? Being led in triumph? What, are there no statues like Pygmalion's, just turned into women, to be had now by putting your hand in your pocket and pulling it out full of cash? What do you say? What do you think of this song, this business, this style? Has it drowned in the last rainstorm? What do you say, old woman? Is the world the way it was, man? What's the fashion? Is it gloomy and quiet? Or what? How does it go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Still thus, and thus; still worse!"],"id":563,"m":"Still like this; always worse!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["How doth my dear morsel, thy mistress? Procures she still, ha?"],"id":564,"m":"How is my dear little morsel, your mistress? Is she still procuring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Troth, sir, she hath eaten up all her beef, and she is herself in the","tub."],"id":565,"m":"Honestly, sir, she has eaten all her beef, and now she's in the tub herself (taking the sweating cure for disease)."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Why, 'tis good. It is the right of it. It must be so. Ever your fresh","whore and your powdered bawd; an unshunned consequence; it must be so.","Art going to prison, Pompey?"],"id":566,"m":"Why, good. That's right. It has to be like that. Always your fresh whore and your salted bawd; it's unavoidable; it has to be like that. Are you going to prison, Pompey?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Yes, faith, sir."],"id":567,"m":"Yes, I'm afraid so, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Why, 'tis not amiss, Pompey. Farewell. Go, say I sent thee thither. For","debt, Pompey? Or how?"],"id":568,"m":"Why, that's not a bad thing, Pompey. Goodbye. Go and say I sent you there. For debt, Pompey? Or what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["For being a bawd, for being a bawd."],"id":569,"m":"For being a pimp, for being a pimp."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Well, then, imprison him. If imprisonment be the due of a bawd, why,","'tis his right. Bawd is he doubtless, and of antiquity, too. Bawd born.","Farewell, good Pompey. Commend me to the prison, Pompey. You will turn","good husband now, Pompey; you will keep the house."],"id":570,"m":"Well then, lock him up. If prison is what a pimp deserves, why, he's got his rights. He's a pimp, no doubt about it, and from an old family too. A born pimp. Goodbye, good Pompey. Give my regards to the prison, Pompey. You'll be a good householder now, Pompey; you'll stay at home."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I hope, sir, your good worship will be my bail."],"id":571,"m":"I hope, sir, your good worship will put up my bail."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["No, indeed, will I not, Pompey; it is not the wear. I will pray,","Pompey, to increase your bondage. If you take it not patiently, why,","your mettle is the more. Adieu, trusty Pompey.—Bless you, friar."],"id":572,"m":"No, I certainly won't, Pompey. It isn't the fashion. I'll pray, Pompey, for your sentence to be made longer. If you don't take it patiently, why, you'll show all the more spirit. Goodbye, faithful Pompey. God bless you, friar."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["And you."],"id":573,"m":"And you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Does Bridget paint still, Pompey, ha?"],"id":574,"m":"Does Bridget still paint her face, Pompey, eh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Come your ways, sir, come."],"id":575,"m":"Come along, sir, come."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["You will not bail me then, sir?"],"id":576,"m":"So you won't bail me, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Then, Pompey, nor now.—What news abroad, friar? What news?"],"id":577,"m":"Not then, Pompey, and not now. What's the news out there, friar? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ELBOW","lines":["Come your ways, sir, come."],"id":578,"m":"Come along, sir, come."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Go to kennel, Pompey, go."],"id":579,"m":"Off to your kennel, Pompey, go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Elbow, Pompey and Officers.","id":580},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","cont":1,"lines":["What news, friar, of the Duke?"],"id":581,"m":"What news, friar, of the Duke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I know none. Can you tell me of any?"],"id":582,"m":"I don't know any. Can you tell me some?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Some say he is with the Emperor of Russia; other some, he is in Rome.","But where is he, think you?"],"id":583,"m":"Some say he's with the Emperor of Russia; others say he's in Rome. But where do you think he is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I know not where, but wheresoever, I wish him well."],"id":584,"m":"I don't know where, but wherever he is, I wish him well."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["It was a mad fantastical trick of him to steal from the state and usurp","the beggary he was never born to. Lord Angelo dukes it well in his","absence. He puts transgression to't."],"id":585,"m":"It was a crazy, fanciful trick of his to sneak away from the state and take up a beggar's life he was never born to. Lord Angelo plays the duke well while he's away. He comes down hard on wrongdoing."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["He does well in't."],"id":586,"m":"He does well to."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["A little more lenity to lechery would do no harm in him. Something too","crabbed that way, friar."],"id":587,"m":"A bit more leniency towards lechery wouldn't hurt him. He's a bit too sour in that department, friar."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It is too general a vice, and severity must cure it."],"id":588,"m":"It is too widespread a vice, and severity must cure it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Yes, in good sooth, the vice is of a great kindred; it is well allied;","but it is impossible to extirp it quite, friar, till eating and","drinking be put down. They say this Angelo was not made by man and","woman after this downright way of creation. Is it true, think you?"],"id":589,"m":"Yes, honestly, the vice has a big family; it is well connected. But it is impossible to root it out completely, friar, until eating and drinking are banned. They say this Angelo wasn't made by a man and a woman in the usual straightforward way of creation. Do you think it's true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["How should he be made, then?"],"id":590,"m":"How was he made, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Some report a sea-maid spawned him; some, that he was begot between two","stockfishes. But it is certain that when he makes water, his urine is","congealed ice; that I know to be true. And he is a motion ungenerative;","that's infallible."],"id":591,"m":"Some say a mermaid spawned him; some that he was fathered between two dried cod. But it's certain that when he pisses, his urine is frozen ice; I know that's true. And he's a puppet that can't breed; that's certain."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You are pleasant, sir, and speak apace."],"id":592,"m":"You are amusing, sir, and you talk fast."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Why, what a ruthless thing is this in him, for the rebellion of a","codpiece to take away the life of a man! Would the Duke that is absent","have done this? Ere he would have hanged a man for the getting a","hundred bastards, he would have paid for the nursing a thousand. He had","some feeling of the sport; he knew the service, and that instructed him","to mercy."],"id":593,"m":"Why, how heartless of him, to take a man's life because his codpiece rebelled! Would the absent Duke have done this? Before he hanged a man for fathering a hundred bastards, he'd have paid to nurse a thousand. He had some feeling for the sport. He knew the game, and that taught him mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I never heard the absent Duke much detected for women; he was not","inclined that way."],"id":594,"m":"I never heard that the absent Duke was much accused over women. He wasn't that way inclined."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["O, sir, you are deceived."],"id":595,"m":"Oh, sir, you are wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["'Tis not possible."],"id":596,"m":"It's not possible."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Who, not the Duke? Yes, your beggar of fifty; and his use was to put a","ducat in her clack-dish. The Duke had crotchets in him. He would be","drunk too, that let me inform you."],"id":597,"m":"What, not the Duke? Yes, with a beggar woman of fifty. And his habit was to drop a ducat in her begging bowl. The Duke had his quirks. He'd get drunk too, let me tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You do him wrong, surely."],"id":598,"m":"Surely you do him wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Sir, I was an inward of his. A shy fellow was the Duke; and I believe I","know the cause of his withdrawing."],"id":599,"m":"Sir, I was one of his close friends. The Duke was a secretive fellow, and I think I know why he went away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What, I prithee, might be the cause?"],"id":600,"m":"What, please, might the reason be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["No, pardon. 'Tis a secret must be locked within the teeth and the lips.","But this I can let you understand: the greater file of the subject held","the Duke to be wise."],"id":601,"m":"No, forgive me. It's a secret that must stay locked behind the teeth and lips. But I can tell you this much: most of his subjects thought the Duke was wise."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Wise? Why, no question but he was."],"id":602,"m":"Wise? Why, there's no question he was."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["A very superficial, ignorant, unweighing fellow."],"id":603,"m":"A very shallow, ignorant, thoughtless fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Either this is envy in you, folly, or mistaking. The very stream of his","life, and the business he hath helmed, must upon a warranted need give","him a better proclamation. Let him be but testimonied in his own","bringings-forth, and he shall appear to the envious a scholar, a","statesman, and a soldier. Therefore you speak unskilfully. Or, if your","knowledge be more, it is much darkened in your malice."],"id":604,"m":"This is either envy in you, or foolishness, or a mistake. The whole course of his life and the business he has steered must, if they were properly examined, give him a better reputation. Judge him only by what he has done, and even the envious will see a scholar, a statesman and a soldier. So you speak without knowledge; or, if you know more, your malice has darkened it badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Sir, I know him, and I love him."],"id":605,"m":"Sir, I know him, and I love him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Love talks with better knowledge, and knowledge with dearer love."],"id":606,"m":"Love speaks with better knowledge, and knowledge with deeper love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Come, sir, I know what I know."],"id":607,"m":"Come, sir, I know what I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I can hardly believe that, since you know not what you speak. But, if","ever the Duke return, as our prayers are he may, let me desire you to","make your answer before him. If it be honest you have spoke, you have","courage to maintain it. I am bound to call upon you, and I pray you","your name?"],"id":608,"m":"I can hardly believe that, since you don't know what you're saying. But if the Duke ever returns, as we pray he will, I ask you to answer for this in front of him. If you have spoken honestly, you'll have the courage to stand by it. I am duty bound to call on you. What is your name, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Sir, my name is Lucio, well known to the Duke."],"id":609,"m":"Sir, my name is Lucio, well known to the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["He shall know you better, sir, if I may live to report you."],"id":610,"m":"He shall know you better, sir, if I live to report you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I fear you not."],"id":611,"m":"I'm not afraid of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O, you hope the Duke will return no more; or you imagine me too","unhurtful an opposite. But indeed, I can do you little harm. You'll","forswear this again."],"id":612,"m":"Oh, you hope the Duke won't come back, or you think I'm too harmless an opponent. And in truth I can do you little harm. You'll deny all this later."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I'll be hanged first! Thou art deceived in me, friar. But no more of","this. Canst thou tell if Claudio die tomorrow or no?"],"id":613,"m":"I'll be hanged first! You've got me wrong, friar. But enough of this. Can you tell me whether Claudio dies tomorrow or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Why should he die, sir?"],"id":614,"m":"Why should he die, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Why? For filling a bottle with a tun-dish. I would the Duke we talk of","were returned again. This ungenitured agent will unpeople the province","with continency. Sparrows must not build in his house-eaves because","they are lecherous. The Duke yet would have dark deeds darkly answered.","He would never bring them to light. Would he were returned! Marry, this","Claudio is condemned for untrussing. Farewell, good friar, I prithee","pray for me. The Duke, I say to thee again, would eat mutton on","Fridays. He's now past it; yet, and, I say to thee, he would mouth with","a beggar though she smelt brown bread and garlic. Say that I said so.","Farewell."],"id":615,"m":"Why? For filling a bottle with a funnel. I wish the Duke we're talking about would come back. This sexless deputy will empty the province with his chastity. Sparrows won't be allowed to nest under his eaves because they're lecherous. The Duke would have had dark deeds dealt with in the dark; he'd never have dragged them into the light. I wish he'd come back! Why, this Claudio is condemned for undoing his breeches. Goodbye, good friar; please pray for me. The Duke, I tell you again, would eat mutton (loose women) on Fridays. He's past it now; and yet, I tell you, he'd kiss a beggar woman even if she stank of brown bread and garlic. Say I said so. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":616},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["No might nor greatness in mortality","Can censure 'scape. Back-wounding calumny","The whitest virtue strikes. What king so strong","Can tie the gall up in the slanderous tongue?","But who comes here?"],"id":617,"m":"No power or greatness in this world can escape criticism. Slander that stabs in the back strikes even the purest virtue. What king is strong enough to tie up the poison in a slanderous tongue? But who is coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Escalus, Provost and Officers with Mistress Overdone, a Bawd.","id":618},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Go, away with her to prison."],"id":619,"m":"Go on, take her away to prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["Good my lord, be good to me. Your honour is accounted a merciful man,","good my lord."],"id":620,"m":"My good lord, be kind to me. Your honour is thought to be a merciful man, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Double and treble admonition, and still forfeit in the same kind? This","would make mercy swear and play the tyrant."],"id":621,"m":"Warned twice and three times, and still guilty of the same offence? This would make mercy swear and act the tyrant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["A bawd of eleven years' continuance, may it please your honour."],"id":622,"m":"She has been a madam for eleven years, if it please your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAWD","lines":["My lord, this is one Lucio's information against me. Mistress Kate","Keepdown was with child by him in the Duke's time; he promised her","marriage. His child is a year and a quarter old come Philip and Jacob.","I have kept it myself; and see how he goes about to abuse me."],"id":623,"m":"My lord, this is that Lucio informing against me. Mistress Kate Keepdown was pregnant by him in the Duke's time; he promised to marry her. His child will be a year and a quarter old on the first of May. I have looked after it myself, and look how he sets about ruining me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["That fellow is a fellow of much license. Let him be called before us.","Away with her to prison. Go to, no more words."],"id":624,"m":"That fellow takes a lot of liberties. Have him called before us. Take her away to prison. Go on, not another word."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Officers with Bawd.","id":625},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","cont":1,"lines":["Provost, my brother Angelo will not be altered; Claudio must die","tomorrow. Let him be furnished with divines, and have all charitable","preparation. If my brother wrought by my pity, it should not be so with","him."],"id":626,"m":"Provost, my colleague Angelo will not change his mind. Claudio must die tomorrow. Give him priests and every charitable preparation. If my colleague acted with my pity, it would not be like this for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["So please you, this friar hath been with him, and advised him for th'","entertainment of death."],"id":627,"m":"If you please, this friar has been with him and advised him how to meet death."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Good even, good father."],"id":628,"m":"Good evening, good father."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Bliss and goodness on you!"],"id":629,"m":"Blessings and goodness on you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Of whence are you?"],"id":630,"m":"Where are you from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Not of this country, though my chance is now","To use it for my time. I am a brother","Of gracious order, late come from the See","In special business from his Holiness."],"id":631,"m":"Not from this country, though I happen now to be staying here for a time. I am a brother of a holy order, recently arrived from the Pope's court on special business for his Holiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["What news abroad i' th' world?"],"id":632,"m":"What news is there out in the world?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["None, but that there is so great a fever on goodness that the","dissolution of it must cure it. Novelty is only in request, and as it","is as dangerous to be aged in any kind of course as it is virtuous to","be constant in any undertaking. There is scarce truth enough alive to","make societies secure; but security enough to make fellowships","accursed. Much upon this riddle runs the wisdom of the world. This news","is old enough, yet it is every day's news. I pray you, sir, of what","disposition was the Duke?"],"id":633,"m":"None, except that goodness has such a fever that only its death can cure it. Only novelty is wanted, and it is as dangerous to stick to any one way of life for long as it is virtuous to be constant in anything you undertake. There is hardly enough honesty alive to make people safe in their dealings, but there are plenty of guarantees to make partnerships a curse. The wisdom of the world mostly turns on this riddle. This news is old, and yet it is every day's news. Tell me, sir, what kind of man was the Duke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["One that, above all other strifes, contended especially to know","himself."],"id":634,"m":"One who struggled above all else to know himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What pleasure was he given to?"],"id":635,"m":"What pleasures did he enjoy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Rather rejoicing to see another merry, than merry at anything which","professed to make him rejoice. A gentleman of all temperance. But leave","we him to his events, with a prayer they may prove prosperous, and let","me desire to know how you find Claudio prepared. I am made to","understand that you have lent him visitation."],"id":636,"m":"He was happier seeing others merry than being merry at anything meant to entertain him. A gentleman of complete self-control. But let us leave him to his fortunes, with a prayer that they turn out well, and let me ask how you find Claudio prepared. I understand you have visited him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["He professes to have received no sinister measure from his judge, but","most willingly humbles himself to the determination of justice. Yet had","he framed to himself, by the instruction of his frailty, many deceiving","promises of life, which I, by my good leisure, have discredited to him,","and now he is resolved to die."],"id":637,"m":"He says his judge has not treated him unfairly, and he submits most willingly to the decision of justice. But, led on by his weakness, he had invented many false hopes of life for himself, which I have patiently shown him to be false, and now he is resolved to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["You have paid the heavens your function, and the prisoner the very debt","of your calling. I have laboured for the poor gentleman to the","extremest shore of my modesty, but my brother justice have I found so","severe that he hath forced me to tell him he is indeed Justice."],"id":638,"m":"You have done your duty to heaven and paid the prisoner what your calling owes him. I have worked for the poor gentleman to the furthest limit of what my position allows, but I have found my colleague so severe in his justice that he has made me tell him he is Justice itself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["If his own life answer the straitness of his proceeding, it shall","become him well; wherein if he chance to fail, he hath sentenced","himself."],"id":639,"m":"If his own life lives up to the strictness of his actions, that will be to his credit; but if he happens to fail, he has sentenced himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I am going to visit the prisoner. Fare you well."],"id":640,"m":"I am going to visit the prisoner. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Peace be with you."],"id":641,"m":"Peace be with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Escalus and Provost.","id":642},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["He who the sword of heaven will bear","Should be as holy as severe,","Pattern in himself to know,","Grace to stand, and virtue go;","More nor less to others paying","Than by self-offences weighing.","Shame to him whose cruel striking","Kills for faults of his own liking!","Twice treble shame on Angelo,","To weed my vice, and let his grow!","O, what may man within him hide,","Though angel on the outward side!","How may likeness, made in crimes,","Make practice on the times,","To draw with idle spiders' strings","Most ponderous and substantial things!","Craft against vice I must apply.","With Angelo tonight shall lie","His old betrothed but despised.","So disguise shall, by th' disguised,","Pay with falsehood false exacting,","And perform an old contracting."],"id":643,"m":"The man who carries heaven's sword of justice should be as holy as he is severe. He should find the pattern in himself, with grace to stand firm and virtue to act, punishing others no more and no less than he would weigh his own offences. Shame on the man whose cruel blows kill others for faults he likes to commit himself! Twice and three times shame on Angelo, who weeds out my vice and lets his own grow! Oh, what a man may hide inside, though he looks like an angel on the outside! How false appearances, made up of crimes, can deceive the world, pulling in heavy, solid things with flimsy spiders' threads! I must use cunning against vice. Tonight his old fiancée, whom he scorned, will lie with Angelo. So disguise will use a disguised woman to pay back his false demand with falsehood, and fulfil an old contract."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":644},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"A room in Mariana's house.","id":645},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mariana and a Boy singing.","id":646},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["~Take, O take those lips away,","~That so sweetly were forsworn,","~And those eyes, the break of day,","~Lights that do mislead the morn.","~But my kisses bring again,","~Bring again;","~Seals of love, but sealed in vain,","~Sealed in vain."],"id":647,"m":"~Take your lips away, oh take them, those lips that broke their promises so sweetly; and take those eyes, bright as daybreak, lights that fool the morning. But bring my kisses back, bring them back: seals of love, but sealed for nothing, sealed for nothing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke as a Friar.","id":648},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Break off thy song, and haste thee quick away;","Here comes a man of comfort, whose advice","Hath often stilled my brawling discontent."],"id":649,"m":"Stop your song and hurry away. Here comes a man of comfort, whose advice has often calmed my noisy unhappiness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Boy.","id":650},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","cont":1,"lines":["I cry you mercy, sir, and well could wish","You had not found me here so musical.","Let me excuse me, and believe me so,","My mirth it much displeased, but pleased my woe."],"id":651,"m":"I beg your pardon, sir, and I wish you had not found me here enjoying music. Let me explain, and believe me: it did not please my mirth, but it soothed my sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["'Tis good; though music oft hath such a charm","To make bad good and good provoke to harm.","I pray you tell me, hath anybody inquired for me here today? Much upon","this time have I promised here to meet."],"id":652,"m":"That is good, though music often has the power to make bad seem good and stir good to harm. Please tell me, has anyone asked for me here today? I promised to meet someone here at about this time."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["You have not been inquired after. I have sat here all day."],"id":653,"m":"No one has asked for you. I have been sitting here all day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Isabella.","id":654},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I do constantly believe you. The time is come even now. I shall crave","your forbearance a little. Maybe I will call upon you anon for some","advantage to yourself."],"id":655,"m":"I believe you completely. The moment has come. I must ask you to leave us for a little. I may call you back soon for something to your advantage."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["I am always bound to you."],"id":656,"m":"I am always in your debt."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":657},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Very well met, and welcome.","What is the news from this good deputy?"],"id":658,"m":"Good to see you, and welcome. What is the news from this good deputy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["He hath a garden circummured with brick,","Whose western side is with a vineyard backed;","And to that vineyard is a planched gate","That makes his opening with this bigger key.","This other doth command a little door","Which from the vineyard to the garden leads;","There have I made my promise, upon the","Heavy middle of the night to call on him."],"id":659,"m":"He has a garden walled in with brick, with a vineyard on its western side. The vineyard has a gate of planks that this bigger key opens. This other key unlocks a little door that leads from the vineyard into the garden. That is where I have promised to come to him in the dead middle of the night."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["But shall you on your knowledge find this way?"],"id":660,"m":"But will you be able to find the way by yourself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I have ta'en a due and wary note upon't;","With whispering and most guilty diligence,","In action all of precept, he did show me","The way twice o'er."],"id":661,"m":"I took careful note of it. Whispering, and with very guilty care, he showed me the way twice over, with gestures as well as words."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Are there no other tokens","Between you 'greed concerning her observance?"],"id":662,"m":"Were there no other signs agreed between you that she must observe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["No, none, but only a repair i' th' dark,","And that I have possessed him my most stay","Can be but brief, for I have made him know","I have a servant comes with me along,","That stays upon me; whose persuasion is","I come about my brother."],"id":663,"m":"No, none, except that I come in the dark; and I have told him I can only stay a short time, because I have made him believe I bring a servant with me who waits for me and thinks I have come about my brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["'Tis well borne up.","I have not yet made known to Mariana","A word of this.—What ho, within! Come forth."],"id":664,"m":"That is well managed. I have not told Mariana a word of this yet. Hello in there! Come out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mariana.","id":665},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["I pray you be acquainted with this maid;","She comes to do you good."],"id":666,"m":"Please get to know this young woman. She has come to do you good."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I do desire the like."],"id":667,"m":"That is what I want too."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Do you persuade yourself that I respect you?"],"id":668,"m":"Are you convinced that I care for you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Good friar, I know you do, and have found it."],"id":669,"m":"Good friar, I know you do, and I have found it so."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Take, then, this your companion by the hand,","Who hath a story ready for your ear.","I shall attend your leisure; but make haste.","The vaporous night approaches."],"id":670,"m":"Then take your companion by the hand; she has a story ready for you. I will wait for you, but hurry. The misty night is coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Will't please you walk aside?"],"id":671,"m":"Will you walk aside with me?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Mariana and Isabella.","id":672},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O place and greatness, millions of false eyes","Are stuck upon thee; volumes of report","Run with these false, and most contrarious quest","Upon thy doings; thousand escapes of wit","Make thee the father of their idle dream","And rack thee in their fancies."],"id":673,"m":"Oh rank and greatness, millions of false eyes are fixed on you! Volumes of rumour run after your actions on false and contradictory trails. A thousand flights of wit make you the father of their silly dreams and stretch you on the rack of their imaginations."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mariana and Isabella.","id":674},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["Welcome; how agreed?"],"id":675,"m":"Welcome. What have you agreed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["She'll take the enterprise upon her, father,","If you advise it."],"id":676,"m":"She'll take on the task, father, if you advise it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It is not my consent,","But my entreaty too."],"id":677,"m":"I don't just consent to it; I beg her to."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Little have you to say","When you depart from him, but, soft and low,","\"Remember now my brother.\""],"id":678,"m":"You have little to say when you leave him, except, softly and quietly, \"Now remember my brother.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Fear me not."],"id":679,"m":"Don't worry about me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Nor, gentle daughter, fear you not at all.","He is your husband on a pre-contract.","To bring you thus together 'tis no sin,","Sith that the justice of your title to him","Doth flourish the deceit. Come, let us go;","Our corn's to reap, for yet our tithe's to sow."],"id":680,"m":"And, gentle daughter, don't you be afraid at all. He is your husband by an earlier contract. It is no sin to bring you together like this, since the justice of your claim on him makes the trick an honest one. Come, let's go. We have corn to reap, because we still have the seed to sow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":681},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"A room in the prison.","id":682},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost and Pompey.","id":683},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Come hither, sirrah. Can you cut off a man's head?"],"id":684,"m":"Come here, fellow. Can you cut off a man's head?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["If the man be a bachelor, sir, I can; but if he be a married man, he's","his wife's head, and I can never cut off a woman's head."],"id":685,"m":"If the man's a bachelor, sir, I can; but if he's married, he's his wife's head, and I could never cut off a woman's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Come, sir, leave me your snatches, and yield me a direct answer.","Tomorrow morning are to die Claudio and Barnardine. Here is in our","prison a common executioner, who in his office lacks a helper; if you","will take it on you to assist him, it shall redeem you from your gyves;","if not, you shall have your full time of imprisonment, and your","deliverance with an unpitied whipping; for you have been a notorious","bawd."],"id":686,"m":"Come on, sir, spare me your quips and give me a straight answer. Tomorrow morning Claudio and Barnardine are to die. We have a common executioner here in the prison who needs an assistant. If you'll agree to help him, it will free you from your chains. If not, you'll serve your full sentence and be let out with a whipping nobody will pity, because you have been a notorious pimp."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, I have been an unlawful bawd time out of mind, but yet I will be","content to be a lawful hangman. I would be glad to receive some","instruction from my fellow-partner."],"id":687,"m":"Sir, I've been an unlawful pimp for as long as anyone can remember, but I'll be happy to be a lawful hangman. I'd be glad to have some lessons from my new partner."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["What ho, Abhorson! Where's Abhorson, there?"],"id":688,"m":"Hello there, Abhorson! Where's Abhorson?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Abhorson.","id":689},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Do you call, sir?"],"id":690,"m":"Did you call, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Sirrah, here's a fellow will help you tomorrow in your execution. If","you think it meet, compound with him by the year, and let him abide","here with you; if not, use him for the present, and dismiss him. He","cannot plead his estimation with you; he hath been a bawd."],"id":691,"m":"Fellow, here's a man who will help you tomorrow with your execution. If you think fit, hire him by the year and let him live here with you; if not, use him for now and then let him go. He can't stand on his dignity with you; he has been a pimp."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["A bawd, sir? Fie upon him, he will discredit our mystery."],"id":692,"m":"A pimp, sir? Shame on him! He'll disgrace our craft."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Go to, sir; you weigh equally. A feather will turn the scale."],"id":693,"m":"Come off it, sir; you two weigh the same. A feather would tip the scales."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":694},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Pray, sir, by your good favour—for surely, sir, a good favour you have,","but that you have a hanging look—do you call, sir, your occupation a","mystery?"],"id":695,"m":"Excuse me, sir, if you please, for you surely have a good face, sir, except that it has a hanging look: do you call your occupation a craft, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Ay, sir, a mystery."],"id":696,"m":"Yes, sir, a craft."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Painting, sir, I have heard say, is a mystery; and your whores, sir,","being members of my occupation, using painting, do prove my occupation","a mystery. But what mystery there should be in hanging, if I should be","hanged, I cannot imagine."],"id":697,"m":"Painting, sir, I've heard, is a craft; and your whores, sir, who are members of my profession, use paint, which proves my profession is a craft. But what craft there could be in hanging, if I were to be hanged, I can't imagine."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Sir, it is a mystery."],"id":698,"m":"Sir, it is a craft."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Proof."],"id":699,"m":"Prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Every true man's apparel fits your thief. If it be too little for your","thief, your true man thinks it big enough; if it be too big for your","thief, your thief thinks it little enough. So every true man's apparel","fits your thief."],"id":700,"m":"Every honest man's clothes fit a thief. If they're too small for the thief, the honest man thinks they're big enough; if they're too big for the thief, the thief thinks they're small enough. So every honest man's clothes fit a thief. (The hangman keeps his victims' clothes.)"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost.","id":701},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Are you agreed?"],"id":702,"m":"Have you agreed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Sir, I will serve him; for I do find your hangman is a more penitent","trade than your bawd. He doth oftener ask forgiveness."],"id":703,"m":"Sir, I'll work for him, because I find a hangman is a more repentant trade than a pimp: he asks for forgiveness more often."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["You, sirrah, provide your block and your axe tomorrow four o'clock."],"id":704,"m":"You, fellow, have your block and your axe ready tomorrow at four o'clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Come on, bawd. I will instruct thee in my trade. Follow."],"id":705,"m":"Come on, pimp. I'll teach you my trade. Follow me."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I do desire to learn, sir; and I hope, if you have occasion to use me","for your own turn, you shall find me yare. For truly, sir, for your","kindness I owe you a good turn."],"id":706,"m":"I'm keen to learn, sir; and I hope, if you ever need me to do the job on you yourself, you'll find me quick. Honestly, sir, for your kindness I owe you a good turn."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Call hither Barnardine and Claudio."],"id":707,"m":"Bring Barnardine and Claudio here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Abhorson and Pompey.","id":708},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["Th' one has my pity; not a jot the other,","Being a murderer, though he were my brother."],"id":709,"m":"One of them has my pity; the other not a scrap, because he is a murderer, even if he were my brother."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Claudio.","id":710},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["Look, here's the warrant, Claudio, for thy death.","'Tis now dead midnight, and by eight tomorrow","Thou must be made immortal. Where's Barnardine?"],"id":711,"m":"Look, Claudio, here's the warrant for your death. It is now the middle of the night, and by eight tomorrow you must be made immortal. Where's Barnardine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["As fast locked up in sleep as guiltless labour","When it lies starkly in the traveller's bones.","He will not wake."],"id":712,"m":"As soundly locked in sleep as honest tiredness when it lies stiff in a traveller's bones. He won't wake up."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Who can do good on him?","Well, go, prepare yourself. [Knocking within] But hark, what noise?","Heaven give your spirits comfort!"],"id":713,"m":"Who can do him any good? Well, go and prepare yourself. (Knocking) But listen, what's that noise? Heaven comfort your spirit!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Claudio. Knock within.","id":714},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["By and by!—","I hope it is some pardon or reprieve","For the most gentle Claudio."],"id":715,"m":"Just a moment! I hope it is some pardon or reprieve for the most gentle Claudio."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke.","id":716},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["Welcome, father."],"id":717,"m":"Welcome, father."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The best and wholesom'st spirits of the night","Envelop you, good Provost! Who called here of late?"],"id":718,"m":"May the best and most healing spirits of the night surround you, good Provost! Who has called here recently?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["None, since the curfew rung."],"id":719,"m":"No one since the curfew bell."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Not Isabel?"],"id":720,"m":"Not Isabel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["No."],"id":721,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["They will then, ere't be long."],"id":722,"m":"Then they will come before long."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["What comfort is for Claudio?"],"id":723,"m":"What comfort is there for Claudio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["There's some in hope."],"id":724,"m":"There is some in hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["It is a bitter deputy."],"id":725,"m":"He is a bitter deputy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Not so, not so. His life is paralleled","Even with the stroke and line of his great justice.","He doth with holy abstinence subdue","That in himself which he spurs on his power","To qualify in others. Were he mealed with that","Which he corrects, then were he tyrannous;","But this being so, he's just."],"id":726,"m":"Not so, not so. His life runs exactly parallel with the stroke and line of his great justice. With holy self-denial he masters in himself the very things he uses his power to curb in others. If he were stained with what he punishes, he would be a tyrant; but since he is not, he is just."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knocking within. Provost goes to the door.","id":727},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["Now are they come.","This is a gentle provost. Seldom when","The steeled gaoler is the friend of men."],"id":728,"m":"Now they have come. This is a kind provost. It is rare for a hard-hearted jailer to be a friend to men."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knocking within.","id":729},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["How now? What noise? That spirit's possessed with haste","That wounds th' unsisting postern with these strokes."],"id":730,"m":"What's this? What's that noise? Whoever is beating the unresisting back gate like that must be in a great hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Provost returns.","id":731},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["There he must stay until the officer","Arise to let him in. He is called up."],"id":732,"m":"He will have to wait there until the officer gets up to let him in. He has been called."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Have you no countermand for Claudio yet,","But he must die tomorrow?"],"id":733,"m":"Have you still no order cancelling Claudio's death? Must he die tomorrow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["None, sir, none."],"id":734,"m":"None, sir, none."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["As near the dawning, Provost, as it is,","You shall hear more ere morning."],"id":735,"m":"Close as it is to dawn, Provost, you will hear more before morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Happily","You something know, yet I believe there comes","No countermand. No such example have we.","Besides, upon the very siege of justice","Lord Angelo hath to the public ear","Professed the contrary."],"id":736,"m":"Perhaps you know something, but I don't believe any countermand will come. There has never been such a case. Besides, Lord Angelo has publicly declared the opposite from the very seat of justice."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":737},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","cont":1,"lines":["This is his Lordship's man."],"id":738,"m":"This is his lordship's servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["And here comes Claudio's pardon."],"id":739,"m":"And here comes Claudio's pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord hath sent you this note, and by me this further charge: that","you swerve not from the smallest article of it, neither in time,","matter, or other circumstance. Good morrow; for, as I take it, it is","almost day."],"id":740,"m":"My lord has sent you this note, and through me this further order: that you do not depart from the smallest detail of it, in time, matter or any other circumstance. Good morning, for I believe it is almost day."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I shall obey him."],"id":741,"m":"I will obey him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Messenger.","id":742},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["[Aside] This is his pardon, purchased by such sin","For which the pardoner himself is in.","Hence hath offence his quick celerity,","When it is borne in high authority.","When vice makes mercy, mercy's so extended","That for the fault's love is th' offender friended.","Now, sir, what news?"],"id":743,"m":"(Aside) This is his pardon, bought with the very sin the pardoner himself is guilty of. That is why crime moves so fast when it is carried out by those in high authority. When vice grants mercy, mercy goes so far that the offender is befriended for love of his fault. Now, sir, what's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I told you: Lord Angelo, belike thinking me remiss in mine office,","awakens me with this unwonted putting-on; methinks strangely, for he","hath not used it before."],"id":744,"m":"As I told you: Lord Angelo, probably thinking I'm careless in my duty, stirs me up with this unusual urging. It's strange, I think, because he has never done it before."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Pray you, let's hear."],"id":745,"m":"Please, let's hear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["[Reads] Whatsoever you may hear to the contrary, let Claudio be","executed by four of the clock, and in the afternoon, Barnardine. For my","better satisfaction, let me have Claudio's head sent me by five. Let","this be duly performed, with a thought that more depends on it than we","must yet deliver. Thus fail not to do your office, as you will answer","it at your peril.","What say you to this, sir?"],"id":746,"m":"(Reads) \"Whatever you may hear to the contrary, have Claudio executed by four o'clock, and Barnardine in the afternoon. For my greater satisfaction, have Claudio's head sent to me by five. See that this is carried out properly, remembering that more depends on it than we can yet reveal. Do not fail in your duty, or you will answer for it at your peril.\" What do you say to this, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What is that Barnardine who is to be executed in th' afternoon?"],"id":747,"m":"Who is this Barnardine who is to be executed in the afternoon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["A Bohemian born, but here nursed up and bred; one that is a prisoner","nine years old."],"id":748,"m":"A Bohemian by birth, but brought up and raised here. He has been a prisoner for nine years."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["How came it that the absent Duke had not either delivered him to his","liberty, or executed him? I have heard it was ever his manner to do so."],"id":749,"m":"How is it that the absent Duke never either set him free or had him executed? I have heard that was always his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["His friends still wrought reprieves for him; and indeed, his fact till","now in the government of Lord Angelo, came not to an undoubtful proof."],"id":750,"m":"His friends kept getting reprieves for him; and in fact his crime was never proved beyond doubt until now, under Lord Angelo's government."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It is now apparent?"],"id":751,"m":"Is it clear now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Most manifest, and not denied by himself."],"id":752,"m":"Perfectly clear, and he doesn't deny it himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Hath he borne himself penitently in prison? How seems he to be touched?"],"id":753,"m":"Has he behaved like a repentant man in prison? How does he seem to be affected?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["A man that apprehends death no more dreadfully but as a drunken sleep;","careless, reckless, and fearless of what's past, present, or to come;","insensible of mortality and desperately mortal."],"id":754,"m":"He is a man who fears death no more than a drunken sleep; careless, reckless and fearless of what has been, what is and what is to come; with no sense of his mortality, and in desperate danger of dying."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["He wants advice."],"id":755,"m":"He needs spiritual advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["He will hear none. He hath evermore had the liberty of the prison; give","him leave to escape hence, he would not. Drunk many times a day, if not","many days entirely drunk. We have very oft awaked him, as if to carry","him to execution, and showed him a seeming warrant for it. It hath not","moved him at all."],"id":756,"m":"He won't listen to any. He has always had the run of the prison; if you gave him the chance to escape, he wouldn't take it. He's drunk many times a day, if not completely drunk for days at a time. We have very often woken him as if to take him to execution, and shown him a fake warrant for it. It hasn't moved him at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["More of him anon. There is written in your brow, Provost, honesty and","constancy; if I read it not truly, my ancient skill beguiles me. But in","the boldness of my cunning I will lay myself in hazard. Claudio, whom","here you have warrant to execute, is no greater forfeit to the law than","Angelo who hath sentenced him. To make you understand this in a","manifested effect, I crave but four days' respite, for the which you","are to do me both a present and a dangerous courtesy."],"id":757,"m":"More about him later. Honesty and steadiness are written on your forehead, Provost; if I don't read it right, my long experience has fooled me. But, trusting boldly in my judgment, I will take a risk. Claudio, whom you have a warrant to execute here, has broken the law no more than Angelo who sentenced him. To make you see this proved in fact, I ask only for four days' delay, for which you must do me a favour, both immediate and dangerous."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Pray, sir, in what?"],"id":758,"m":"In what, sir, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["In the delaying death."],"id":759,"m":"In delaying his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Alack, how may I do it? Having the hour limited, and an express","command, under penalty, to deliver his head in the view of Angelo? I","may make my case as Claudio's, to cross this in the smallest."],"id":760,"m":"Oh, how can I do that, when the hour is fixed and I have an express order, under penalty, to deliver his head to Angelo for him to see? If I go against this in the smallest way, I may end up like Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["By the vow of mine order, I warrant you, if my instructions may be your","guide. Let this Barnardine be this morning executed, and his head borne","to Angelo."],"id":761,"m":"By the vow of my order, I guarantee you will be safe if you follow my instructions. Have this Barnardine executed this morning, and take his head to Angelo."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Angelo hath seen them both, and will discover the favour."],"id":762,"m":"Angelo has seen them both and will recognise the face."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O, death's a great disguiser, and you may add to it. Shave the head and","tie the beard, and say it was the desire of the penitent to be so bared","before his death. You know the course is common. If anything fall to","you upon this, more than thanks and good fortune, by the saint whom I","profess, I will plead against it with my life."],"id":763,"m":"Oh, death is a great disguiser, and you can add to it. Shave the head and tie up the beard, and say it was the prisoner's wish, in his repentance, to be shaved like that before he died. You know it's commonly done. If anything comes to you from this but thanks and good fortune, I swear by the saint I serve that I will defend you with my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Pardon me, good father; it is against my oath."],"id":764,"m":"Forgive me, good father, it is against my oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Were you sworn to the Duke, or to the Deputy?"],"id":765,"m":"Did you swear to serve the Duke, or the deputy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["To him and to his substitutes."],"id":766,"m":"The Duke and those who stand in for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You will think you have made no offence if the Duke avouch the justice","of your dealing?"],"id":767,"m":"Will you think you have done no wrong if the Duke vouches that you acted justly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["But what likelihood is in that?"],"id":768,"m":"But how likely is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Not a resemblance, but a certainty. Yet since I see you fearful, that","neither my coat, integrity, nor persuasion, can with ease attempt you,","I will go further than I meant, to pluck all fears out of you. Look","you, sir, here is the hand and seal of the Duke. You know the","character, I doubt not, and the signet is not strange to you."],"id":769,"m":"Not likely: certain. But since I see you are afraid, and neither my habit, my honesty nor my persuasion can easily move you, I will go further than I intended to take all your fears away. Look, sir, here is the Duke's handwriting and seal. You know his hand, I'm sure, and the seal is familiar to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I know them both."],"id":770,"m":"I know them both."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The contents of this is the return of the Duke; you shall anon","over-read it at your pleasure, where you shall find within these two","days he will be here. This is a thing that Angelo knows not; for he","this very day receives letters of strange tenour, perchance of the","Duke's death, perchance entering into some monastery; but, by chance,","nothing of what is writ. Look, th' unfolding star calls up the","shepherd. Put not yourself into amazement how these things should be.","All difficulties are but easy when they are known. Call your","executioner, and off with Barnardine's head. I will give him a present","shrift, and advise him for a better place. Yet you are amazed; but this","shall absolutely resolve you. Come away; it is almost clear dawn."],"id":771,"m":"This letter announces the return of the Duke. You can read it through at your leisure in a moment, and you will find he will be here within two days. Angelo does not know this, because today he is receiving letters with strange news, perhaps of the Duke's death, perhaps of his entering a monastery, but none of it what is written here. Look, the morning star is calling the shepherd to unfold his sheep. Don't be bewildered about how all this can be. Every difficulty is easy once it is understood. Call your executioner, and off with Barnardine's head. I will hear his confession at once and advise him for a better place. You are still amazed, but this will settle your mind completely. Come; it's almost broad daylight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":772},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Another room in the prison.","id":773},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pompey.","id":774},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["I am as well acquainted here as I was in our house of profession. One","would think it were Mistress Overdone's own house, for here be many of","her old customers. First, here's young Master Rash; he's in for a","commodity of brown paper and old ginger, nine score and seventeen","pounds; of which he made five marks ready money. Marry, then ginger was","not much in request, for the old women were all dead. Then is there","here one Master Caper, at the suit of Master Three-pile the mercer, for","some four suits of peach-coloured satin, which now peaches him a","beggar. Then have we here young Dizie, and young Master Deep-vow, and","Master Copperspur, and Master Starve-lackey, the rapier and dagger man,","and young Drop-heir that killed lusty Pudding, and Master Forthright","the tilter, and brave Master Shoe-tie the great traveller, and wild","Half-can that stabbed Pots, and I think forty more, all great doers in","our trade, and are now \"for the Lord's sake.\""],"id":775,"m":"I feel as much at home here as I did in our house of business. You'd think it was Mistress Overdone's own place, there are so many of her old customers here. First, there's young Master Rash. He's in for a loan of brown paper and old ginger worth a hundred and ninety-seven pounds, which he sold for five marks cash. Well, ginger wasn't much in demand then, because all the old women had died. Then there's a Master Caper, sued by Master Three-pile the cloth dealer for four suits of peach-coloured satin, which has now impeached him as a beggar. Then we have young Dizzy, young Master Deep-vow, Master Copperspur, Master Starve-lackey the rapier-and-dagger man, young Drop-heir who killed lusty Pudding, Master Forthright the jouster, brave Master Shoe-tie the great traveller, wild Half-can who stabbed Pots, and I think forty more, all big dealers in our trade, and all now begging through the bars \"for the Lord's sake\"."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Abhorson.","id":776},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Sirrah, bring Barnardine hither."],"id":777,"m":"Fellow, bring Barnardine here."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Master Barnardine! You must rise and be hanged, Master Barnardine."],"id":778,"m":"Master Barnardine! You must get up and be hanged, Master Barnardine."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["What ho, Barnardine!"],"id":779,"m":"Hello there, Barnardine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["[Within] A pox o' your throats! Who makes that noise there? What are","you?"],"id":780,"m":"(From inside) Damn your throats! Who's making that noise? Who are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Your friends, sir; the hangman. You must be so good, sir, to rise and","be put to death."],"id":781,"m":"Your friends, sir: the hangman. Be so good, sir, as to get up and be put to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["[Within] Away, you rogue, away; I am sleepy."],"id":782,"m":"(From inside) Go away, you rogue, go away. I'm sleepy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Tell him he must awake, and that quickly too."],"id":783,"m":"Tell him he must wake up, and quickly too."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Pray, Master Barnardine, awake till you are executed, and sleep","afterwards."],"id":784,"m":"Please, Master Barnardine, stay awake until you're executed, and sleep afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Go in to him, and fetch him out."],"id":785,"m":"Go in and fetch him out."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["He is coming, sir, he is coming. I hear his straw rustle."],"id":786,"m":"He's coming, sir, he's coming. I can hear his straw rustling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Barnardine.","id":787},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Is the axe upon the block, sirrah?"],"id":788,"m":"Is the axe on the block, fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["Very ready, sir."],"id":789,"m":"All ready, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["How now, Abhorson? What's the news with you?"],"id":790,"m":"Well, Abhorson? What news do you have?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Truly, sir, I would desire you to clap into your prayers; for, look","you, the warrant's come."],"id":791,"m":"Honestly, sir, I'd like you to get on with your prayers, because, look, the warrant has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["You rogue, I have been drinking all night; I am not fitted for't."],"id":792,"m":"You rogue, I've been drinking all night. I'm not fit for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"POMPEY","lines":["O, the better, sir; for he that drinks all night and is hanged betimes","in the morning may sleep the sounder all the next day."],"id":793,"m":"Oh, all the better, sir, because a man who drinks all night and is hanged early in the morning will sleep all the sounder all the next day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke.","id":794},{"t":"sp","who":"ABHORSON","lines":["Look you, sir, here comes your ghostly father. Do we jest now, think","you?"],"id":795,"m":"Look, sir, here comes your spiritual father. Do you think we're joking now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir, induced by my charity, and hearing how hastily you are to depart,","I am come to advise you, comfort you, and pray with you."],"id":796,"m":"Sir, moved by charity, and hearing how soon you are to depart, I have come to advise you, comfort you and pray with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["Friar, not I. I have been drinking hard all night, and I will have more","time to prepare me, or they shall beat out my brains with billets. I","will not consent to die this day, that's certain."],"id":797,"m":"Not me, friar. I've been drinking hard all night, and I'll have more time to get ready, or they'll have to beat my brains out with logs. I won't agree to die today, that's for sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O, sir, you must; and therefore I beseech you","Look forward on the journey you shall go."],"id":798,"m":"Oh, sir, you must; and so I beg you, look ahead to the journey you are about to take."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["I swear I will not die today for any man's persuasion."],"id":799,"m":"I swear I won't die today, whoever tries to persuade me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["But hear you—"],"id":800,"m":"But listen..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARNARDINE","lines":["Not a word. If you have anything to say to me, come to my ward, for","thence will not I today."],"id":801,"m":"Not a word. If you've got anything to say to me, come to my cell, because I'm not leaving it today."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":802},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Unfit to live or die. O gravel heart!","After him, fellows; bring him to the block."],"id":803,"m":"Unfit to live or die. Oh, heart of gravel! Go after him, fellows, and bring him to the block."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Abhorson and Pompey.","id":804},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost.","id":805},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Now, sir, how do you find the prisoner?"],"id":806,"m":"Now, sir, how do you find the prisoner?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["A creature unprepared, unmeet for death;","And to transport him in the mind he is","Were damnable."],"id":807,"m":"A creature unprepared and unfit for death; and to send him off in his present state of mind would be damnable."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Here in the prison, father,","There died this morning of a cruel fever","One Ragozine, a most notorious pirate,","A man of Claudio's years; his beard and head","Just of his colour. What if we do omit","This reprobate till he were well inclined,","And satisfy the Deputy with the visage","Of Ragozine, more like to Claudio?"],"id":808,"m":"Here in the prison, father, a man called Ragozine died this morning of a cruel fever. He was a notorious pirate, a man of Claudio's age, with beard and hair exactly his colour. What if we leave this wretch until he is in a better frame of mind, and satisfy the deputy with Ragozine's face, which is more like Claudio's?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O, 'tis an accident that heaven provides!","Dispatch it presently; the hour draws on","Prefixed by Angelo. See this be done,","And sent according to command, whiles I","Persuade this rude wretch willingly to die."],"id":809,"m":"Oh, this is a chance sent by heaven! Do it at once. The hour Angelo set is coming. See that it is done and the head sent as ordered, while I persuade this rough wretch to die willingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["This shall be done, good father, presently.","But Barnardine must die this afternoon;","And how shall we continue Claudio,","To save me from the danger that might come","If he were known alive?"],"id":810,"m":"It shall be done at once, good father. But Barnardine must die this afternoon. And how shall we keep Claudio, to save me from the danger I could be in if he were known to be alive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Let this be done:","Put them in secret holds, both Barnardine and Claudio.","Ere twice the sun hath made his journal greeting","To yonder generation, you shall find","Your safety manifested."],"id":811,"m":"Do this: put them both, Barnardine and Claudio, in secret cells. Before the sun has twice made its daily greeting to the people beyond us, you will find your safety made clear."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I am your free dependant."],"id":812,"m":"I am entirely at your service."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Quick, dispatch, and send the head to Angelo."],"id":813,"m":"Quick, hurry, and send the head to Angelo."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Provost.","id":814},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["Now will I write letters to Angelo,","The Provost, he shall bear them, whose contents","Shall witness to him I am near at home;","And that by great injunctions I am bound","To enter publicly. Him I'll desire","To meet me at the consecrated fount,","A league below the city; and from thence,","By cold gradation and well-balanced form.","We shall proceed with Angelo."],"id":815,"m":"Now I will write letters to Angelo, and the Provost shall carry them, saying that I am near home and that, for important reasons, I must make a public entry. I will ask him to meet me at the holy spring, three miles outside the city; and from there, by careful steps and in proper order, we shall proceed against Angelo."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost.","id":816},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Here is the head; I'll carry it myself."],"id":817,"m":"Here is the head. I'll take it myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Convenient is it. Make a swift return;","For I would commune with you of such things","That want no ear but yours."],"id":818,"m":"That is best. Come back quickly, because I want to talk to you about things that no one else should hear."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["I'll make all speed."],"id":819,"m":"I'll be as fast as I can."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":820},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["[Within] Peace, ho, be here!"],"id":821,"m":"(From outside) Peace be here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The tongue of Isabel. She's come to know","If yet her brother's pardon be come hither.","But I will keep her ignorant of her good,","To make her heavenly comforts of despair","When it is least expected."],"id":822,"m":"That is Isabel's voice. She has come to find out whether her brother's pardon has arrived. But I will keep her in ignorance of her good fortune, so as to turn her despair into heavenly comfort when she least expects it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Isabella.","id":823},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Ho, by your leave!"],"id":824,"m":"Hello, may I come in!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Good morning to you, fair and gracious daughter."],"id":825,"m":"Good morning to you, lovely and gracious daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["The better, given me by so holy a man.","Hath yet the Deputy sent my brother's pardon?"],"id":826,"m":"It is a better morning for being wished by so holy a man. Has the deputy sent my brother's pardon yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["He hath released him, Isabel, from the world.","His head is off, and sent to Angelo."],"id":827,"m":"He has released him from the world, Isabel. His head has been cut off and sent to Angelo."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Nay, but it is not so."],"id":828,"m":"No, it can't be true."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It is no other.","Show your wisdom, daughter, in your close patience."],"id":829,"m":"It is just so. Show your wisdom, daughter, by keeping patiently quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, I will to him and pluck out his eyes!"],"id":830,"m":"Oh, I'll go to him and tear his eyes out!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You shall not be admitted to his sight."],"id":831,"m":"You won't be allowed to see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Unhappy Claudio! Wretched Isabel!","Injurious world! Most damned Angelo!"],"id":832,"m":"Unhappy Claudio! Wretched Isabel! Cruel world! Utterly damned Angelo!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This nor hurts him nor profits you a jot.","Forbear it, therefore; give your cause to heaven.","Mark what I say, which you shall find","By every syllable a faithful verity.","The Duke comes home tomorrow;—nay, dry your eyes.","One of our convent, and his confessor,","Gives me this instance. Already he hath carried","Notice to Escalus and Angelo,","Who do prepare to meet him at the gates,","There to give up their power. If you can, pace your wisdom","In that good path that I would wish it go,","And you shall have your bosom on this wretch,","Grace of the Duke, revenges to your heart,","And general honour."],"id":833,"m":"This doesn't hurt him or help you in the least. So stop it, and leave your cause to heaven. Listen to what I say; you will find every syllable of it is the plain truth. The Duke comes home tomorrow. No, dry your eyes. A brother of our convent, his confessor, tells me so. He has already taken the news to Escalus and Angelo, who are getting ready to meet him at the gates and hand back their power there. If you can, walk your wisdom down the good path I want it to take, and you will have your heart's desire on this wretch, the Duke's favour, revenge to satisfy you, and honour from everyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I am directed by you."],"id":834,"m":"I will do as you direct."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This letter, then, to Friar Peter give;","'Tis that he sent me of the Duke's return.","Say, by this token, I desire his company","At Mariana's house tonight. Her cause and yours","I'll perfect him withal, and he shall bring you","Before the Duke; and to the head of Angelo","Accuse him home and home. For my poor self,","I am combined by a sacred vow,","And shall be absent. Wend you with this letter.","Command these fretting waters from your eyes","With a light heart; trust not my holy order,","If I pervert your course.—Who's here?"],"id":835,"m":"Then give this letter to Friar Peter. It is the one he sent me about the Duke's return. Tell him that, by this sign, I want him to come to Mariana's house tonight. I will tell him everything about her case and yours, and he will bring you before the Duke, and you can accuse Angelo to his face, all the way. As for poor me, I am bound by a sacred vow and must be absent. Go with this letter. Command those stinging tears to stop, and keep your heart light. Don't trust my holy order if I lead you wrong. Who's here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucio.","id":836},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Good even. Friar, where is the Provost?"],"id":837,"m":"Good evening. Friar, where is the Provost?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Not within, sir."],"id":838,"m":"Not in, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["O pretty Isabella, I am pale at mine heart to see thine eyes so red.","Thou must be patient. I am fain to dine and sup with water and bran. I","dare not for my head fill my belly. One fruitful meal would set me","to't. But they say the Duke will be here tomorrow. By my troth, Isabel,","I loved thy brother. If the old fantastical duke of dark corners had","been at home, he had lived."],"id":839,"m":"Oh, pretty Isabella, my heart goes pale to see your eyes so red. You must be patient. I have to make do with bread and water for dinner and supper; I daren't fill my belly for fear of losing my head. One good meal would set me off. But they say the Duke will be here tomorrow. Honestly, Isabel, I loved your brother. If the strange old Duke who lurks in dark corners had been at home, your brother would have lived."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Isabella.","id":840},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir, the Duke is marvellous little beholding to your reports; but the","best is, he lives not in them."],"id":841,"m":"Sir, the Duke owes very little thanks to your reports of him; but the good thing is that his real life is not in them."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Friar, thou knowest not the Duke so well as I do. He's a better woodman","than thou tak'st him for."],"id":842,"m":"Friar, you don't know the Duke as well as I do. He's a better hunter of women than you think."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Well, you'll answer this one day. Fare ye well."],"id":843,"m":"Well, you'll answer for this one day. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Nay, tarry, I'll go along with thee. I can tell thee pretty tales of","the Duke."],"id":844,"m":"No, wait, I'll come with you. I can tell you some fine stories about the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You have told me too many of him already, sir, if they be true; if not","true, none were enough."],"id":845,"m":"You have told me too many already, sir, if they are true; and if they're not, even one was too many."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I was once before him for getting a wench with child."],"id":846,"m":"I was once brought before him for getting a girl pregnant."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Did you such a thing?"],"id":847,"m":"Did you really do that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Yes, marry, did I; but I was fain to forswear it. They would else have","married me to the rotten medlar."],"id":848,"m":"Yes, by heaven, I did; but I had to swear I hadn't. Otherwise they would have married me to that rotten fruit."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir, your company is fairer than honest. Rest you well."],"id":849,"m":"Sir, your company is more pleasant than honest. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["By my troth, I'll go with thee to the lane's end. If bawdy talk offend","you, we'll have very little of it. Nay, friar, I am a kind of burr; I","shall stick."],"id":850,"m":"Honestly, I'll go with you to the end of the lane. If dirty talk offends you, we'll keep it to a minimum. No, friar, I'm a kind of burr: I'll stick."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":851},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"A room in Angelo's house.","id":852},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo and Escalus.","id":853},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Every letter he hath writ hath disvouched other."],"id":854,"m":"Every letter he has written has contradicted the one before."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["In most uneven and distracted manner. His actions show much like to","madness; pray heaven his wisdom be not tainted. And why meet him at the","gates and redeliver our authorities there?"],"id":855,"m":"In a most erratic, confused way. His actions look very like madness; heaven grant his mind is not affected. And why meet him at the gates and hand our authority back there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I guess not."],"id":856,"m":"I can't guess."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["And why should we proclaim it in an hour before his entering, that if","any crave redress of injustice, they should exhibit their petitions in","the street?"],"id":857,"m":"And why should we announce, an hour before he enters, that anyone who wants an injustice put right should present their petitions in the street?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["He shows his reason for that: to have a dispatch of complaints, and to","deliver us from devices hereafter, which shall then have no power to","stand against us."],"id":858,"m":"He gives his reason for that: to have all complaints dealt with at once, and to protect us from plots later on, which will then have no power against us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Well, I beseech you, let it be proclaimed.","Betimes i' th' morn I'll call you at your house.","Give notice to such men of sort and suit","As are to meet him."],"id":859,"m":"Well, please have it announced. I will call for you at your house early in the morning. Let the men of rank and standing who are to meet him know."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I shall, sir. Fare you well."],"id":860,"m":"I will, sir. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":861},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Good night.","This deed unshapes me quite, makes me unpregnant","And dull to all proceedings. A deflowered maid;","And by an eminent body that enforced","The law against it! But that her tender shame","Will not proclaim against her maiden loss,","How might she tongue me! Yet reason dares her no,","For my authority bears so credent bulk","That no particular scandal once can touch","But it confounds the breather. He should have lived,","Save that his riotous youth, with dangerous sense,","Might in the times to come have ta'en revenge","By so receiving a dishonoured life","With ransom of such shame. Would yet he had lived.","Alack, when once our grace we have forgot,","Nothing goes right; we would, and we would not."],"id":862,"m":"Good night. This deed has unmade me completely. It leaves me witless and dull for any business. A young woman deflowered, and by a man of high position who enforced the law against that very thing! If her modest shame did not keep her from announcing the loss of her virginity, how she could denounce me! But reason tells her not to, because my authority carries such solid credit that no private accusation can touch it without ruining the person who makes it. He should have lived, except that his wild young blood, feeling the dishonour, might have taken revenge in time to come for being given a disgraced life bought with such shame. And yet I wish he had lived. Oh, once we have forgotten grace, nothing goes right. We want to, and we don't want to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":863},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"Fields without the town.","id":864},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke, in his own habit, and Friar Peter.","id":865},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["These letters at fit time deliver me.","The Provost knows our purpose and our plot.","The matter being afoot, keep your instruction","And hold you ever to our special drift,","Though sometimes you do blench from this to that","As cause doth minister. Go call at Flavius' house,","And tell him where I stay. Give the like notice","To Valencius, Rowland, and to Crassus,","And bid them bring the trumpets to the gate.","But send me Flavius first."],"id":866,"m":"Deliver these letters for me at the right time. The Provost knows our purpose and our plan. Now that it is under way, keep to your instructions and always hold to our particular aim, even if you sometimes have to swerve from one thing to another as the occasion demands. Go and call at Flavius's house and tell him where I am. Give the same message to Valencius, Rowland and Crassus, and tell them to bring the trumpeters to the gate. But send Flavius to me first."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["It shall be speeded well."],"id":867,"m":"It will be done quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Friar Peter.","id":868},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Varrius.","id":869},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I thank thee, Varrius, thou hast made good haste.","Come, we will walk. There's other of our friends","Will greet us here anon. My gentle Varrius."],"id":870,"m":"Thank you, Varrius, you have come very quickly. Come, let us walk. Others of our friends will greet us here soon. My gentle Varrius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":871},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"Street near the city gate.","id":872},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Isabella and Mariana.","id":873},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["To speak so indirectly I am loath;","I would say the truth, but to accuse him so","That is your part; yet I am advised to do it,","He says, to veil full purpose."],"id":874,"m":"I hate to speak in such a roundabout way. I would rather tell the truth, but accusing him like that is your part. Still, he advises me to do it, he says, to hide our full purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Be ruled by him."],"id":875,"m":"Do as he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Besides, he tells me that, if peradventure","He speak against me on the adverse side,","I should not think it strange, for 'tis a physic","That's bitter to sweet end."],"id":876,"m":"Besides, he tells me that if he happens to speak against me, on the other side, I shouldn't think it strange, because it is bitter medicine for a sweet result."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["I would Friar Peter—"],"id":877,"m":"I wish Friar Peter..."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Friar Peter.","id":878},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, peace, the friar is come."],"id":879,"m":"Oh, hush, the friar has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["Come, I have found you out a stand most fit,","Where you may have such vantage on the Duke","He shall not pass you. Twice have the trumpets sounded.","The generous and gravest citizens","Have hent the gates, and very near upon","The Duke is entering. Therefore hence, away."],"id":880,"m":"Come, I have found you a perfect place to stand, where you will be so well placed for the Duke that he can't pass you by. The trumpets have sounded twice. The noblest and most respected citizens have taken up their places at the gates, and the Duke is about to enter. So come on, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":881},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"A public place near the city gate.","id":882},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter at several doors Duke, Varrius, Lords; Angelo, Escalus, Lucio, Provost, Officers and Citizens.","id":883},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["My very worthy cousin, fairly met.","Our old and faithful friend, we are glad to see you."],"id":884,"m":"My most worthy cousin, well met. Our old and faithful friend, we are glad to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO AND ESCALUS","lines":["Happy return be to your royal grace!"],"id":885,"m":"A happy return to your royal Grace!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Many and hearty thankings to you both.","We have made inquiry of you, and we hear","Such goodness of your justice that our soul","Cannot but yield you forth to public thanks,","Forerunning more requital."],"id":886,"m":"Many warm thanks to you both. We have asked about you, and we hear such good things of your justice that we cannot help thanking you publicly, ahead of greater rewards."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["You make my bonds still greater."],"id":887,"m":"You make me even more indebted to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["O, your desert speaks loud, and I should wrong it","To lock it in the wards of covert bosom,","When it deserves with characters of brass","A forted residence 'gainst the tooth of time","And rasure of oblivion. Give me your hand","And let the subject see, to make them know","That outward courtesies would fain proclaim","Favours that keep within.—Come, Escalus,","You must walk by us on our other hand.","And good supporters are you."],"id":888,"m":"Oh, your merit speaks loudly, and I would wrong it if I locked it away inside my heart, when it deserves to be carved in brass and kept in a fortress against the teeth of time and the erasing of forgetfulness. Give me your hand, and let the people see it, so they know that outward courtesy is eager to show the favour held within. Come, Escalus, you must walk on my other side. You are both good supporters."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Friar Peter and Isabella.","id":889},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["Now is your time. Speak loud, and kneel before him."],"id":890,"m":"Now is your chance. Speak loudly, and kneel in front of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Justice, O royal Duke! Vail your regard","Upon a wronged—I would fain have said, a maid.","O worthy prince, dishonour not your eye","By throwing it on any other object","Till you have heard me in my true complaint,","And given me justice, justice, justice, justice!"],"id":891,"m":"Justice, oh royal Duke! Lower your eyes to a wronged... I wanted to say, maiden. Oh worthy prince, do not dishonour your eyes by turning them on anything else until you have heard my true complaint and given me justice, justice, justice, justice!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Relate your wrongs. In what? By whom? Be brief.","Here is Lord Angelo shall give you justice.","Reveal yourself to him."],"id":892,"m":"Tell us your wrongs. In what? By whom? Be brief. Here is Lord Angelo, who will give you justice. Explain yourself to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O worthy Duke,","You bid me seek redemption of the devil.","Hear me yourself, for that which I must speak","Must either punish me, not being believed,","Or wring redress from you. Hear me, O hear me, here!"],"id":893,"m":"Oh worthy Duke, you are telling me to ask the devil for salvation. Hear me yourself, because what I have to say must either get me punished, if I am not believed, or force you to put it right. Hear me, oh hear me, here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["My lord, her wits, I fear me, are not firm.","She hath been a suitor to me for her brother,","Cut off by course of justice."],"id":894,"m":"My lord, I am afraid her mind is not steady. She came to me to plead for her brother, who has been executed in the course of justice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["By course of justice!"],"id":895,"m":"In the course of justice!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["And she will speak most bitterly and strange."],"id":896,"m":"And she will say very bitter and strange things."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Most strange, but yet most truly will I speak.","That Angelo's forsworn, is it not strange?","That Angelo's a murderer, is't not strange?","That Angelo is an adulterous thief,","An hypocrite, a virgin-violator,","Is it not strange and strange?"],"id":897,"m":"Very strange, but very true is what I'll say. That Angelo has broken his word, isn't that strange? That Angelo is a murderer, isn't that strange? That Angelo is an adulterous thief, a hypocrite, a violator of virgins, isn't that strange and strange again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Nay, it is ten times strange."],"id":898,"m":"Why, it is ten times strange."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["It is not truer he is Angelo","Than this is all as true as it is strange.","Nay, it is ten times true, for truth is truth","To th' end of reckoning."],"id":899,"m":"It is no truer that he is Angelo than that all this is as true as it is strange. No, it is ten times true, because truth is truth to the end of time."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Away with her. Poor soul,","She speaks this in th' infirmity of sense."],"id":900,"m":"Take her away. Poor soul, she says this because her mind is sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O Prince, I conjure thee, as thou believ'st","There is another comfort than this world,","That thou neglect me not with that opinion","That I am touched with madness. Make not impossible","That which but seems unlike. 'Tis not impossible","But one, the wicked'st caitiff on the ground,","May seem as shy, as grave, as just, as absolute,","As Angelo; even so may Angelo,","In all his dressings, characts, titles, forms,","Be an arch-villain. Believe it, royal Prince,","If he be less, he's nothing; but he's more,","Had I more name for badness."],"id":901,"m":"Oh prince, I beg you, as you believe there is a comfort beyond this world, don't dismiss me by thinking I am touched with madness. Don't make something impossible just because it seems unlikely. It is not impossible that the most wicked wretch on earth could seem as modest, as serious, as just and as perfect as Angelo; and in the same way Angelo, in all his robes, badges, titles and outward forms, could be an arch-villain. Believe it, royal prince: if he is any less than that, he is nothing; but he is more, if I had more words for wickedness."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["By mine honesty,","If she be mad, as I believe no other,","Her madness hath the oddest frame of sense,","Such a dependency of thing on thing,","As e'er I heard in madness."],"id":902,"m":"On my honour, if she is mad, as I can only believe, her madness has the strangest pattern of sense, with one thing following from another, that I ever heard in madness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O gracious Duke,","Harp not on that; nor do not banish reason","For inequality; but let your reason serve","To make the truth appear where it seems hid,","And hide the false seems true."],"id":903,"m":"Oh gracious Duke, don't keep harping on that, and don't throw out reason because the two of us are unequal. Use your reason instead to bring out the truth where it seems hidden, and hide the falsehood that seems true."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Many that are not mad","Have, sure, more lack of reason. What would you say?"],"id":904,"m":"Many people who are not mad certainly have less reason than this. What do you want to say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I am the sister of one Claudio,","Condemned upon the act of fornication","To lose his head; condemned by Angelo.","I, in probation of a sisterhood,","Was sent to by my brother; one Lucio","As then the messenger."],"id":905,"m":"I am the sister of a man named Claudio, condemned to lose his head for the act of fornication; condemned by Angelo. I was in my trial period before joining a sisterhood when my brother sent to me. A man named Lucio was the messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["That's I, an't like your Grace.","I came to her from Claudio and desired her","To try her gracious fortune with Lord Angelo","For her poor brother's pardon."],"id":906,"m":"That's me, if it please your Grace. I came to her from Claudio and asked her to try her gracious luck with Lord Angelo for her poor brother's pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["That's he, indeed."],"id":907,"m":"That's him, indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You were not bid to speak."],"id":908,"m":"You were not asked to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["No, my good lord,","Nor wished to hold my peace."],"id":909,"m":"No, my good lord, nor asked to keep quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I wish you now, then;","Pray you take note of it; and when you have","A business for yourself, pray heaven you then","Be perfect."],"id":910,"m":"Then I ask you now. Please take note of it; and when you have business of your own to answer for, pray heaven you are word perfect then."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I warrant your honour."],"id":911,"m":"I guarantee it, your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The warrant's for yourself. Take heed to it."],"id":912,"m":"The guarantee is on your own head. Be careful of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["This gentleman told somewhat of my tale."],"id":913,"m":"This gentleman has told part of my story."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Right."],"id":914,"m":"Right."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It may be right, but you are i' the wrong","To speak before your time.—Proceed."],"id":915,"m":"It may be right, but you are wrong to speak out of turn. Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I went","To this pernicious caitiff deputy."],"id":916,"m":"I went to this wicked, villainous deputy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["That's somewhat madly spoken."],"id":917,"m":"That sounds rather mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["Pardon it;","The phrase is to the matter."],"id":918,"m":"Forgive it; the words fit the facts."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Mended again. The matter; proceed."],"id":919,"m":"Corrected again. The facts; go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["In brief, to set the needless process by:","How I persuaded, how I prayed and kneeled,","How he refelled me, and how I replied—","For this was of much length—the vile conclusion","I now begin with grief and shame to utter.","He would not, but by gift of my chaste body","To his concupiscible intemperate lust,","Release my brother; and after much debatement,","My sisterly remorse confutes mine honour,","And I did yield to him. But the next morn betimes,","His purpose surfeiting, he sends a warrant","For my poor brother's head."],"id":920,"m":"In short, leaving out the needless detail of how I persuaded him, how I prayed and knelt, how he refused me and how I answered, for this went on a long time, I now begin, with grief and shame, to tell the vile conclusion. He would not release my brother unless I gave my chaste body to his greedy, unrestrained lust. After much struggle my pity for my brother overcame my honour, and I gave in to him. But early the next morning, his desire satisfied, he sent a warrant for my poor brother's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This is most likely!"],"id":921,"m":"This is very likely!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, that it were as like as it is true!"],"id":922,"m":"Oh, I wish it were as likely as it is true!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["By heaven, fond wretch, thou know'st not what thou speak'st,","Or else thou art suborned against his honour","In hateful practice. First, his integrity","Stands without blemish; next, it imports no reason","That with such vehemency he should pursue","Faults proper to himself. If he had so offended,","He would have weighed thy brother by himself,","And not have cut him off. Someone hath set you on.","Confess the truth, and say by whose advice","Thou cam'st here to complain."],"id":923,"m":"By heaven, foolish wretch, you don't know what you're saying, or else you have been bribed to attack his honour in some hateful plot. First, his integrity is spotless. Second, it makes no sense that he would pursue so fiercely faults that were his own. If he had sinned like that, he would have judged your brother by himself and not had him executed. Someone has put you up to this. Confess the truth, and say on whose advice you came here to complain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["And is this all?","Then, O you blessed ministers above,","Keep me in patience, and with ripened time","Unfold the evil which is here wrapt up","In countenance! Heaven shield your Grace from woe,","As I, thus wronged, hence unbelieved go."],"id":924,"m":"And is this all? Then, oh you blessed angels above, keep me patient, and when the time is ripe reveal the evil that is wrapped up here in favour! Heaven protect your Grace from sorrow, as I, wronged like this, go away unbelieved."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I know you'd fain be gone. An officer!","To prison with her! Shall we thus permit","A blasting and a scandalous breath to fall","On him so near us? This needs must be a practice.","Who knew of your intent and coming hither?"],"id":925,"m":"I know you'd like to leave. Officer! Take her to prison! Are we to let a poisonous, scandalous breath fall on a man so close to us? This must be a plot. Who knew you intended to come here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["One that I would were here, Friar Lodowick."],"id":926,"m":"Someone I wish were here: Friar Lodowick."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Officer with Isabella.","id":927},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["A ghostly father, belike. Who knows that Lodowick?"],"id":928,"m":"A priest, I suppose. Who knows this Lodowick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["My lord, I know him. 'Tis a meddling friar.","I do not like the man. Had he been lay, my lord,","For certain words he spake against your Grace","In your retirement, I had swinged him soundly."],"id":929,"m":"My lord, I know him. He's a meddling friar. I don't like the man. If he hadn't been a churchman, my lord, I would have given him a sound beating for certain things he said against your Grace while you were away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Words against me? This's a good friar, belike.","And to set on this wretched woman here","Against our substitute! Let this friar be found."],"id":930,"m":"Words against me? He sounds like a fine friar. And setting this wretched woman on to attack our deputy! Find this friar."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["But yesternight, my lord, she and that friar,","I saw them at the prison. A saucy friar,","A very scurvy fellow."],"id":931,"m":"Only last night, my lord, I saw her and that friar at the prison. An insolent friar, a really nasty fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["Blessed be your royal Grace!","I have stood by, my lord, and I have heard","Your royal ear abused. First hath this woman","Most wrongfully accused your substitute,","Who is as free from touch or soil with her","As she from one ungot."],"id":932,"m":"Blessings on your royal Grace! I have been standing by, my lord, and I have heard your royal ear abused. First, this woman has falsely accused your deputy, who is as innocent of touching or staining her as she is of someone not yet conceived."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["We did believe no less.","Know you that Friar Lodowick that she speaks of?"],"id":933,"m":"We believed as much. Do you know this Friar Lodowick she talks about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["I know him for a man divine and holy,","Not scurvy, nor a temporary meddler,","As he's reported by this gentleman;","And, on my trust, a man that never yet","Did, as he vouches, misreport your Grace."],"id":934,"m":"I know him as a godly and holy man, not nasty, nor a meddler in worldly affairs, as this gentleman says; and, on my word, a man who has never slandered your Grace, as this man claims."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["My lord, most villainously; believe it."],"id":935,"m":"My lord, he slandered you most villainously, believe me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["Well, he in time may come to clear himself;","But at this instant he is sick, my lord,","Of a strange fever. Upon his mere request,","Being come to knowledge that there was complaint","Intended 'gainst Lord Angelo, came I hither","To speak, as from his mouth, what he doth know","Is true and false; and what he with his oath","And all probation will make up full clear","Whensoever he's convented. First, for this woman,","To justify this worthy nobleman,","So vulgarly and personally accused,","Her shall you hear disproved to her eyes,","Till she herself confess it."],"id":936,"m":"Well, he may come in time to clear himself. But just now he is ill, my lord, with a strange fever. At his request alone, having learned that a complaint against Lord Angelo was planned, I came here to say, as if from his own mouth, what he knows to be true and false, and what he will prove with his oath and every kind of evidence whenever he is summoned. First, as for this woman: to clear this worthy nobleman, so publicly and personally accused, you will hear her proved wrong to her face, until she confesses it herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Good friar, let's hear it.","Do you not smile at this, Lord Angelo?","O heaven, the vanity of wretched fools!","Give us some seats.—Come, cousin Angelo,","In this I'll be impartial. Be you judge","Of your own cause."],"id":937,"m":"Good friar, let's hear it. Don't you smile at this, Lord Angelo? Oh heaven, the stupidity of wretched fools! Bring us some seats. Come, cousin Angelo, I will take no side in this. You be judge in your own case."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mariana, veiled.","id":938},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["Is this the witness, friar?","First let her show her face, and after speak."],"id":939,"m":"Is this the witness, friar? First let her show her face, and then speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Pardon, my lord; I will not show my face","Until my husband bid me."],"id":940,"m":"Forgive me, my lord, I will not show my face until my husband tells me to."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What, are you married?"],"id":941,"m":"What, are you married?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["No, my lord."],"id":942,"m":"No, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Are you a maid?"],"id":943,"m":"Are you a virgin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["No, my lord."],"id":944,"m":"No, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["A widow, then?"],"id":945,"m":"A widow, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Neither, my lord."],"id":946,"m":"Neither, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Why, you are nothing then, neither maid, widow, nor wife?"],"id":947,"m":"Why, then you are nothing: neither virgin, widow nor wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["My lord, she may be a punk; for many of them are neither maid, widow,","nor wife."],"id":948,"m":"My lord, she may be a whore, because a lot of them are neither virgin, widow nor wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Silence that fellow. I would he had some cause to prattle for himself."],"id":949,"m":"Silence that fellow. I wish he had some reason to babble on his own behalf."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Well, my lord."],"id":950,"m":"Very well, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["My lord, I do confess I ne'er was married,","And I confess besides, I am no maid.","I have known my husband; yet my husband","Knows not that ever he knew me."],"id":951,"m":"My lord, I admit I have never been married, and I admit too that I am not a virgin. I have slept with my husband, but my husband does not know that he ever slept with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["He was drunk, then, my lord; it can be no better."],"id":952,"m":"Then he was drunk, my lord; there's no other explanation."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["For the benefit of silence, would thou wert so too."],"id":953,"m":"For the sake of some quiet, I wish you were drunk too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Well, my lord."],"id":954,"m":"Very well, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This is no witness for Lord Angelo."],"id":955,"m":"This is no witness for Lord Angelo."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Now I come to't, my lord.","She that accuses him of fornication","In self-same manner doth accuse my husband,","And charges him, my lord, with such a time","When I'll depose I had him in mine arms","With all th' effect of love."],"id":956,"m":"I am coming to that, my lord. The woman who accuses him of fornication is accusing my husband in exactly the same way, and she names a time, my lord, when I will swear I had him in my arms, with all the acts of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Charges she more than me?"],"id":957,"m":"Is she accusing anyone else besides me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Not that I know."],"id":958,"m":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["No? You say your husband."],"id":959,"m":"No? You said your husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Why, just, my lord, and that is Angelo,","Who thinks he knows that he ne'er knew my body,","But knows, he thinks, that he knows Isabel's."],"id":960,"m":"Yes, exactly, my lord, and that is Angelo, who thinks he knows he never slept with me, but thinks he knows he slept with Isabel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["This is a strange abuse. Let's see thy face."],"id":961,"m":"This is a strange slander. Let's see your face."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["My husband bids me; now I will unmask. [Unveiling]","This is that face, thou cruel Angelo,","Which once thou swor'st was worth the looking on.","This is the hand which, with a vowed contract,","Was fast belocked in thine. This is the body","That took away the match from Isabel","And did supply thee at thy garden-house","In her imagined person."],"id":962,"m":"My husband tells me to; now I will unmask. (She takes off her veil) This is the face, cruel Angelo, that you once swore was worth looking at. This is the hand that was locked tight in yours by a sworn contract. This is the body that took Isabel's place in the meeting and satisfied you in your garden house, while you imagined it was her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Know you this woman?"],"id":963,"m":"Do you know this woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Carnally, she says."],"id":964,"m":"Carnally, she says."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sirrah, no more."],"id":965,"m":"Fellow, no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Enough, my lord."],"id":966,"m":"That's enough, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["My lord, I must confess I know this woman;","And five years since there was some speech of marriage","Betwixt myself and her; which was broke off,","Partly for that her promised proportions","Came short of composition; but in chief","For that her reputation was disvalued","In levity. Since which time of five years","I never spake with her, saw her, nor heard from her,","Upon my faith and honour."],"id":967,"m":"My lord, I must admit I know this woman. Five years ago there was some talk of marriage between her and me. It was broken off, partly because her promised dowry fell short of what had been agreed, but mainly because her reputation was damaged by reports of loose behaviour. Since then, in five years, I have never spoken to her, seen her or heard from her, on my faith and honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Noble Prince,","As there comes light from heaven and words from breath,","As there is sense in truth and truth in virtue,","I am affianced this man's wife as strongly","As words could make up vows. And, my good lord,","But Tuesday night last gone, in's garden-house,","He knew me as a wife. As this is true,","Let me in safety raise me from my knees,","Or else for ever be confixed here,","A marble monument!"],"id":968,"m":"Noble prince, as surely as light comes from heaven and words from breath, as surely as there is meaning in truth and truth in virtue, I am this man's promised wife, as firmly as words could make vows. And, my good lord, only last Tuesday night, in his garden house, he slept with me as his wife. If this is true, let me rise safely from my knees; if not, let me be fixed here for ever, a marble statue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I did but smile till now.","Now, good my lord, give me the scope of justice.","My patience here is touched. I do perceive","These poor informal women are no more","But instruments of some more mightier member","That sets them on. Let me have way, my lord,","To find this practice out."],"id":969,"m":"Up to now I have only smiled. Now, my good lord, give me full power to do justice. My patience is wearing thin. I can see that these poor, foolish women are only the tools of some more powerful person who has put them up to this. Give me leave, my lord, to uncover this plot."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Ay, with my heart;","And punish them to your height of pleasure.","Thou foolish friar, and thou pernicious woman,","Compact with her that's gone, think'st thou thy oaths,","Though they would swear down each particular saint,","Were testimonies against his worth and credit,","That's sealed in approbation? You, Lord Escalus,","Sit with my cousin; lend him your kind pains","To find out this abuse, whence 'tis derived.","There is another friar that set them on;","Let him be sent for."],"id":970,"m":"Yes, with all my heart; and punish them as much as you like. You foolish friar, and you wicked woman, in league with the one who has gone: do you think your oaths, even if they swore by every single saint, could count as evidence against his worth and good name, which have been sealed by long approval? You, Lord Escalus, sit with my cousin and lend him your kind help to find out where this slander comes from. There is another friar who put them up to it; send for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR PETER","lines":["Would he were here, my lord; for he indeed","Hath set the women on to this complaint.","Your Provost knows the place where he abides,","And he may fetch him."],"id":971,"m":"I wish he were here, my lord, because he really did set the women on to make this complaint. Your Provost knows where he lives and can fetch him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Go, do it instantly."],"id":972,"m":"Go, do it immediately."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Provost.","id":973},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["And you, my noble and well-warranted cousin,","Whom it concerns to hear this matter forth,","Do with your injuries as seems you best","In any chastisement. I for a while","Will leave you; but stir not you till you have","Well determined upon these slanderers."],"id":974,"m":"And you, my noble cousin, whose good name is well proven and who has every right to hear this matter through, punish these injuries to you however you think best. I will leave you for a while; but don't move until you have dealt thoroughly with these slanderers."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["My lord, we'll do it throughly."],"id":975,"m":"My lord, we'll do it thoroughly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Duke.","id":976},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","cont":1,"lines":["Signior Lucio, did not you say you knew that Friar Lodowick to be a","dishonest person?"],"id":977,"m":"Signior Lucio, didn't you say you knew this Friar Lodowick to be a dishonest man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Cucullus non facit monachum, honest in nothing but in his clothes,","and one that hath spoke most villainous speeches of the Duke."],"id":978,"m":"The hood doesn't make the monk. He's honest in nothing but his clothes, and he has said the most villainous things about the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["We shall entreat you to abide here till he come, and enforce them","against him. We shall find this friar a notable fellow."],"id":979,"m":"We will ask you to stay here until he comes, and repeat those charges against him. We'll find this friar is a notorious fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["As any in Vienna, on my word."],"id":980,"m":"As notorious as any in Vienna, on my word."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Call that same Isabel here once again. I would speak with her."],"id":981,"m":"Call that Isabel here again. I want to speak to her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant.","id":982},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","cont":1,"lines":["Pray you, my lord, give me leave to question; you shall see how I'll","handle her."],"id":983,"m":"Please, my lord, let me question her. You'll see how I handle her."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Not better than he, by her own report."],"id":984,"m":"Not better than he did, by her own account."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Say you?"],"id":985,"m":"What did you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Marry, sir, I think, if you handled her privately, she would sooner","confess; perchance, publicly, she'll be ashamed."],"id":986,"m":"Why, sir, I think if you handled her in private, she'd confess sooner. In public, perhaps, she'll be ashamed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter at several doors Duke as a friar, Provost and Isabella with Officers.","id":987},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I will go darkly to work with her."],"id":988,"m":"I will work on her subtly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["That's the way; for women are light at midnight."],"id":989,"m":"That's the way, because women are light (and loose) at midnight."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["[To Isabella] Come on, mistress, here's a gentlewoman denies all","that you have said."],"id":990,"m":"(To Isabella) Come on, mistress, here's a lady who denies everything you said."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["My lord, here comes the rascal I spoke of, here with the Provost."],"id":991,"m":"My lord, here comes the rascal I told you about, with the Provost."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["In very good time. Speak not you to him till we call upon you."],"id":992,"m":"At just the right time. Don't speak to him until we call on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Mum."],"id":993,"m":"Not a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Come, sir, did you set these women on to slander Lord Angelo? They have","confessed you did."],"id":994,"m":"Come, sir, did you put these women up to slandering Lord Angelo? They have confessed that you did."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["'Tis false."],"id":995,"m":"That's false."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["How! Know you where you are?"],"id":996,"m":"What! Do you know where you are?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Respect to your great place; and let the devil","Be sometime honoured for his burning throne.","Where is the Duke? 'Tis he should hear me speak."],"id":997,"m":"I respect your high position; and the devil too is sometimes honoured for his burning throne. Where is the Duke? He is the one who should hear me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["The Duke's in us; and we will hear you speak.","Look you speak justly."],"id":998,"m":"The Duke's power is in us, and we will hear you. Make sure you speak fairly."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Boldly, at least. But, O, poor souls,","Come you to seek the lamb here of the fox,","Good night to your redress! Is the Duke gone?","Then is your cause gone too. The Duke's unjust","Thus to retort your manifest appeal,","And put your trial in the villain's mouth","Which here you come to accuse."],"id":999,"m":"Boldly, at least. But oh, poor souls, have you come to ask the fox for the lamb? Then say goodbye to any remedy! Has the Duke gone? Then your case has gone too. The Duke is unjust to throw back your open appeal like this and leave your trial in the mouth of the villain you came here to accuse."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["This is the rascal; this is he I spoke of."],"id":1000,"m":"This is the rascal; this is the one I told you about."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Why, thou unreverend and unhallowed friar,","Is't not enough thou hast suborned these women","To accuse this worthy man, but, in foul mouth,","And in the witness of his proper ear,","To call him villain? And then to glance from him","To th' Duke himself, to tax him with injustice?","Take him hence! To th' rack with him! We'll touse you","Joint by joint, but we will know his purpose.","What! Unjust?"],"id":1001,"m":"Why, you irreverent, unholy friar! Isn't it enough that you bribed these women to accuse this worthy man, but you must call him a villain with your foul mouth, in his own hearing? And then turn from him to the Duke himself and accuse him of injustice? Take him away! To the rack with him! We'll pull you apart joint by joint, but we'll find out what he's up to. What! Unjust?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Be not so hot. The Duke","Dare no more stretch this finger of mine than he","Dare rack his own. His subject am I not,","Nor here provincial. My business in this state","Made me a looker-on here in Vienna,","Where I have seen corruption boil and bubble","Till it o'errun the stew. Laws for all faults,","But faults so countenanced that the strong statutes","Stand like the forfeits in a barber's shop,","As much in mock as mark."],"id":1002,"m":"Don't be so hot. The Duke would no more dare to stretch this finger of mine than he would dare to rack his own. I am not his subject, nor under the authority of this province. My business in this state made me an observer here in Vienna, where I have seen corruption boil and bubble until it overflows the pot. There are laws for every fault, but faults are so tolerated that the strong statutes are like the list of penalties hanging in a barber's shop, as much laughed at as noticed."},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Slander to the state! Away with him to prison!"],"id":1003,"m":"Slander against the state! Take him away to prison!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["What can you vouch against him, Signior Lucio?","Is this the man that you did tell us of?"],"id":1004,"m":"What can you say against him, Signior Lucio? Is this the man you told us about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["'Tis he, my lord. Come hither, goodman Baldpate.","Do you know me?"],"id":1005,"m":"That's him, my lord. Come here, Mister Baldhead. Do you know me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I remember you, sir, by the sound of your voice. I met you at the","prison, in the absence of the Duke."],"id":1006,"m":"I remember you, sir, by the sound of your voice. I met you at the prison while the Duke was away."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["O did you so? And do you remember what you said of the Duke?"],"id":1007,"m":"Oh, did you? And do you remember what you said about the Duke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Most notedly, sir."],"id":1008,"m":"Very clearly, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Do you so, sir? And was the Duke a fleshmonger, a fool, and a coward,","as you then reported him to be?"],"id":1009,"m":"Do you, sir? And was the Duke a lecher, a fool and a coward, as you said then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You must, sir, change persons with me ere you make that my report. You","indeed spoke so of him, and much more, much worse."],"id":1010,"m":"You must change places with me, sir, before you can call that my report. It was you who said that about him, and much more, much worse."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["O thou damnable fellow! Did not I pluck thee by the nose for thy","speeches?"],"id":1011,"m":"Oh, you damnable fellow! Didn't I pull your nose for what you said?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I protest I love the Duke as I love myself."],"id":1012,"m":"I swear I love the Duke as I love myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Hark how the villain would close now, after his treasonable abuses!"],"id":1013,"m":"Listen to how the villain tries to make peace now, after his treasonous insults!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["Such a fellow is not to be talked withal. Away with him to prison!","Where is the provost? Away with him to prison! Lay bolts enough upon","him. Let him speak no more. Away with those giglets too, and with the","other confederate companion!"],"id":1014,"m":"There's no talking with a fellow like this. Take him away to prison! Where is the Provost? Take him away to prison! Put plenty of chains on him. Don't let him speak any more. Take those flirts away too, and the other accomplice!"},{"t":"sd","text":"The Provost lays hands on the Duke.","id":1015},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Stay, sir, stay a while."],"id":1016,"m":"Wait, sir, wait a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["What, resists he? Help him, Lucio."],"id":1017,"m":"What, is he resisting? Help him, Lucio."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Come, sir, come, sir, come, sir. Foh, sir! Why, you bald-pated lying","rascal! You must be hooded, must you? Show your knave's visage, with a","pox to you! Show your sheep-biting face, and be hanged an hour! Will't","not off?"],"id":1018,"m":"Come on, sir, come on, sir, come on, sir. Ugh, sir! Why, you bald-headed lying rascal! You have to wear a hood, do you? Show your villain's face, damn you! Show your sheep-killing face, and be hanged in an hour! Won't it come off?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Pulls off the friar's hood and discovers the Duke.","id":1019},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Thou art the first knave that e'er mad'st a duke.","First, Provost, let me bail these gentle three.","[To Lucio] Sneak not away, sir, for the friar and you","Must have a word anon.—Lay hold on him."],"id":1020,"m":"You are the first villain who ever made a duke. First, Provost, let me release these three good people. (To Lucio) Don't sneak away, sir; the friar and you must have a word shortly. Hold him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["This may prove worse than hanging."],"id":1021,"m":"This may turn out worse than hanging."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["[To Escalus] What you have spoke I pardon. Sit you down.","We'll borrow place of him. [To Angelo] Sir, by your leave.","Hast thou or word, or wit, or impudence,","That yet can do thee office? If thou hast,","Rely upon it till my tale be heard,","And hold no longer out."],"id":1022,"m":"(To Escalus) I pardon what you said. Sit down. We'll borrow his seat. (To Angelo) Sir, if you'll allow me. Have you any word, wit or nerve left that can still serve you? If you have, rely on it until my story has been heard, and then hold out no longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["O my dread lord,","I should be guiltier than my guiltiness","To think I can be undiscernible,","When I perceive your Grace, like power divine,","Hath looked upon my passes. Then, good Prince,","No longer session hold upon my shame,","But let my trial be mine own confession.","Immediate sentence then, and sequent death","Is all the grace I beg."],"id":1023,"m":"Oh, my dread lord, I would be guiltier than my guilt if I thought I could stay hidden, now that I see your Grace, like God, has watched all my actions. So, good prince, do not keep my shame on trial any longer, but let my own confession be my trial. An immediate sentence and death to follow are all the mercy I ask for."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Come hither, Mariana.","Say, wast thou e'er contracted to this woman?"],"id":1024,"m":"Come here, Mariana. Tell me, were you ever contracted to this woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I was, my lord."],"id":1025,"m":"I was, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Go, take her hence and marry her instantly.","Do you the office, friar; which consummate,","Return him here again.—Go with him, Provost."],"id":1026,"m":"Go, take her away and marry her at once. Friar, you perform the ceremony; and when it is done, bring him back here. Go with him, Provost."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Angelo, Mariana, Friar Peter and Provost.","id":1027},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["My lord, I am more amazed at his dishonour","Than at the strangeness of it."],"id":1028,"m":"My lord, I am more amazed at his dishonour than at the strangeness of all this."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Come hither, Isabel.","Your friar is now your prince. As I was then","Advertising and holy to your business,","Not changing heart with habit, I am still","Attorneyed at your service."],"id":1029,"m":"Come here, Isabel. Your friar is now your prince. Just as I was then devoted to your business, both as adviser and as holy man, I have not changed my heart along with my clothes, and I am still at your service as your advocate."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["O, give me pardon,","That I, your vassal, have employed and pained","Your unknown sovereignty."],"id":1030,"m":"Oh, forgive me that I, your subject, have used and troubled you without knowing you were my sovereign."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You are pardoned, Isabel.","And now, dear maid, be you as free to us.","Your brother's death, I know, sits at your heart,","And you may marvel why I obscured myself,","Labouring to save his life, and would not rather","Make rash remonstrance of my hidden power","Than let him so be lost. O most kind maid,","It was the swift celerity of his death,","Which I did think with slower foot came on,","That brained my purpose. But peace be with him.","That life is better life, past fearing death,","Than that which lives to fear. Make it your comfort,","So happy is your brother."],"id":1031,"m":"You are forgiven, Isabel. And now, dear maiden, forgive me as freely. I know your brother's death weighs on your heart, and you may wonder why I kept myself hidden while working to save his life, rather than suddenly revealing my secret power and not letting him be lost like that. Oh, kindest of maidens, it was the sudden speed of his death, which I thought was coming more slowly, that dashed out my plan. But peace be with him. A life beyond the fear of death is a better life than one that lives in fear. Take comfort from that: your brother is happy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["I do, my lord."],"id":1032,"m":"I do, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo, Mariana, Friar Peter and Provost.","id":1033},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["For this new-married man approaching here,","Whose salt imagination yet hath wronged","Your well-defended honour, you must pardon","For Mariana's sake. But as he adjudged your brother,","Being criminal in double violation","Of sacred chastity and of promise-breach","Thereon dependent, for your brother's life,","The very mercy of the law cries out","Most audible, even from his proper tongue,","\"An Angelo for Claudio, death for death.\"","Haste still pays haste, and leisure answers leisure;","Like doth quit like, and measure still for measure.","Then, Angelo, thy fault's thus manifested,","Which, though thou wouldst deny, denies thee vantage.","We do condemn thee to the very block","Where Claudio stooped to death, and with like haste.","Away with him."],"id":1034,"m":"As for this newly married man coming here, whose lustful imagination wronged your well-defended honour, you must forgive him for Mariana's sake. But because he condemned your brother, while he himself was guilty twice over, of violating sacred chastity and of breaking the promise that depended on it, to spare your brother's life, the very mercy of the law cries out loud and clear, even in his own words: \"An Angelo for Claudio, death for death.\" Haste always pays for haste, and delay answers delay; like repays like, and always measure for measure. So, Angelo, your crime is plain, and even if you denied it, the denial would do you no good. We condemn you to the very block where Claudio bowed to death, and with the same haste. Take him away."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["O my most gracious lord,","I hope you will not mock me with a husband."],"id":1035,"m":"Oh, my most gracious lord, I hope you are not mocking me by giving me a husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It is your husband mocked you with a husband.","Consenting to the safeguard of your honour,","I thought your marriage fit. Else imputation,","For that he knew you, might reproach your life,","And choke your good to come. For his possessions,","Although by confiscation they are ours,","We do instate and widow you with all","To buy you a better husband."],"id":1036,"m":"It was your husband who mocked you with a husband. To protect your honour, I thought this marriage right. Otherwise, because he slept with you, gossip might shame you for life and choke the good fortune still to come for you. As for his property, though it is ours by confiscation, we give it all to you as his widow, to buy you a better husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["O my dear lord,","I crave no other, nor no better man."],"id":1037,"m":"Oh, my dear lord, I want no other and no better man."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Never crave him; we are definitive."],"id":1038,"m":"Don't ask for him; our decision is final."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["[Kneeling] Gentle my liege—"],"id":1039,"m":"(Kneeling) My gracious lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You do but lose your labour.","Away with him to death. [To Lucio] Now, sir, to you."],"id":1040,"m":"You are only wasting your effort. Take him away to his death. (To Lucio) Now, sir, it's your turn."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["O my good lord.—Sweet Isabel, take my part;","Lend me your knees, and all my life to come","I'll lend you all my life to do you service."],"id":1041,"m":"Oh, my good lord! Sweet Isabel, take my side. Lend me your knees, and all my life to come I'll give my whole life to serving you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Against all sense you do importune her.","Should she kneel down in mercy of this fact,","Her brother's ghost his paved bed would break,","And take her hence in horror."],"id":1042,"m":"You are begging her against all reason. If she knelt to ask mercy for this deed, her brother's ghost would break out of his stone grave and carry her off in horror."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Isabel,","Sweet Isabel, do yet but kneel by me;","Hold up your hands, say nothing. I'll speak all.","They say best men are moulded out of faults,","And, for the most, become much more the better","For being a little bad. So may my husband.","O Isabel, will you not lend a knee?"],"id":1043,"m":"Isabel, sweet Isabel, only kneel beside me. Hold up your hands and say nothing; I'll do all the speaking. They say the best men are made out of faults, and most of them become much better for being a little bad. So may my husband. Oh, Isabel, won't you lend me a knee?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["He dies for Claudio's death."],"id":1044,"m":"He dies for Claudio's death."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISABELLA","lines":["[Kneeling] Most bounteous sir,","Look, if it please you, on this man condemned","As if my brother lived. I partly think","A due sincerity governed his deeds","Till he did look on me. Since it is so,","Let him not die. My brother had but justice,","In that he did the thing for which he died.","For Angelo,","His act did not o'ertake his bad intent,","And must be buried but as an intent","That perished by the way. Thoughts are no subjects;","Intents but merely thoughts."],"id":1045,"m":"(Kneeling) Most generous sir, if you please, look on this condemned man as if my brother were alive. I partly think a real sincerity ruled his actions until he looked at me. Since that is so, don't let him die. My brother received only justice, because he did the thing he died for. As for Angelo, his act did not carry out his evil intention, and must be buried as an intention that died on the way. Thoughts are not subject to the law, and intentions are only thoughts."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Merely, my lord."],"id":1046,"m":"Only thoughts, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Your suit's unprofitable. Stand up, I say.","I have bethought me of another fault.","Provost, how came it Claudio was beheaded","At an unusual hour?"],"id":1047,"m":"Your plea does no good. Stand up, I tell you. I have thought of another crime. Provost, how did it happen that Claudio was beheaded at an unusual hour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["It was commanded so."],"id":1048,"m":"That was the order."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Had you a special warrant for the deed?"],"id":1049,"m":"Did you have a special warrant for it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["No, my good lord, it was by private message."],"id":1050,"m":"No, my good lord, it came by private message."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["For which I do discharge you of your office.","Give up your keys."],"id":1051,"m":"For that I dismiss you from your post. Hand over your keys."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["Pardon me, noble lord.","I thought it was a fault, but knew it not;","Yet did repent me after more advice.","For testimony whereof, one in the prison","That should by private order else have died,","I have reserved alive."],"id":1052,"m":"Forgive me, noble lord. I thought it was wrong, but I wasn't sure; and on further thought I regretted it. As proof of that, I have kept alive one man in the prison who would otherwise have died by private order."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What's he?"],"id":1053,"m":"Who is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["His name is Barnardine."],"id":1054,"m":"His name is Barnardine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I would thou hadst done so by Claudio.","Go fetch him hither, let me look upon him."],"id":1055,"m":"I wish you had done the same for Claudio. Go and fetch him here; let me see him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Provost.","id":1056},{"t":"sp","who":"ESCALUS","lines":["I am sorry one so learned and so wise","As you, Lord Angelo, have still appeared,","Should slip so grossly, both in the heat of blood","And lack of tempered judgement afterward."],"id":1057,"m":"I am sorry, Lord Angelo, that a man as learned and wise as you have always seemed should slip so badly, both in the heat of passion and in your lack of balanced judgment afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I am sorry that such sorrow I procure,","And so deep sticks it in my penitent heart","That I crave death more willingly than mercy;","'Tis my deserving, and I do entreat it."],"id":1058,"m":"I am sorry to cause such sorrow, and it sticks so deep in my repentant heart that I ask for death more willingly than mercy. It is what I deserve, and I beg for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Provost with Barnardine, Claudio (muffled) and Juliet.","id":1059},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Which is that Barnardine?"],"id":1060,"m":"Which one is Barnardine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["This, my lord."],"id":1061,"m":"This one, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["There was a friar told me of this man.","Sirrah, thou art said to have a stubborn soul","That apprehends no further than this world,","And squar'st thy life according. Thou'rt condemned;","But, for those earthly faults, I quit them all,","And pray thee take this mercy to provide","For better times to come. Friar, advise him;","I leave him to your hand.—What muffled fellow's that?"],"id":1062,"m":"A friar told me about this man. Fellow, they say you have a stubborn soul that sees nothing beyond this world, and that you shape your life to match. You are condemned; but I pardon all those earthly crimes, and ask you to use this mercy to prepare for better times to come. Friar, advise him; I leave him in your hands. Who is that muffled fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROVOST","lines":["This is another prisoner that I saved,","Who should have died when Claudio lost his head;","As like almost to Claudio as himself."],"id":1063,"m":"This is another prisoner I saved, who should have died when Claudio lost his head. He is almost as like Claudio as Claudio himself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Unmuffles Claudio.","id":1064},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["[To Isabella] If he be like your brother, for his sake","Is he pardoned; and for your lovely sake,","Give me your hand and say you will be mine.","He is my brother too. But fitter time for that.","By this Lord Angelo perceives he's safe;","Methinks I see a quick'ning in his eye.","Well, Angelo, your evil quits you well.","Look that you love your wife, her worth worth yours.","I find an apt remission in myself.","And yet here's one in place I cannot pardon.","[To Lucio] You, sirrah, that knew me for a fool, a coward,","One all of luxury, an ass, a madman.","Wherein have I so deserved of you","That you extol me thus?"],"id":1065,"m":"(To Isabella) If he is like your brother, he is pardoned for your brother's sake; and for your lovely sake, give me your hand and say you will be mine, and he will be my brother too. But there will be a better time for that. By now Lord Angelo realises he is safe; I think I see life coming back into his eyes. Well, Angelo, your sin has paid you back well. Make sure you love your wife; her worth equals yours. I find myself ready to forgive. And yet there is one here I cannot pardon. (To Lucio) You, fellow, who knew me for a fool, a coward, a man full of lust, an ass, a madman: what have I done to you that you should praise me like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Faith, my lord, I spoke it but according to the trick. If you will hang","me for it, you may, but I had rather it would please you I might be","whipped."],"id":1066,"m":"Honestly, my lord, I only said it as a joke, as is the fashion. If you want to hang me for it, you can, but I'd rather you were pleased to have me whipped."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Whipped first, sir, and hanged after.","Proclaim it, Provost, round about the city,","If any woman wronged by this lewd fellow,","As I have heard him swear himself there's one","Whom he begot with child—let her appear,","And he shall marry her. The nuptial finished,","Let him be whipped and hanged."],"id":1067,"m":"Whipped first, sir, and hanged after. Provost, have it announced throughout the city that if any woman has been wronged by this lewd fellow (I have heard him swear himself that there is one he got pregnant), she should come forward, and he shall marry her. When the wedding is over, let him be whipped and hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["I beseech your Highness, do not marry me to a whore. Your highness said","even now I made you a duke; good my lord, do not recompense me in","making me a cuckold."],"id":1068,"m":"I beg your Highness, don't marry me to a whore. Your Highness said just now that I made you a duke. My good lord, don't pay me back by making me a cuckold."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Upon mine honour, thou shalt marry her.","Thy slanders I forgive, and therewithal","Remit thy other forfeits.—Take him to prison,","And see our pleasure herein executed."],"id":1069,"m":"On my honour, you shall marry her. I forgive your slanders, and with them I let off your other penalties. Take him to prison and see that our wishes are carried out."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIO","lines":["Marrying a punk, my lord, is pressing to death, whipping, and hanging."],"id":1070,"m":"Marrying a whore, my lord, is like being pressed to death, whipped and hanged all at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Slandering a prince deserves it."],"id":1071,"m":"Slandering a prince deserves it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Officers with Lucio.","id":1072},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","cont":1,"lines":["She, Claudio, that you wronged, look you restore.","Joy to you, Mariana! Love her, Angelo.","I have confessed her, and I know her virtue.","Thanks, good friend Escalus, for thy much goodness;","There's more behind that is more gratulate.","Thanks, Provost, for thy care and secrecy;","We shall employ thee in a worthier place.","Forgive him, Angelo, that brought you home","The head of Ragozine for Claudio's.","Th' offence pardons itself. Dear Isabel,","I have a motion much imports your good;","Whereto if you'll a willing ear incline,","What's mine is yours, and what is yours is mine.","So, bring us to our palace, where we'll show","What's yet behind that's meet you all should know."],"id":1073,"m":"Claudio, make sure you make amends to the woman you wronged. Joy to you, Mariana! Love her, Angelo. I have heard her confession, and I know how good she is. Thank you, my good friend Escalus, for all your goodness; there is more reward to come, more worth celebrating. Thank you, Provost, for your care and your secrecy; we will give you a worthier post. Forgive him, Angelo, for bringing you the head of Ragozine instead of Claudio's. The offence pardons itself. Dear Isabel, I have a proposal that greatly concerns your good. If you will lend it a willing ear, what is mine is yours, and what is yours is mine. So, take us to our palace, where we will reveal what still remains that you should all know."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1074}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"The Duke","group":"The state","desc":"Vincentio, the ruler of Vienna. He has let the city's strict laws sleep for fourteen years and, rather than enforce them himself, he hands his power to Angelo and pretends to leave for Poland. Disguised as a friar (he is called Friar Lodowick in Act 5), he watches his deputy, hears confessions, overhears Claudio and Isabella, and arranges the bed trick and the head trick. In the last scene he unmasks, judges everyone, and twice asks Isabella to marry him. She never answers in words."},{"name":"Angelo","group":"The state","desc":"The Duke's deputy, famous for strictness and self-denial: Lucio says his blood is 'very snow-broth'. He revives an old law and sentences Claudio to death for getting Juliet pregnant. When Isabella pleads for her brother he is overcome by desire and offers to spare Claudio if she will sleep with him. Believing she has, he still orders Claudio's execution. Five years earlier he broke off his engagement to Mariana when her dowry was lost at sea. Exposed in Act 5, he asks only for death."},{"name":"Escalus","group":"The state","desc":"An old, experienced lord, named second to Angelo. He argues for mercy, asking Angelo whether he has never felt the same temptation as Claudio, and handles the comic case of Elbow, Froth and Pompey with patience and warnings rather than punishment. In Act 5 he is completely fooled, and threatens the disguised Duke with the rack."},{"name":"The Provost","group":"The state","desc":"The officer in charge of the prison. He pities Claudio, questions Angelo's sentence, and agrees to the Duke's head trick once he is shown the Duke's seal. He keeps Barnardine and Claudio alive in secret, and the Duke promises him a better post."},{"name":"Isabella","group":"Claudio's family","desc":"Claudio's sister, a novice about to take her vows with the strict nuns of Saint Clare. She goes to Angelo at Lucio's urging and argues brilliantly for mercy. When Angelo demands her body as the price of her brother's life, she refuses and threatens to expose him, but realises no one will believe her. She tells Claudio to prepare to die, and turns on him furiously when he begs her to agree. She takes part in the Duke's plans, accuses Angelo publicly, and finally kneels to beg for Angelo's life, believing her brother dead."},{"name":"Claudio","group":"Claudio's family","desc":"A young gentleman of Vienna who has slept with Juliet, his wife in all but the public ceremony. Arrested under Angelo's revived law and sentenced to death, he sends Lucio to ask Isabella to plead for him. He first faces death bravely, then breaks down in terror and begs her to give in to Angelo. The Provost saves him, and he is unmuffled alive in the last scene. He hardly speaks after the prison scene."},{"name":"Juliet","group":"Claudio's family","desc":"Claudio's partner, pregnant with his child. In prison she tells the disguised Duke that she loves Claudio and repents her sin. She appears silently in the last scene."},{"name":"Mariana","group":"Claudio's family","desc":"A lady who was engaged to Angelo until her brother Frederick drowned with her dowry; Angelo then abandoned her, claiming her reputation was damaged. She has lived in grief at a moated grange ever since. She agrees to take Isabella's place in Angelo's bed, reveals the trick in Act 5, marries Angelo on the Duke's orders, and begs for his life."},{"name":"Lucio","group":"Vienna's streets","desc":"A witty, foul-mouthed gentleman about town. He is a loyal friend to Claudio and coaches Isabella in her first meeting with Angelo, but he lies freely. He slanders the absent Duke to the disguised Duke's face, calling him a drunk and a lecher, then denies it and blames the 'friar'. He pulls off the friar's hood in Act 5 and so reveals the Duke. His punishment is to marry Kate Keepdown, the mother of his child."},{"name":"Pompey","group":"Vienna's streets","desc":"A barman who is also a pimp for Mistress Overdone. He runs rings round the law in front of Escalus, is arrested again, and in prison is made assistant to the executioner Abhorson. Funny, shameless and practical, he speaks for the ordinary life that Angelo's law cannot stamp out."},{"name":"Mistress Overdone","group":"Vienna's streets","desc":"A brothel keeper, called the Bawd in speech headings, who has had nine husbands. Her houses in the suburbs are to be pulled down. She is arrested and sent to prison, and she informs on Lucio, who fathered a child on Kate Keepdown."},{"name":"Elbow","group":"Vienna's streets","desc":"A foolish constable who uses the wrong words, accusing Froth and Pompey of being 'benefactors'. He has served as constable for seven and a half years because no one else wants the job."},{"name":"Froth","group":"Vienna's streets","desc":"A dim young gentleman with eighty pounds a year, arrested at Mistress Overdone's house. Escalus lets him go with a warning to stay out of taverns."},{"name":"Abhorson","group":"The prison","desc":"The executioner, proud of his 'mystery' or craft, who takes Pompey on as his assistant."},{"name":"Barnardine","group":"The prison","desc":"A Bohemian prisoner held for nine years for a proven murder. Drunk and careless of death, he simply refuses to be executed. The Duke cannot send him to his death unprepared, and pardons him in Act 5."},{"name":"Friar Thomas","group":"The Church","desc":"The friar who gives the Duke his habit and hears his reasons for handing power to Angelo."},{"name":"Friar Peter","group":"The Church","desc":"The friar who helps the Duke in Act 5, bringing Isabella and Mariana to speak, and who marries Angelo to Mariana."},{"name":"Francisca","group":"The Church","desc":"A nun of Saint Clare who explains the order's rules about speaking with men."},{"name":"Varrius","group":"The state","desc":"A gentleman and friend of the Duke who meets him outside the town. He does not speak."},{"name":"Kate Keepdown","group":"Vienna's streets","desc":"Never seen. A woman Lucio got pregnant and promised to marry; Mistress Overdone has looked after the child."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"The Duke tells old Escalus that he is leaving Vienna and sends for Angelo. He gives Angelo a commission to rule in his place, with full power over death and mercy, and names Escalus as his second. Angelo asks to be tested further first, but the Duke insists and leaves at once, refusing any farewell ceremony because he dislikes crowds. Escalus and Angelo go off together to work out how much power each of them has.","key":["The Duke gives Angelo 'Mortality and mercy in Vienna': the play's two central ideas appear in the first scene.","Angelo asks for 'some more test' of his metal: the play will give him one.","The Duke's reasons for leaving are not explained here, which makes him a puzzle from the start."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is made the Duke's deputy?","o":["Escalus","Angelo","Lucio","Claudio"],"a":1},{"q":"What role is Escalus given?","o":["He is to go with the Duke","He is second to Angelo","He is made Provost","He is sent to Poland"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does the Duke leave without ceremony?","o":["He is in danger in Vienna","He dislikes public applause","Angelo asks him to","He is ill"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Duke say about virtues kept hidden?","o":["They are best kept quiet so that none can envy them","Heaven gives them to us to be used, like torches","They should be shown only to God in prayer at night","They are wasted on a city that does not deserve them"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the Duke say he will send word from?","o":["He will write as things fall out","From the court of the King of Poland","From the monastery outside the walls","From a ship at sea on its way to Rome"],"a":0}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Lucio and two gentlemen trade bawdy jokes about war and disease until Mistress Overdone tells them that Claudio has been arrested and will be beheaded within three days for getting Juliet pregnant. Pompey brings news of a proclamation pulling down the brothels in the suburbs. The Provost leads Claudio and Juliet through the streets. Claudio tells Lucio that Juliet is his wife in all but the public ceremony, which they delayed until her relatives, who hold her dowry, could be won round, and that the new deputy has revived a law unused for nineteen years. He asks Lucio to find his sister Isabella, who is entering a convent today, and send her to plead with Angelo.","key":["Claudio blames 'too much liberty', using the image of rats that eat poison and die of thirst.","Claudio and Juliet are bound by a 'true contract': the law treats a near-marriage as fornication.","Claudio hopes Isabella's youth and her skill in argument will move Angelo."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Claudio arrested?","o":["For debts he cannot pay to Mistress Overdone","For getting Juliet pregnant","For murder","For slandering Angelo"],"a":1},{"q":"Why had Claudio and Juliet not publicly married?","o":["Her family forbade the match because he was too poor","They were waiting to secure her dowry","Claudio was abroad at the wars","The Duke refused them"],"a":1},{"q":"Who does Claudio send Lucio to find?","o":["Escalus, the old lord","The Duke, wherever he is","His sister Isabella","Mariana at the moated grange"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Claudio say about liberty?","o":["Liberty is the gift of a good ruler","Too much liberty brings restraint","Liberty is worth more than any gold","Liberty belongs to all the citizens"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Claudio think Angelo is enforcing old laws?","o":["To punish Claudio for an old private quarrel","To please the Duke who has ordered it in secret","To raise money for the city with heavy fines","To make a name for himself in his new office"],"a":3}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"In a monastery the Duke assures Friar Thomas that he is not hiding because of a love affair. He explains that for fourteen years Vienna's strict laws have gone unenforced, so that the people mock them. Since that was his fault, it would be tyranny for him to start punishing now, so he has given Angelo the job. He asks for a friar's habit so that he can watch Angelo's rule in disguise, adding that Angelo is so strict he barely admits to being human, and that they will see 'If power change purpose, what our seemers be'.","key":["The Duke admits the city's disorder is his own fault.","He lets Angelo take the blame for harshness: he can 'strike home' while the Duke's name stays clean.","His last line sets up the test: power may change what people who only 'seem' good really are."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long have the laws been neglected, according to the Duke?","o":["Four years","Nine years","Fourteen years","Nineteen years"],"a":2},{"q":"What does the Duke ask Friar Thomas for?","o":["Money","A friar's habit","A letter to Angelo","Somewhere to hide Claudio"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Duke say he will test?","o":["Whether power changes what 'seemers' are","Whether Escalus is loyal","Whether the people love him","Whether Claudio will repent"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the Duke compare the unused laws to?","o":["A sword that has grown blunt from use in war","A rod that is feared no more than it is used","A lion that has grown old and lost its teeth","A clock that has stopped and no longer strikes"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Duke say of Angelo's nature?","o":["He is warm and kind to all men","He is a drunkard and a gambler","He scarce admits his blood flows","He is the wisest man in Vienna"],"a":2}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"Isabella, about to join the nuns of Saint Clare, wishes their rules were even stricter. Francisca explains that once she has taken her vows she may not speak to men except in front of the prioress. Lucio arrives and tells Isabella that Claudio is in prison for getting Juliet pregnant. Isabella cries that he should marry her. Lucio explains that Angelo, a man of icy self-control, has revived an old law to make an example of Claudio, and that only her pleading can save him. Isabella doubts her power, but Lucio tells her doubts are traitors, and she agrees to go to Angelo at once.","key":["Isabella wants 'a more strict restraint': she matches Angelo in her love of discipline.","Lucio calls her 'a thing enskied and sainted' and promises to speak sincerely to her.","'Our doubts are traitors' persuades her to act."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Isabella wish about the nuns' rules?","o":["That they were looser","That they were stricter","That they allowed visitors","That they had more holidays"],"a":1},{"q":"What is Isabella's first response to the news about Juliet?","o":["She faints at the news and must be carried in","She says Claudio should marry her","She refuses to help","She blames Lucio"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Lucio describe Angelo's blood?","o":["As fire and brimstone","As 'snow-broth'","As wine","As ink"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Lucio say Isabella's prayers can do?","o":["Bring her brother back to life from the dead","Turn Angelo's heart away from the law of Vienna","Make men give like gods when maidens sue","Win her a place among the saints in heaven"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Lucio say about Isabella's doubts?","o":["Doubts make us wise","Doubts are for the weak","Our doubts are traitors","Doubts keep us safe"],"a":2}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Angelo tells Escalus that the law must not become a scarecrow that birds learn to perch on. Escalus pleads for Claudio, asking whether Angelo himself might not have fallen in the same way, but Angelo answers that being tempted is not the same as falling, and that if he ever offends he should be judged by his own standard. He orders Claudio's execution for nine the next morning. Constable Elbow then brings in Froth and Pompey, accusing them, in muddled words, of some offence against his wife at Mistress Overdone's house. Pompey's endless story about stewed prunes drives Angelo out, leaving Escalus to hear the case. Escalus lets Froth go with a warning, tells Pompey he will be whipped if he comes back, and quietly arranges for Elbow to be replaced. He still grieves for Claudio, but sees 'no remedy'.","key":["Angelo: 'Tis one thing to be tempted ... Another thing to fall' and 'Let mine own judgement pattern out my death'.","Escalus sees the unfairness: 'Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall'.","The comic hearing shows how hard it is to apply strict law to real, messy lives."],"quiz":[{"q":"What image does Angelo use for a law that is never enforced?","o":["A rusty sword","A scarecrow","A sleeping dog","A broken clock"],"a":1},{"q":"When is Claudio to be executed?","o":["At once, before the Duke can hear of it","By nine the next morning","In three days, after a trial","After the Duke returns to Vienna"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Escalus deal with Pompey?","o":["He has him whipped","He sends him to prison","He lets him go with a warning","He makes him a constable"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Angelo say about the jury?","o":["The jury is always fair and must never be questioned","The jury should be chosen from the Duke's own men","A jury may have a thief or two guiltier than the accused","The jury is not needed when the crime is so clear"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Escalus say about Angelo's sentence?","o":["The law is just and Claudio must surely die","Angelo is a wise and merciful judge in this","Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall","Mercy is not the same as weakness in a ruler"],"a":2}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"The Provost asks Angelo whether Claudio must really die and is told to do his job or resign. Isabella arrives with Lucio. She begins weakly, condemning her brother's sin, and almost gives up, but Lucio urges her on. She argues that mercy suits the great better than any symbol of office, that God redeemed souls that were forfeit, and that 'man, proud man, Dressed in a little brief authority' plays tricks that make the angels weep. She asks Angelo to look into his own heart. Angelo is moved and tells her to come back tomorrow. When she offers to 'bribe' him with prayers, he answers 'Amen' aside. Alone, he is horrified to find that he desires her because of her virtue.","key":["'O, it is excellent To have a giant's strength; but it is tyrannous To use it like a giant.'","Isabella's advice to 'Go to your bosom' works too well: Angelo finds temptation there.","Angelo's soliloquy: 'Is this her fault or mine?' He sees himself as the carrion rotting beside the violet."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who urges Isabella on when she nearly gives up?","o":["The Provost","Lucio","Escalus","Juliet"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Isabella offer as a 'bribe'?","o":["Gold","Jewels","Prayers","Her dowry"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Angelo realise in his soliloquy?","o":["That Claudio is innocent","That he desires Isabella","That the Duke is watching","That Escalus is plotting"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Isabella say about great men's power?","o":["Great men are always kind to those who serve them","Power should be used only to punish the wicked","Man, dressed in brief authority, plays fantastic tricks","The Duke has given Angelo too little power to act"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Isabella say about a giant's strength?","o":["It is a gift from heaven that must be used for others","It is excellent to have it, tyrannous to use it like a giant","It is better to be weak and honest than strong and false","It is something that every ruler of a city must have"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"The Duke, disguised as a friar, asks the Provost to let him visit the prisoners. The Provost points out Juliet, who is pregnant, and says Claudio will die tomorrow. The Duke questions Juliet. She says she loves Claudio, that their act was mutual, and accepts that her sin was the heavier. When he warns her against repenting only from shame, she says she repents because it is evil. As the Duke leaves to prepare Claudio, she cries out that her life now holds nothing but horror.","key":["The Duke's first act in disguise is to hear a confession he has no right to hear.","Juliet takes on more of the blame than Claudio, and does it willingly.","The Provost's pity, 'More fit to do another such offence Than die for this', shows sympathy for Claudio."],"quiz":[{"q":"How is the Duke disguised?","o":["As a soldier","As a friar","As a merchant","As a jailer"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Juliet say about her sin compared with Claudio's?","o":["It was lighter","It was heavier","It was the same","It was not a sin"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does this scene take place?","o":["The nunnery","A prison","Angelo's house","The street"],"a":1},{"q":"Why must Juliet's sin be repented properly, says the Duke?","o":["Because the law of Vienna demands it of every woman","Not from fear, but from sorrow for offending heaven","Because her child will be taken from her at birth","Because Claudio has already repented of his own"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Provost say of Claudio?","o":["He is a young man more fit to do another such offence than die for this","He is a hardened criminal who deserves his punishment for it","He is a wise man who has given good advice to others","He is a coward who has begged for his life in prison"],"a":0}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Angelo cannot pray: his words go to heaven while his thoughts are on Isabella. When she comes, he tells her Claudio must die, then edges towards his real meaning with a series of hypothetical questions. Isabella misunderstands, or chooses to, until he says plainly that he loves her and that Claudio will live if she sleeps with him. She threatens to proclaim his hypocrisy to the world. He answers that no one will believe her against his reputation, and that if she refuses, Claudio will die slowly under torture. Alone, she decides that Claudio has such honour that he would give twenty heads rather than let her be shamed, and ends: 'More than our brother is our chastity'.","key":["Angelo uses Isabella's own arguments about frailty against her.","'Who will believe thee, Isabel?': his power protects him completely.","Isabella's couplet 'Then, Isabel, live chaste, and, brother, die' shows her choice before she has spoken to Claudio."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Angelo threaten if Isabella refuses?","o":["To exile her from Vienna and close her convent for ever","To have Claudio tortured before he dies","To arrest Lucio","To close the convent"],"a":1},{"q":"Why can't Isabella expose Angelo?","o":["She has no time before the execution at nine o'clock","No one would believe her against his reputation","She is sworn to silence by her vows","Escalus forbids it"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Isabella expect Claudio to say?","o":["That she should give in to Angelo to save his life at any cost","That he would rather die than see her shamed","That she should flee the city before Angelo can find her","That he will appeal to the Duke himself from prison"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Isabella say she would do rather than yield?","o":["Run away to Rome to find the Duke","Kill Angelo with his own dagger","Marry Angelo in front of the court","Strip herself to death as to a bed"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Isabella say at the end of the scene?","o":["She will go back to the convent tonight","She will tell all of Vienna about Angelo","More than our brother is our chastity","She will give up and do what Angelo asks"],"a":2}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"The disguised Duke tells Claudio to 'Be absolute for death', listing the miseries of life until Claudio says he is ready to die. Isabella arrives, and the Duke and Provost hide to listen. She tells Claudio about Angelo's offer, and Claudio at first says she must not agree. But then his courage breaks, and he describes the horror of death and begs her to save him. Isabella calls him a beast and a coward and says she will pray for his death. The Duke steps in, lies to Claudio that Angelo was only testing her, and sends him to prepare for death. He then tells Isabella about Mariana, whom Angelo abandoned, and proposes the bed trick: Isabella will agree to Angelo's demand, and Mariana will go to him in the dark in her place. Isabella gladly agrees.","key":["'Ay, but to die, and go we know not where': Claudio's terror is the emotional centre of the play.","Isabella's 'Is't not a kind of incest' is her harshest speech, aimed at her brother, not at Angelo.","The Duke now changes from watcher to plotter, introducing Mariana and the bed trick."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Claudio beg Isabella to do?","o":["Escape the city","Agree to Angelo's demand","Write to the Duke","Kill Angelo"],"a":1},{"q":"Who was Mariana engaged to?","o":["Claudio","Lucio","Angelo","Escalus"],"a":2},{"q":"Why did Angelo abandon Mariana?","o":["She loved another man in secret","Her dowry was lost at sea","She became a nun","The Duke forbade it"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Claudio fear about death?","o":["That the Duke will never return to Vienna","To lie in cold obstruction and to rot","That Juliet will marry another man","That his name will be shamed in the city"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Isabella call Claudio when he begs her?","o":["A faithless coward and a dishonest wretch","A loving brother who must be saved by her","A wise man who has made a sad mistake","A foolish boy who knows nothing of sin"],"a":0}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Elbow takes Pompey to prison again. The disguised Duke lectures Pompey on living off vice. Lucio passes by, mocks Pompey and refuses to pay his bail. Left with the 'friar', Lucio jokes that Angelo was spawned by a mermaid and pees ice, and then slanders the absent Duke, claiming he was a drunkard and a lecher who would have spared Claudio out of fellow-feeling. The Duke defends himself and takes Lucio's name. Escalus sends Mistress Overdone to prison; she says Lucio has a child by Kate Keepdown. Escalus tells the 'friar' that Angelo is so severe he is Justice itself. Alone, the Duke condemns Angelo in rhyming couplets and announces the bed trick.","key":["Lucio's slanders, 'the old fantastical duke of dark corners', show how rulers are talked about when they are absent.","'Back-wounding calumny The whitest virtue strikes': the Duke learns what people really say about him.","The Duke's couplets 'He who the sword of heaven will bear Should be as holy as severe' state the play's standard for judges."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Pompey arrested this time?","o":["For theft from a lord's house","For being a pimp","For murder","For debt"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Lucio say about the absent Duke?","o":["That he was a saint who lived only on bread and water","That he was a drunkard and a lecher","That he was a soldier who loved war more than law","That he has died abroad"],"a":1},{"q":"Who reveals that Lucio has a child by Kate Keepdown?","o":["Pompey","Mistress Overdone","Elbow","Escalus"],"a":1},{"q":"Who does Lucio say Angelo was made by?","o":["A great lord and a nun","A king and a queen of Spain","Two old men in a monastery","A sea-maid and two stockfish"],"a":3},{"q":"What does the Duke say in his rhyming speech?","o":["A ruler must be feared before he can be loved","He who bears the sword of heaven should be holy","Every law should be enforced to the full letter","The Duke should never have left his city at all"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"A boy sings to Mariana about broken promises. The Duke arrives and Mariana sends the boy away, embarrassed to be found enjoying music. Isabella brings news: Angelo has given her two keys, to his vineyard gate and a little door into his walled garden, and she has promised to come at midnight and stay only briefly. Isabella tells Mariana the plan in private, and Mariana agrees. The Duke assures her it is no sin, because Angelo is her husband by their earlier contract.","key":["The song 'Take, O take those lips away' captures Mariana's grief at Angelo's broken vows.","Angelo's two keys and walled garden show how secretly he has planned the meeting.","The Duke's defence of the trick: a 'pre-contract' makes the meeting lawful, unlike Claudio's."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the song about?","o":["War and the soldiers' return","Broken promises of love","Spring","The sea"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Isabella to meet Angelo?","o":["In the prison, in Claudio's cell","In his walled garden","At the nunnery","At the city gate"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does the Duke say the bed trick is no sin?","o":["Angelo will be too drunk to know who is with him","Angelo and Mariana were contracted to marry","Mariana is a widow and free to do as she likes","The Duke commands it and so it cannot be a sin"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Mariana say to the Duke about the music?","o":["She sings it every day to remember Angelo","She was sent the song as a gift from Angelo","She hates the music but the boy loves to sing","It displeased her mirth but pleased her woe"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Isabella say Angelo told her of the meeting?","o":["It must be long, at least until dawn","It must happen with the lights on","It must be short, with a servant waiting","It must take place at his house"],"a":2}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"The Provost offers Pompey release if he will assist the executioner Abhorson, and Pompey agrees. At midnight the Provost shows Claudio his death warrant. The Duke arrives expecting a pardon, since Angelo should now be satisfied, but Angelo's messenger brings an order to execute Claudio by four and send his head by five. The Duke persuades the Provost to execute Barnardine instead and send his head, disguised, to Angelo. When the Provost hesitates, the Duke shows him the Duke's own seal and letters announcing the Duke's return within two days.","key":["Angelo breaks his bargain: he orders Claudio's head even after he believes Isabella has slept with him.","The Duke's confident 'here comes Claudio's pardon' is proved wrong at once.","The Duke has to show his seal: his disguise is not enough to make others act."],"quiz":[{"q":"What job is Pompey given?","o":["Jailer","Executioner's assistant","Messenger","Cook"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Angelo's message order?","o":["Claudio's pardon, signed by Angelo's own hand and seal","Claudio's execution and his head sent by five","Isabella's arrest","The Duke's return"],"a":1},{"q":"How does the Duke convince the Provost?","o":["He bribes him with gold from the Duke's own purse","He shows him the Duke's seal and letters","He threatens him with the Duke's anger","He confesses who he is"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Abhorson think of Pompey as an assistant?","o":["He will make a fine executioner","He is too kind for the trade","He is a good and honest man","A bawd will discredit his mystery"],"a":3},{"q":"What does the Provost say of Barnardine?","o":["He is terrified and begs for mercy","He fears death no more than a drunken sleep","He is innocent of his crime","He has escaped twice before"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Pompey lists all the old customers of Mistress Overdone's house now in prison. Barnardine, called to his execution, refuses to die because he has been drinking all night. The Duke decides it would be damnable to send him to death unprepared. The Provost reports that a pirate called Ragozine, who looks like Claudio, has just died of fever, and his head is sent to Angelo. The Duke writes to Angelo to meet him at the gates. When Isabella comes, the Duke tells her Claudio has been executed, planning to comfort her later, and directs her to accuse Angelo before the Duke at the gates. Lucio, meeting them, repeats his slanders to the friar and admits he once denied fathering a child.","key":["Barnardine's refusal, 'I will not consent to die this day', comically blocks the Duke's plan.","Ragozine's convenient death looks like a stroke of providence, or of plotting.","The Duke lets Isabella believe her brother is dead, to make her comfort greater 'When it is least expected'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Barnardine refuse to be executed?","o":["He is innocent of the murder he was charged with nine years ago","He has been drinking all night and is not ready","He has a pardon from the Duke in his cell","He is ill"],"a":1},{"q":"Whose head is sent to Angelo?","o":["Barnardine's","Claudio's","Ragozine's","Abhorson's"],"a":2},{"q":"What does the Duke tell Isabella?","o":["That Claudio is alive","That Claudio has been executed","That Angelo is dead","That he is the Duke"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does the Duke not tell Isabella Claudio lives?","o":["To make her heavenly comforts of despair","He fears that she will tell Angelo of it","He is not sure himself that Claudio lives","He wants her to forget her brother quickly"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Lucio say about the Duke to the friar?","o":["The Duke was a wise ruler who kept the law","The Duke of dark corners would have spared Claudio","The Duke left because he was afraid of Angelo","The Duke has gone to Poland to find a wife"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Angelo and Escalus puzzle over the Duke's contradictory letters, and his orders to meet him at the gates and to invite anyone with a complaint to present it in the street. Alone, Angelo is tormented by guilt. He thinks Isabella's shame will keep her silent, and that his authority would crush any accusation. He admits he had Claudio killed for fear of revenge, and wishes he had let him live.","key":["Angelo's fear shows in his questions: why meet at the gates, why invite complaints?","'This deed unshapes me quite': guilt has broken his self-control.","'We would, and we would not': he is divided against himself."],"quiz":[{"q":"What worries Angelo about the Duke's orders?","o":["The cost of the Duke's grand entry into the city","The public invitation to complain","The weather","The Duke's guests"],"a":1},{"q":"Why did Angelo have Claudio killed, he says?","o":["The law demanded it and he had no choice but to obey","He feared Claudio would take revenge","Escalus advised it in council after the trial","Isabella asked him to"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Angelo feel at the end of the scene?","o":["Triumphant","Calm","Divided and guilty","Angry at Escalus"],"a":2},{"q":"Where must the city's officers meet the Duke?","o":["At the gates of the city","At the Duke's palace","At the prison","At the church"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Angelo say happens when we lose grace?","o":["All our sins are forgiven at the end","We find comfort in our power instead","Nothing goes right; we would, and we would not","We become wiser for having fallen"],"a":2}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"Outside the city, the Duke, now in his own clothes, gives Friar Peter letters and instructions and sends for his friends to bring trumpets to the gate. Varrius arrives to greet him.","key":["The Duke has returned to his own robes: the public stage of Act 5 is being set.","He keeps to a 'special drift' even if the plan has to swerve.","This short scene shows the Duke organising his entrance like a play."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does the Duke give letters to?","o":["Friar Thomas","Friar Peter","Escalus","Lucio"],"a":1},{"q":"What should the Duke's friends bring to the gate?","o":["Horses","Trumpets","Soldiers","Prisoners"],"a":1},{"q":"Who arrives at the end?","o":["Angelo","Varrius","Isabella","Mariana"],"a":1},{"q":"Where should Friar Peter send for the Duke's friends?","o":["Flavius, Valencius, Rowland and Crassus","Lucio, Escalus and the Provost","Angelo, Mariana and Isabella","Elbow, Pompey and Froth"],"a":0},{"q":"How does the Duke tell Friar Peter to proceed?","o":["Wait for the Duke's next word","Tell Angelo everything at once","Go back to his monastery","Follow the letters' instructions"],"a":3}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"Isabella tells Mariana she dislikes speaking so indirectly, but the friar has advised it. He has also warned her that he may seem to speak against her, as bitter medicine with a sweet end. Friar Peter arrives and leads them to a place where the Duke cannot pass them by. The trumpets have sounded twice.","key":["Isabella hates the indirection: 'I would say the truth'.","She is told to accuse Angelo of what Mariana actually did: the plan asks her to lie publicly.","The warning that the friar may turn against her prepares for Act 5."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Isabella dislike about the plan?","o":["Its danger to her and to the friar who planned it","Speaking indirectly","Its cost","Lying to Lucio and the other gentlemen at the gate"],"a":1},{"q":"What has the friar warned Isabella about?","o":["That he may seem to speak against her","That Claudio is alive","That Angelo will flee","That Mariana will refuse"],"a":0},{"q":"Who leads Isabella and Mariana to their place?","o":["Lucio","Friar Peter","The Provost","Escalus"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Friar Peter found for the women?","o":["A boat waiting by the river to take them away","A letter from the Duke telling them to wait","A place most fit where the Duke cannot avoid them","A room in the palace to rest and prepare"],"a":2},{"q":"Who has entered the city gate?","o":["The Duke's army","The Provost's guards","The noble citizens","Angelo's servants"],"a":2}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"The Duke enters Vienna and praises Angelo publicly. Isabella kneels and accuses Angelo of murder, hypocrisy and violating her. The Duke pretends to disbelieve her and sends her to prison. Mariana, veiled, says she is neither maid, widow nor wife, and that Angelo slept with her on the night Isabella describes. Angelo denies it and asks to punish them. The Duke leaves him and Escalus to judge, then returns disguised as Friar Lodowick, who criticises the corruption of Vienna. Lucio accuses the friar of slandering the Duke and pulls off his hood, revealing the Duke. Angelo confesses. The Duke has him married to Mariana, then sentences him to death, 'measure still for measure'. Mariana begs Isabella to kneel with her, and Isabella pleads for Angelo's life. The Provost produces Barnardine, who is pardoned, and a muffled prisoner who turns out to be Claudio. The Duke pardons Angelo, orders Lucio to marry the mother of his child, and twice asks Isabella to marry him.","key":["'An Angelo for Claudio, death for death ... measure still for measure': the title is spoken here.","Isabella pleads for Angelo while still believing he killed her brother.","Isabella never answers the Duke's proposal in words: the ending is left open."],"quiz":[{"q":"How is the Duke revealed?","o":["He takes off his hood","Lucio pulls off his hood","Friar Peter announces him","Isabella recognises his voice"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Mariana say she is?","o":["A widow whose husband died at sea long ago","Neither maid, widow nor wife","A nun who has broken her vows to come","Angelo's sister"],"a":1},{"q":"What is Lucio's punishment?","o":["Death","Exile","Marriage to the mother of his child","Prison for life"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Isabella do when Mariana asks for help?","o":["Kneels and pleads for Angelo's life","Refuses and leaves the court in anger","Asks the Duke to punish Angelo harder","Stays silent and looks away from them"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the Duke offer Isabella at the end?","o":["A convent","Money","Land","Marriage"],"a":3}]}},"notes":{"4":"Line 6 seems to have something missing ('But that, to your sufficiency, as your worth is able, And let them work'). Editors usually guess that a phrase like 'add your authority' has dropped out.","11":"The Duke echoes Christ's parable of the talents and the sermon about not hiding a light under a bushel: gifts are given to be used. Angelo's virtue is about to be tested in use.","29":"Lucio's joke: soldiers pray for peace before meals, but secretly want war, like a pirate who scraped 'Thou shalt not steal' off the Ten Commandments.","41":"Lists were the cheap edges of cloth; velvet was the luxury. 'Three-piled' velvet was the thickest, but 'piled' also puns on going bald from syphilis, the 'French disease'.","47":"'Madam Mitigation': a brothel keeper eases (mitigates) desire. 'Dolours' puns on dollars and pains.","75":"'Groping for trouts in a peculiar river' is a crude image for sex with another man's woman; 'peculiar' means private.","79":"Brothels in the suburbs, outside the city walls, are to be torn down; those in the city survive because a 'wise burgher' (perhaps an investor) speaks for them. The law falls hardest on the poor.","92":"Claudio echoes St Paul (Romans 9): God has mercy on whom he will. Authority acts like God, and Claudio accepts its justice even while he suffers.","105":"A 'true contract' or spousal: in Shakespeare's day a promise to marry made before witnesses was binding, but the couple were expected to wait for the church ceremony before sleeping together.","107":"'Nineteen zodiacs' means nineteen years. In 1.3 the Duke says fourteen years: one of several small inconsistencies in the text.","121":"The Duke compares lax laws to a birch rod that fathers only show their children, until the children laugh at it.","123":"The Duke admits he wants Angelo to enforce the laws so that he himself will not be blamed: 'in th' ambush of my name strike home'.","129":"The Poor Clares, founded by St Clare of Assisi, were among the strictest orders of nuns, living in poverty and silence.","142":"The lapwing draws hunters away from its nest by fluttering elsewhere: Lucio admits he says one thing to women and means another.","150":"Lucio's 'snow-broth' (melted snow) is the first of many images of Angelo as cold and bloodless.","165":"The scarecrow image matches the Duke's birch rod in 1.3: laws that are never used become a joke.","167":"Angelo's 'Let mine own judgement pattern out my death' is a promise the last scene will hold him to.","178":"Elbow mixes up words: 'benefactors' for malefactors, 'profanation' for profession, 'detest' for protest, 'respected' for suspected, 'Hannibal' for cannibal. Escalus and Pompey enjoy the confusion.","199":"Stewed prunes were served in brothels and were supposed to help against venereal disease. Pompey's rambling story is a parody of evidence in court.","216":"Russian nights in winter were proverbially long. Angelo has no patience for comedy.","260":"Pompey Bum: 'Pompey the Great' was the Roman general beaten by Julius Caesar, which is why Escalus threatens to be 'a shrewd Caesar' to him.","269":"'Threepence a bay': if the law is enforced, Vienna will be so empty that the best house will rent for almost nothing.","289":"Escalus repeats a common argument for strictness: pardon only breeds more crime.","321":"Angelo insists that a crime cannot be separated from the person who commits it.","339":"Isabella's strongest argument is Christian: all souls were forfeit through Adam's sin, and Christ redeemed them. A judge who expects mercy from God should show it.","346":"A famous line of Isabella's: power is not wrong, but using all of it is.","348":"Jove (Jupiter) throws thunderbolts. Isabella pictures petty officials using thunder all day, and proud man playing tricks before heaven like an ape.","356":"'Go to your bosom': Isabella asks Angelo to look into his own heart. It is exactly what undoes him.","364":"'Shekels' and 'tested gold' (gold proved pure by testing) set worldly bribes against prayers from 'fasting maids'.","368":"Angelo's 'Amen' answers her prayer, but he means he needs heaven to keep him safe from the temptation he is heading into.","373":"The image of the carrion beside the violet: the same sun that makes the flower bloom makes the corpse rot. Angelo sees the corruption as his, not hers.","390":"Juliet's 'as I love the woman that wronged him' shows she shares the blame equally, and loves Claudio still.","404":"'Blood, thou art blood': Angelo admits he is only human. 'Let's write good angel on the devil's horn' plays on his name: an angel was also a gold coin.","421":"Angelo compares fornication to forging coins: making a child outside marriage is like stamping heaven's image on illegal metal.","425":"'Our compelled sins Stand more for number than for accompt': sins we are forced into are counted but not charged against us. Angelo is tempting her with an argument.","438":"Isabella's images of whips as rubies and death as a longed-for bed are startlingly physical, as if her imagination gives away the passion she denies.","448":"Isabella agrees that women are frail, but blames men for taking advantage of them.","457":"'Who will believe thee, Isabel?': Angelo's reputation is his weapon.","459":"Isabella's couplet ending ('More than our brother is our chastity') has divided audiences: some see it as heroic, others as cold.","465":"The Duke's speech is a set piece of consolation: life is a breath, sleep and death are alike, riches are carried like an ass's load. He speaks as a friar but never mentions heaven or salvation.","491":"'Enew' is a term from falconry: the hawk drives its prey into the water. Angelo is an 'outward-sainted' devil, and his inner filth would make him a pond as deep as hell.","506":"Claudio's speech on death draws on images of purgatory, hell and the restless winds that blow souls in Dante's second circle. It is the opposite of the Duke's calm advice.","509":"'Is't not a kind of incest': Isabella accuses Claudio of wanting life from his sister's sexual shame.","519":"The Duke lies to Claudio here: Angelo was not just testing Isabella.","535":"Mariana's brother Frederick drowned with her dowry. Angelo broke off the engagement because the money was gone, and claimed she was unchaste as an excuse.","541":"The bed trick, also used in All's Well That Ends Well, relies on darkness and silence so that the man cannot tell one woman from another.","550":"Pompey compares two 'usuries': pimping (now banned) and moneylending (allowed, and even dressed in fur). The law lets the rich and crafty flourish.","558":"The Duke's riddling couplet wishes that people were really as good as they seem, or that faults could not hide behind seeming.","562":"Pygmalion was a sculptor whose statue came to life: Lucio asks if there are any fresh young women to be had for money.","565":"The 'tub' was the sweating treatment for venereal disease. 'Powdered' means pickled in salt, and also treated with powders.","591":"Lucio's jokes about Angelo: spawned by a mermaid, fathered by stockfish (dried cod), with frozen urine. He cannot believe Angelo is a man.","597":"A clack-dish was a beggar's bowl with a lid that clacked. Lucio's slanders are probably invented, and he is telling them to the Duke himself.","615":"'Eat mutton on Fridays': eat meat on a fast day, and also visit loose women ('mutton').","623":"'Philip and Jacob' is the feast of Saints Philip and James, 1 May.","633":"The Duke's riddling answer: people want novelty, and honesty is so scarce that friendships are spoiled by the 'security' (guarantees) they require.","643":"The Duke's soliloquy is in short rhyming lines (tetrameter couplets), unlike anything else he says. It sounds like a moral fable or a chorus.","647":"The only song in the play, sung for Mariana. A second verse appears in a later play by John Fletcher, so the song may be by Shakespeare, Fletcher, or both.","659":"The walled garden and the vineyard recall the garden of the Song of Solomon and the Bible's vineyards: Angelo's secret place of desire.","680":"The Duke argues that a 'pre-contract' makes Mariana Angelo's wife already. This is the same kind of contract that Claudio and Juliet had, which the law condemned.","700":"The hangman kept his victims' clothes, so every honest man's clothes 'fit' the thief.","743":"The Duke expects the messenger to bring Claudio's pardon: he is wrong, and his plan nearly fails.","746":"Angelo orders Claudio's head to be sent to him, perhaps so that Isabella can never claim her brother back.","775":"Pompey's list of prisoners is a comic roll-call of debtors and brawlers with invented names: Rash, Caper, Three-pile, Deep-vow, Starve-lackey, Shoe-tie. 'For the Lord's sake' was the cry of prisoners begging from the window.","797":"Barnardine's refusal to die breaks the Duke's plan. It is one of the play's funniest moments, but also shows that a man cannot be forced to be ready for death.","808":"Ragozine, a pirate, dies just when needed. Pirates appear in the play's first jokes (1.2), as does the idea of stealing.","822":"The Duke's decision to let Isabella think Claudio is dead is often questioned: is it kind, cruel, or theatrical?","839":"'The old fantastical duke of dark corners': Lucio's phrase is accidentally accurate. The Duke has spent the play hiding in dark corners.","862":"Angelo's soliloquy shows that he thinks he has deflowered Isabella; he does not know about the bed trick.","878":"Friar Peter speaks of trumpets twice sounded: the Duke's entrance has been staged like a play.","891":"Isabella's 'I would fain have said, a maid' pretends she has lost her virginity, as the plan requires.","897":"Isabella's pattern of repeated 'strange' gives her accusation the rhythm of a legal charge.","947":"Neither maid, widow nor wife: Mariana's riddle depends on the bed trick. Lucio's joke that she might be a 'punk' (prostitute) gets him silenced.","962":"Mariana's unveiling matches the Duke's later unhooding: Act 5 is a series of revelations.","978":"'Cucullus non facit monachum' is Latin for 'the hood does not make the monk'. Lucio is right in a way he does not intend.","1002":"The barber's shop 'forfeits' were mock lists of fines for customers who touched the instruments: everyone laughed at them.","1020":"'Thou art the first knave that e'er mad'st a duke': Lucio has literally uncovered the Duke.","1034":"The title comes from the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 7): 'with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you again'. The same sermon begins 'Judge not, that ye be not judged'.","1045":"Isabella argues that Angelo's intention was never carried out, because of the bed trick. She does not yet know Claudio is alive.","1065":"The Duke's first proposal to Isabella comes in the middle of a speech about Claudio. Isabella's answer is not written.","1073":"The Duke's second proposal is in a rhyming couplet. Productions must decide whether Isabella accepts, refuses, or simply stays silent."},"gloss":{"properties":"special qualities, features","pregnant":"well informed, full of knowledge; also obvious","commission":"written authority to act","deputation":"office of deputy","organs":"instruments","scruple":"tiny amount","advertise":"instruct, inform","mortality":"the power of life and death","leavened":"carefully prepared, matured","qualify":"moderate, soften","Aves":"cheers (from Latin 'Ave', hail)","composition":"agreement, terms","kersey":"coarse woollen cloth","three-piled":"with the thickest pile, the best quality","dolours":"pains (with a pun on dollars)","sciatica":"pain in the hip and back (thought a symptom of syphilis)","custom-shrunk":"short of customers","tapster":"barman","burgher":"citizen","demi-god":"half-god","surfeit":"overeating, excess","ravin":"greedily devour","bane":"poison","foppery":"foolishness","denunciation":"public announcement","propagation":"increasing, securing","dower":"dowry","enrolled":"written in the law books","zodiacs":"years","tickle":"insecurely","tick-tack":"a board game (with a sexual pun)","approbation":"trial period as a novice","assay":"try, test","prone":"eager, appealing","complete":"fully armed","bravery":"show, finery","stricture":"strictness","abstinence":"self-denial","athwart":"wrong, crosswise","sith":"since","habit":"religious dress","precise":"strict, puritanical","votarists":"people bound by vows","lapwing":"plover, a bird that lures people away from its nest","enskied":"placed in heaven","fewness":"few words","seedness":"sowing","foison":"plenty, harvest","tilth":"tilling, farming","snow-broth":"melted snow","rebate":"blunt","censured":"sentenced","brakes":"thickets","benefactors":"Elbow's mistake for malefactors, criminals","varlets":"rogues","caitiff":"wretch","Hallowmas":"All Saints' Day, 1 November","All-hallond Eve":"Halloween, 31 October","geld":"castrate","splay":"spay, sterilise","drabs":"whores","carman":"carter","jade":"worn-out horse","forfeit":"one whose life is owed to the law","fornicatress":"woman guilty of fornication","cipher":"zero, nothing","truncheon":"baton, symbol of military command","pelting":"paltry","sulphurous":"fiery, smelling of brimstone","unwedgeable":"impossible to split with a wedge","myrtle":"a small shrub, sacred to Venus","glassy essence":"fragile soul","spleens":"tempers, fits of laughter","choleric":"angry","shekels":"coins (Hebrew money)","carrion":"rotting flesh","strumpet":"whore","fond":"foolish, infatuated","flaws":"sudden gusts; faults","blistered":"scarred","benedicite":"bless you (Latin)","respites":"spares, delays","sere":"dry, withered","plume":"feather","obsequious":"dutiful, fawning","pose":"puzzle with a question","accompt":"account, reckoning","feodary":"partner, accomplice","enshield":"hidden, protected","livery":"uniform","prolixious":"long-drawn-out","calumny":"slander","prompture":"prompting","approof":"approval","skyey":"of the sky, of the stars","serpigo":"a skin disease","rheum":"catarrh, running nose","palsied":"shaking","eld":"old age","leiger":"resident ambassador","durance":"imprisonment","vastidity":"vastness","enew":"drive into the water (falconry)","guards":"trimmings on clothes","perdurably":"everlastingly","obstruction":"stillness of death","thrilling":"piercing with cold","pendent":"hanging in space","dispenses with":"excuses","wilderness":"wildness","resolve":"answer, tell","affianced":"engaged","combinate":"betrothed","avail":"benefit","scaled":"weighed; exposed","grange":"farmhouse","bastard":"a sweet Spanish wine (with a pun)","usuries":"moneylending at interest","maw":"belly","Pygmalion":"sculptor whose statue came to life","trot":"old woman","powdered":"pickled in salt; treated for disease","extirp":"root out","stockfishes":"dried cod","codpiece":"pouch at the front of men's breeches","clack-dish":"beggar's bowl","crotchets":"odd fancies","helmed":"steered","tun-dish":"funnel","untrussing":"undoing his breeches","mutton":"sheep's meat; loose women","censure":"criticism","divines":"priests","entertainment":"reception, welcome","circummured":"walled round","planched":"made of planks","vaporous":"misty","tithe":"tenth part of a harvest, paid to the church","snatches":"quips","gyves":"chains","mystery":"craft, trade","yare":"ready, quick","postern":"back gate","unsisting":"unresting or unresisting (the word is uncertain)","siege":"seat","celerity":"speed","shrift":"confession","countermand":"order cancelling another","commodity":"goods taken instead of cash on a loan","mercer":"cloth dealer","tilter":"jouster","billets":"logs","ward":"cell","gravel":"hard, stony","journal":"daily","woodman":"hunter (of women)","medlar":"a fruit eaten rotten; a loose woman","burr":"a sticky seed head","unpregnant":"slow-witted","credent":"believable","blench":"swerve","hent":"taken, occupied","rasure":"erasing","vail":"lower","refelled":"refused","concupiscible":"lustful","suborned":"bribed to give false evidence","swinged":"beaten","punk":"prostitute","proportions":"amount of dowry","informal":"out of their wits","touse":"pull apart","forfeits":"lists of fines","giglets":"flirts","pox":"plague (often syphilis)","sheep-biting":"thieving, sneaking","cuckold":"a man whose wife is unfaithful","quit":"repay; acquit","gratulate":"worth celebrating"}};
const PARTS={"4":[[0,4,"To lecture you on the principles of government would only make me look as if I loved the sound of my own voice, since I know your own knowledge of it goes beyond any advice I could give."],[5,6,"So all that is left is to add authority to your ability, and let the two work together."],[7,12,"You know our people's character, our city's institutions and the workings of public justice as well as anyone I can remember, in both theory and practice. Here is your commission; do not stray from it. (To an Attendant) Go and tell Angelo to come here."]],"11":[[0,5,"Angelo, there is something in the way you live that tells anyone watching your whole story. You and your gifts do not belong to you so completely that you may spend yourself on your virtues, or they on you."],[6,14,"Heaven treats us the way we treat torches: we do not light them for their own sake. If our virtues did not shine out from us, we might as well not have them. Fine spirits are given for fine purposes, and nature never lends even the smallest piece of her excellence without claiming, like a careful lender, both thanks and interest."],[15,15,"But I am speaking to a man who could teach me my own part."],[16,21,"So take this, Angelo. While I am away, be me in full. Death and mercy in Vienna live in your tongue and your heart. Old Escalus, though he was asked first, is your second in command. Take your commission."]],"13":[[0,2,"No more excuses. I chose you after careful thought, so accept the honour."],[3,8,"I have to leave so quickly that more important matters must wait. I will write to you when time and my affairs allow, telling you how things go with me, and I expect to hear what happens here. So, goodbye."],[9,10,"I leave you to carry out your commissions, and I hope you do it well."]],"15":[[0,4,"I am in too much of a hurry for that, and on my honour you need have no doubts. Your power is as great as mine, to enforce the laws strictly or to soften them as your conscience thinks right."],[5,10,"Give me your hand. I will slip away quietly. I love the people, but I do not like to parade myself in front of them. It may be well meant, but I take no pleasure in their loud cheering and shouting; and I do not think a man with any judgment seeks it. Once again, goodbye."]],"105":[[0,1,"This is my situation. Under a binding contract of marriage, I slept with Julietta."],[2,8,"You know the lady. She is truly my wife, except that we have not made the public announcement in church. We held back from that only so that her dowry, which is still held by her relatives, would come to us; we thought it best to hide our love from them until time had won them over to us."],[9,10,"But as it happens, the secret of our love for each other is now written too plainly on Juliet's body."]],"107":[[0,0,"Unfortunately, yes."],[1,9,"And the Duke's new deputy, whether it is the mistake and glare of being new, or whether he thinks the people are a horse for the ruler to ride, and that a new rider must use the spur at once to show he is in charge, whether the tyranny lies in the office or in the man who holds it, I can't tell; but this new governor"],[10,15,"has woken up every penalty in the statute book that has hung unused on the wall like rusty armour for nineteen years. To make a name for himself he has dug out this sleepy, forgotten law and applied it fresh to me. It must be to make a name."]],"109":[[0,6,"I have, but he can't be found. Please, Lucio, do me this favour. Today my sister is due to enter the convent and begin her trial as a novice. Tell her what danger I'm in. Beg her, from me, to make friends with the strict deputy and try to win him over herself."],[7,11,"I have great hopes of that. In her youth there is a silent, willing language that moves men; and besides, she has a real gift when she uses argument and reason, and she can be very persuasive."]],"119":[[0,3,"My holy sir, nobody knows better than you how I have always loved a quiet, private life, and thought little of going where young people gather and spend money on empty show."],[4,9,"I have handed my complete power and position here in Vienna to Lord Angelo, a man of strict discipline and firm self-denial, and he thinks I have gone to Poland. That is the story I have spread among the people, and they believe it."],[10,10,"Now, good sir, you will want to know why I am doing this."]],"121":[[0,4,"We have strict statutes and harsh laws, the necessary bits and bridles for wild horses, which for fourteen years we have let lapse, like an overgrown lion in a cave that never goes out to hunt."],[5,12,"Foolish fathers tie up a threatening bundle of birch twigs and only display it to frighten their children, never to use it, so that in time the rod is mocked instead of feared. In the same way our laws, never enforced, are as good as dead. Freedom pulls justice by the nose, the baby hits the nurse, and all good order is turned upside down."]],"123":[[0,5,"Too frightening, I fear. Since it was my fault that the people were given such freedom, it would be tyranny for me to punish and hurt them for what I let them do; for when we let wrongdoing pass unpunished, we as good as order it."],[6,9,"That, father, is why I have given the office to Angelo. He can strike hard while hiding behind my name, and my own reputation will not be damaged in the fight."],[10,15,"And to watch how he rules, I will visit both prince and people as if I were a friar of your order. So please, give me the habit and teach me how to carry myself like a real friar. I will give you more reasons for this when we have more time."],[16,20,"Just this one: Lord Angelo is strict. He stands on guard against malice and will hardly admit that blood runs in his veins, or that he would rather eat bread than stone. So we shall see, if power changes a man's aims, what these people who only seem good are really like."]],"150":[[0,0,"That's the point."],[1,6,"The Duke has left here very mysteriously. He led many gentlemen, me among them, to expect military action, but we hear from people who know the inner workings of the state that what he said was miles away from what he really intended."],[7,12,"Lord Angelo now rules in his place, with all his authority. He is a man whose blood is melted snow, who never feels the playful stings and impulses of the senses, but dulls and blunts his natural desires with improving the mind, study and fasting."],[13,19,"To frighten people out of the habit of freedom, which has long run past the terrible law like mice past lions, he has picked out a statute under whose harsh terms your brother's life is forfeit. He has arrested him under it and is following the strict letter of the law to make an example of him."],[20,22,"There is no hope left, unless you can soften Angelo with your sweet pleading. And that is the heart of my business between you and your poor brother."]],"166":[[0,3,"Yes, but still, let us be sharp and cut a little rather than knock a man down and crush him to death. This gentleman, whom I would like to save, had a very noble father."],[4,12,"Just think, your honour, though I believe you are perfectly upright: if in the heat of your own feelings time had fitted with place, or place with desire, or if your passion had been able to get what it wanted, consider whether you might not once in your life have done the very thing you are condemning him for, and brought the law down on yourself."]],"167":[[0,6,"Being tempted is one thing, Escalus; falling is another. I do not deny that a jury of twelve men deciding a prisoner's life may include a thief or two who are guiltier than the man they are trying. Justice seizes what is brought before it. What does the law know about thieves sentencing thieves?"],[7,9,"It is obvious: if we see a jewel, we bend down and pick it up because we see it; but what we don't see, we tread on without a thought."],[10,14,"You cannot excuse his crime because I have had such faults. Instead, tell me this: if I who sentence him ever commit the same offence, let my own judgment set the pattern for my death, and let nothing soften it. Sir, he must die."]],"334":[[0,1,"Too late? Why, no. I can speak a word and take it back again. So believe this:"],[2,6,"none of the trappings that belong to the great, not the king's crown, nor the sword of his deputy, nor the marshal's baton, nor the judge's robe, suits them half so gracefully as mercy does."],[7,9,"If he had been in your place, and you in his, you would have slipped as he did; but he, in your place, would not have been so harsh."]],"343":[[0,3,"The law has not been dead, though it has been asleep. All those people would not have dared to do that wrong if the first person who broke the law had been made to answer for it."],[4,9,"Now it is awake. It notices what is done and, like a prophet, looks into a crystal ball that shows future crimes, whether already planned or newly conceived through carelessness and so on their way to being hatched and born, and it means them to have no further stages, but to end where they are."]],"348":[[0,4,"If great men could thunder like Jove himself, Jove would never have any peace, because every paltry little official would use his sky to thunder in. Nothing but thunder."],[5,7,"Merciful heaven, you would rather split the gnarled oak that no wedge can split with your sharp, sulphurous lightning than the soft myrtle."],[8,13,"But man, proud man, dressed up in a little brief authority, most ignorant of what he is most sure of, his own fragile soul, plays such crazy tricks before high heaven, like an angry ape, that he makes the angels weep; and if they had our tempers, they would laugh themselves to death."]],"373":[[0,2,"Safe from you, even from your virtue! What's this? What's this? Is it her fault or mine? Who sins more, the tempter or the tempted?"],[3,6,"Not her. She doesn't tempt me. It is I who, lying beside the violet in the sunshine, behave like the rotting carcass and not like the flower, and go bad in the same good season that makes it bloom."],[7,10,"Can it be that modesty arouses our senses more than a woman's looseness? When there is plenty of waste ground, do we want to knock down the holy place and set up our sins there? Oh, shame, shame, shame!"],[11,15,"What are you doing, Angelo, and what are you? Do you desire her foully for the very things that make her good? Oh, let her brother live. Thieves have a licence to rob when judges steal themselves."],[16,17,"What, do I love her, that I want to hear her speak again and feast my eyes on hers? What am I dreaming about?"],[18,21,"Oh cunning devil, who baits his hook with a saint to catch a saint! The most dangerous temptation is the one that drives us to sin through loving virtue."],[22,25,"The whore, with all her double power of art and nature, could never once stir my passions, but this virtuous young woman conquers me completely. Until now, when men were lovesick, I smiled and wondered how they could be."]],"404":[[0,6,"When I try to pray and think, my thoughts and prayers go to different places. Heaven gets my empty words, while my imagination, not listening to my tongue, is anchored on Isabel. Heaven is in my mouth, as if I were only chewing on its name, and in my heart is the strong and swelling evil of my desire."],[7,11,"The government I used to study has grown dry and boring, like a good book read too often. Yes, even my seriousness, which, let no one hear me say it, I take pride in, I would gladly trade for a silly feather blown about by the wind."],[12,14,"Oh rank, oh outward show, how often your mere outside and costume frighten fools into awe, and trap even wiser souls into believing your false appearance! Flesh, you are only flesh."],[15,16,"Write \"good angel\" on the devil's horn, and it is no longer the devil's badge."]],"411":[[0,4,"Oh heavens, why is my blood rushing to my heart like this, making it unable to work and robbing every other part of me of the strength it needs?"],[5,7,"This is how foolish crowds behave around someone who faints: they all come to help him and so block the air that would revive him."],[8,11,"And in the same way the subjects of a well-loved king leave their own work and in fawning devotion crowd around him, where their clumsy love can only seem an offence."]],"437":[[0,1,"Suppose there is no other way to save his life (not that I agree to that, or to anything else; I am only putting it as a case for argument):"],[2,6,"suppose that you, his sister, found yourself desired by a man whose influence with the judge, or whose own high position, could free your brother from the chains of the law that binds everyone;"],[7,10,"and suppose there were no earthly way to save him except that either you give up the treasures of your body to this imagined man, or else let your brother suffer. What would you do?"]],"457":[[0,5,"Who will believe you, Isabel? My spotless name, the strictness of my life, my word against yours and my position in the state will so outweigh your accusation that you will choke on your own story, and it will stink of slander."],[6,10,"I have started, and now I give my desire its head. Make your consent fit my sharp hunger. Put aside all your fussiness and long blushes that drive away what they really invite. Save your brother by giving your body to my will;"],[11,15,"or else he will not only die, but your unkindness will stretch his death out into lingering torture. Answer me tomorrow, or, by the passion that now rules me most, I will be a tyrant to him."],[16,16,"As for you, say what you like: my lies outweigh your truth."]],"459":[[0,6,"Who can I complain to? If I told this, who would believe me? Oh, dangerous mouths, that keep one and the same tongue for condemning or approving, making the law bow to their will and hooking right and wrong alike onto their appetite, to follow wherever it pulls!"],[7,12,"I'll go to my brother. Though he fell through the urging of his blood, he has such a sense of honour that, if he had twenty heads to lay down on twenty bloody blocks, he would give them all up before his sister should lower her body to such hateful filth."],[13,16,"Then, Isabel, live chaste, and, brother, die. Our chastity is worth more than our brother. Still, I'll tell him about Angelo's demand, and prepare his mind for death, so his soul can be at peace."]],"465":[[0,1,"Be completely ready for death. Then whichever comes, death or life, will be sweeter. Argue with life like this:"],[2,6,"if I lose you, I lose something only fools would want to keep. You are a breath, a slave to every influence of the stars that hour by hour afflicts this body you live in."],[7,10,"You are simply death's fool, because you struggle to escape him by running, and still run towards him. You are not noble, because all the comforts you enjoy are fed by base things."],[11,14,"You are not brave at all, because you fear the soft, delicate forked tongue of a poor worm. Your best rest is sleep, and you often seek it, yet you are terrified of death, which is no more than sleep."],[15,18,"You are not your own self, because you are made of thousands of grains that come from dust. You are not happy, because you keep striving to get what you haven't got, and forget what you have."],[19,23,"You are not constant, because your temperament changes in strange ways with the moon. If you are rich, you are poor; like a donkey whose back bends under gold bars, you carry your heavy wealth for only one journey, and then death unloads you."],[24,27,"You have no friends, because your own children, who call you father, the very offspring of your body, curse gout, skin disease and catarrh for not finishing you off sooner."],[28,33,"You have neither youth nor age, but only a kind of after-dinner nap dreaming of both. All your blessed youth lives like an old man and begs from shaky old age; and when you are old and rich, you have no warmth, no feeling, no strength and no beauty left to enjoy your riches."],[34,36,"What is there in this that deserves the name of life? Yet in this life a thousand more deaths lie hidden; and still we fear death, which evens all these differences out."]],"506":[[0,5,"Yes, but to die and go we don't know where; to lie cold and stiff and rot; for this warm, feeling, moving body to become a lump of clay; and for the spirit that once delighted to bathe in floods of fire, or live in freezing regions of thick walls of ice;"],[6,10,"to be imprisoned in the invisible winds and blown with restless violence round and round the hanging earth; or to be worse than the worst of those that wild, uncertain imagination pictures howling: it is too horrible."],[11,14,"The most weary and hated life on earth that old age, pain, poverty and prison can lay on us is paradise compared with what we fear in death."]],"509":[[0,4,"Oh, you beast! Oh, faithless coward! Oh, dishonourable wretch! Will you be made a man out of my sin? Isn't it a kind of incest to take your life from your own sister's shame?"],[5,7,"What am I to think? Heaven grant my mother was faithful to my father, because such a twisted, wild offshoot never came from his blood."],[8,11,"I reject you. Die, perish! If just my bending down could save you from your fate, I would let it go ahead. I'll pray a thousand prayers for your death and not one word to save you."]],"541":[[0,3,"This woman I mentioned still feels the love she first felt. His unjust cruelty, which by rights should have killed her love, has instead, like a dam in a stream, made it stronger and wilder."],[4,7,"Go to Angelo. Answer his demand with convincing obedience and agree to everything he asks. Only make these conditions: first, that you do not stay with him long; that the meeting happens in darkness and silence; and that the place is convenient."],[8,12,"Once that is agreed, everything follows. We will advise this wronged woman to keep your appointment and go in your place. If the meeting comes out later, it may force him to make amends to her. And in this way your brother is saved, your honour kept clean, poor Mariana helped, and the corrupt deputy weighed and found wanting."],[13,14,"I will prepare the woman and make her ready for his advances. If you are willing to carry this out, the double good it does will protect the trick from blame. What do you think?"]],"615":[[0,3,"Why? For filling a bottle with a funnel. I wish the Duke we're talking about would come back. This sexless deputy will empty the province with his chastity. Sparrows won't be allowed to nest under his eaves because they're lecherous."],[4,5,"The Duke would have had dark deeds dealt with in the dark; he'd never have dragged them into the light. I wish he'd come back! Why, this Claudio is condemned for undoing his breeches."],[6,9,"Goodbye, good friar; please pray for me. The Duke, I tell you again, would eat mutton (loose women) on Fridays. He's past it now; and yet, I tell you, he'd kiss a beggar woman even if she stank of brown bread and garlic. Say I said so. Goodbye."]],"643":[[0,5,"The man who carries heaven's sword of justice should be as holy as he is severe. He should find the pattern in himself, with grace to stand firm and virtue to act, punishing others no more and no less than he would weigh his own offences."],[6,9,"Shame on the man whose cruel blows kill others for faults he likes to commit himself! Twice and three times shame on Angelo, who weeds out my vice and lets his own grow!"],[10,15,"Oh, what a man may hide inside, though he looks like an angel on the outside! How false appearances, made up of crimes, can deceive the world, pulling in heavy, solid things with flimsy spiders' threads!"],[16,21,"I must use cunning against vice. Tonight his old fiancée, whom he scorned, will lie with Angelo. So disguise will use a disguised woman to pay back his false demand with falsehood, and fulfil an old contract."]],"771":[[0,5,"This letter announces the return of the Duke. You can read it through at your leisure in a moment, and you will find he will be here within two days. Angelo does not know this, because today he is receiving letters with strange news, perhaps of the Duke's death, perhaps of his entering a monastery, but none of it what is written here."],[6,7,"Look, the morning star is calling the shepherd to unfold his sheep. Don't be bewildered about how all this can be. Every difficulty is easy once it is understood."],[8,10,"Call your executioner, and off with Barnardine's head. I will hear his confession at once and advise him for a better place. You are still amazed, but this will settle your mind completely. Come; it's almost broad daylight."]],"775":[[0,2,"I feel as much at home here as I did in our house of business. You'd think it was Mistress Overdone's own place, there are so many of her old customers here."],[3,5,"First, there's young Master Rash. He's in for a loan of brown paper and old ginger worth a hundred and ninety-seven pounds, which he sold for five marks cash. Well, ginger wasn't much in demand then, because all the old women had died."],[6,8,"Then there's a Master Caper, sued by Master Three-pile the cloth dealer for four suits of peach-coloured satin, which has now impeached him as a beggar."],[9,13,"Then we have young Dizzy, young Master Deep-vow, Master Copperspur, Master Starve-lackey the rapier-and-dagger man, young Drop-heir who killed lusty Pudding, Master Forthright the jouster, brave Master Shoe-tie the great traveller, wild Half-can who stabbed Pots, and I think forty more, all big dealers in our trade, and all now begging through the bars \"for the Lord's sake\"."]],"833":[[0,3,"This doesn't hurt him or help you in the least. So stop it, and leave your cause to heaven. Listen to what I say; you will find every syllable of it is the plain truth."],[4,9,"The Duke comes home tomorrow. No, dry your eyes. A brother of our convent, his confessor, tells me so. He has already taken the news to Escalus and Angelo, who are getting ready to meet him at the gates and hand back their power there."],[10,13,"If you can, walk your wisdom down the good path I want it to take, and you will have your heart's desire on this wretch, the Duke's favour, revenge to satisfy you, and honour from everyone."]],"835":[[0,6,"Then give this letter to Friar Peter. It is the one he sent me about the Duke's return. Tell him that, by this sign, I want him to come to Mariana's house tonight. I will tell him everything about her case and yours, and he will bring you before the Duke, and you can accuse Angelo to his face, all the way."],[7,8,"As for poor me, I am bound by a sacred vow and must be absent. Go with this letter."],[9,11,"Command those stinging tears to stop, and keep your heart light. Don't trust my holy order if I lead you wrong. Who's here?"]],"862":[[0,2,"Good night. This deed has unmade me completely. It leaves me witless and dull for any business."],[3,6,"A young woman deflowered, and by a man of high position who enforced the law against that very thing! If her modest shame did not keep her from announcing the loss of her virginity, how she could denounce me!"],[7,9,"But reason tells her not to, because my authority carries such solid credit that no private accusation can touch it without ruining the person who makes it."],[10,13,"He should have lived, except that his wild young blood, feeling the dishonour, might have taken revenge in time to come for being given a disgraced life bought with such shame. And yet I wish he had lived."],[14,15,"Oh, once we have forgotten grace, nothing goes right. We want to, and we don't want to."]],"866":[[0,5,"Deliver these letters for me at the right time. The Provost knows our purpose and our plan. Now that it is under way, keep to your instructions and always hold to our particular aim, even if you sometimes have to swerve from one thing to another as the occasion demands."],[6,9,"Go and call at Flavius's house and tell him where I am. Give the same message to Valencius, Rowland and Crassus, and tell them to bring the trumpeters to the gate. But send Flavius to me first."]],"888":[[0,4,"Oh, your merit speaks loudly, and I would wrong it if I locked it away inside my heart, when it deserves to be carved in brass and kept in a fortress against the teeth of time and the erasing of forgetfulness."],[5,9,"Give me your hand, and let the people see it, so they know that outward courtesy is eager to show the favour held within. Come, Escalus, you must walk on my other side. You are both good supporters."]],"901":[[0,4,"Oh prince, I beg you, as you believe there is a comfort beyond this world, don't dismiss me by thinking I am touched with madness. Don't make something impossible just because it seems unlikely."],[5,9,"It is not impossible that the most wicked wretch on earth could seem as modest, as serious, as just and as perfect as Angelo; and in the same way Angelo, in all his robes, badges, titles and outward forms, could be an arch-villain."],[10,11,"Believe it, royal prince: if he is any less than that, he is nothing; but he is more, if I had more words for wickedness."]],"920":[[0,4,"In short, leaving out the needless detail of how I persuaded him, how I prayed and knelt, how he refused me and how I answered, for this went on a long time, I now begin, with grief and shame, to tell the vile conclusion."],[5,9,"He would not release my brother unless I gave my chaste body to his greedy, unrestrained lust. After much struggle my pity for my brother overcame my honour, and I gave in to him."],[10,11,"But early the next morning, his desire satisfied, he sent a warrant for my poor brother's head."]],"923":[[0,2,"By heaven, foolish wretch, you don't know what you're saying, or else you have been bribed to attack his honour in some hateful plot."],[3,7,"First, his integrity is spotless. Second, it makes no sense that he would pursue so fiercely faults that were his own. If he had sinned like that, he would have judged your brother by himself and not had him executed."],[8,9,"Someone has put you up to this. Confess the truth, and say on whose advice you came here to complain."]],"936":[[0,8,"Well, he may come in time to clear himself. But just now he is ill, my lord, with a strange fever. At his request alone, having learned that a complaint against Lord Angelo was planned, I came here to say, as if from his own mouth, what he knows to be true and false, and what he will prove with his oath and every kind of evidence whenever he is summoned."],[9,12,"First, as for this woman: to clear this worthy nobleman, so publicly and personally accused, you will hear her proved wrong to her face, until she confesses it herself."]],"968":[[0,4,"Noble prince, as surely as light comes from heaven and words from breath, as surely as there is meaning in truth and truth in virtue, I am this man's promised wife, as firmly as words could make vows."],[5,6,"And, my good lord, only last Tuesday night, in his garden house, he slept with me as his wife."],[7,9,"If this is true, let me rise safely from my knees; if not, let me be fixed here for ever, a marble statue!"]],"970":[[0,1,"Yes, with all my heart; and punish them as much as you like."],[2,6,"You foolish friar, and you wicked woman, in league with the one who has gone: do you think your oaths, even if they swore by every single saint, could count as evidence against his worth and good name, which have been sealed by long approval?"],[7,10,"You, Lord Escalus, sit with my cousin and lend him your kind help to find out where this slander comes from. There is another friar who put them up to it; send for him."]],"1002":[[0,3,"Don't be so hot. The Duke would no more dare to stretch this finger of mine than he would dare to rack his own. I am not his subject, nor under the authority of this province."],[4,9,"My business in this state made me an observer here in Vienna, where I have seen corruption boil and bubble until it overflows the pot. There are laws for every fault, but faults are so tolerated that the strong statutes are like the list of penalties hanging in a barber's shop, as much laughed at as noticed."]],"1031":[[0,6,"You are forgiven, Isabel. And now, dear maiden, forgive me as freely. I know your brother's death weighs on your heart, and you may wonder why I kept myself hidden while working to save his life, rather than suddenly revealing my secret power and not letting him be lost like that."],[7,9,"Oh, kindest of maidens, it was the sudden speed of his death, which I thought was coming more slowly, that dashed out my plan. But peace be with him."],[10,12,"A life beyond the fear of death is a better life than one that lives in fear. Take comfort from that: your brother is happy."]],"1034":[[0,3,"As for this newly married man coming here, whose lustful imagination wronged your well-defended honour, you must forgive him for Mariana's sake."],[4,9,"But because he condemned your brother, while he himself was guilty twice over, of violating sacred chastity and of breaking the promise that depended on it, to spare your brother's life, the very mercy of the law cries out loud and clear, even in his own words: \"An Angelo for Claudio, death for death.\""],[10,11,"Haste always pays for haste, and delay answers delay; like repays like, and always measure for measure."],[12,16,"So, Angelo, your crime is plain, and even if you denied it, the denial would do you no good. We condemn you to the very block where Claudio bowed to death, and with the same haste. Take him away."]],"1045":[[0,4,"(Kneeling) Most generous sir, if you please, look on this condemned man as if my brother were alive. I partly think a real sincerity ruled his actions until he looked at me."],[5,6,"Since that is so, don't let him die. My brother received only justice, because he did the thing he died for."],[7,11,"As for Angelo, his act did not carry out his evil intention, and must be buried as an intention that died on the way. Thoughts are not subject to the law, and intentions are only thoughts."]],"1065":[[0,3,"(To Isabella) If he is like your brother, he is pardoned for your brother's sake; and for your lovely sake, give me your hand and say you will be mine, and he will be my brother too. But there will be a better time for that."],[4,8,"By now Lord Angelo realises he is safe; I think I see life coming back into his eyes. Well, Angelo, your sin has paid you back well. Make sure you love your wife; her worth equals yours. I find myself ready to forgive."],[9,13,"And yet there is one here I cannot pardon. (To Lucio) You, fellow, who knew me for a fool, a coward, a man full of lust, an ass, a madman: what have I done to you that you should praise me like this?"]],"1073":[[0,2,"Claudio, make sure you make amends to the woman you wronged. Joy to you, Mariana! Love her, Angelo. I have heard her confession, and I know how good she is."],[3,6,"Thank you, my good friend Escalus, for all your goodness; there is more reward to come, more worth celebrating. Thank you, Provost, for your care and your secrecy; we will give you a worthier post."],[7,9,"Forgive him, Angelo, for bringing you the head of Ragozine instead of Claudio's. The offence pardons itself."],[10,12,"Dear Isabel, I have a proposal that greatly concerns your good. If you will lend it a willing ear, what is mine is yours, and what is yours is mine."],[13,14,"So, take us to our palace, where we will reveal what still remains that you should all know."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"justice":["Justice and the law","Old laws revived, sentences passed, and the question of whether strict justice can ever be fair."],"mercy":["Mercy and pardon","Pleas for mercy, Christian forgiveness, and the pardons of the last scene."],"power":["Power and authority","Who holds power, how it changes them, and who watches the watchers."],"desire":["Desire and sex","Lust, love, fornication and the body, from the brothels to Angelo's garden house."],"seeming":["Seeming and disguise","Hypocrisy, friars' habits, veils and hoods: appearance against reality."],"death":["Death and life","Execution, fear of dying and the value of life."],"marriage":["Marriage and contracts","Pre-contracts, broken promises and forced weddings."],"speech":["Speech, slander and silence","Persuasion, lies, slander, and the silences that end the play."]},"quotes":[{"id":"6","who":"DUKE","text":"Lent him our terror, drest him with our love,","themes":["power"],"note":"The Duke divides his power into fear and love, and gives both away."},{"id":"11","who":"DUKE","text":"Heaven doth with us as we with torches do, Not light them for themselves;","themes":["power","seeming"],"note":"Virtue must be used and seen; Angelo's is about to be tested in public."},{"id":"11","who":"DUKE","text":"Mortality and mercy in Vienna Live in thy tongue and heart.","themes":["justice","mercy","power"],"note":"The play's two great themes are handed to Angelo in one line."},{"id":"12","who":"ANGELO","text":"Let there be some more test made of my metal,","themes":["seeming","power"],"note":"Angelo asks to be tested, a wish the play grants cruelly. 'Metal' puns on 'mettle'."},{"id":"15","who":"DUKE","text":"I love the people, But do not like to stage me to their eyes.","themes":["power","seeming"],"note":"The Duke dislikes public show, yet he will end the play with the most public scene of all."},{"id":"92","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Thus can the demi-god Authority Make us pay down for our offence by weight.","themes":["power","justice"],"note":"Authority behaves like a god, and the punishment is weighed out exactly."},{"id":"95","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"From too much liberty, my Lucio, liberty.","themes":["justice","desire"],"note":"Claudio blames himself and his age: freedom turns into its opposite."},{"id":"95","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Like rats that ravin down their proper bane, A thirsty evil; and when we drink, we die.","themes":["desire","death"],"note":"Desire is a poison that makes us thirstier: the more we indulge, the nearer death."},{"id":"105","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"You know the lady; she is fast my wife,","themes":["marriage"],"note":"By a private contract they are married in all but ceremony."},{"id":"107","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Freshly on me. 'Tis surely for a name.","themes":["power","justice"],"note":"Claudio thinks Angelo is punishing him to make a reputation."},{"id":"109","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"There is a prone and speechless dialect Such as moves men;","themes":["speech","desire"],"note":"Isabella's silent youth will persuade as well as her words, a hint of what moves Angelo."},{"id":"121","who":"DUKE","text":"We have strict statutes and most biting laws,","themes":["justice"],"note":"The Duke admits the laws exist but have not been used."},{"id":"121","who":"DUKE","text":"And liberty plucks justice by the nose,","themes":["justice","power"],"note":"Disorder is pictured as a cheeky child insulting a parent."},{"id":"123","who":"DUKE","text":"Who may in th' ambush of my name strike home,","themes":["power","seeming"],"note":"The Duke lets Angelo do the hard work while he stays clean."},{"id":"123","who":"DUKE","text":"If power change purpose, what our seemers be.","themes":["power","seeming"],"note":"The test the play sets up: what power reveals about people who only seem good."},{"id":"129","who":"ISABELLA","text":"But rather wishing a more strict restraint","themes":["desire","justice"],"note":"Isabella's love of strictness matches Angelo's."},{"id":"142","who":"LUCIO","text":"I hold you as a thing enskied and sainted","themes":["seeming"],"note":"Even Lucio treats Isabella as a saint, not a woman."},{"id":"150","who":"LUCIO","text":"a man whose blood Is very snow-broth;","themes":["desire","seeming"],"note":"The cold image of Angelo is about to melt."},{"id":"156","who":"LUCIO","text":"Our doubts are traitors, And make us lose the good we oft might win By fearing to attempt.","themes":["speech"],"note":"Lucio's good advice launches Isabella's mission."},{"id":"165","who":"ANGELO","text":"We must not make a scarecrow of the law,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Laws that are never enforced become a joke."},{"id":"166","who":"ESCALUS","text":"Let us be keen, and rather cut a little Than fall and bruise to death.","themes":["mercy","justice"],"note":"Escalus wants a sharp, light touch, not a crushing blow."},{"id":"167","who":"ANGELO","text":"'Tis one thing to be tempted, Escalus, Another thing to fall.","themes":["desire","justice"],"note":"Angelo's confident distinction, which he will soon break himself."},{"id":"167","who":"ANGELO","text":"Let mine own judgement pattern out my death,","themes":["justice","death"],"note":"Angelo sets the standard by which he will be judged in Act 5."},{"id":"174","who":"ESCALUS","text":"Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Escalus sees that justice falls unevenly."},{"id":"289","who":"ESCALUS","text":"Mercy is not itself that oft looks so;","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Too much mercy is not really mercy: an argument for strictness."},{"id":"296","who":"PROVOST","text":"All sects, all ages, smack of this vice, and he To die for 't!","themes":["justice","desire"],"note":"Even the jailer thinks the sentence absurd."},{"id":"321","who":"ANGELO","text":"Condemn the fault, and not the actor of it?","themes":["justice"],"note":"Angelo rejects Isabella's distinction between the sin and the sinner."},{"id":"330","who":"ANGELO","text":"Look, what I will not, that I cannot do.","themes":["power"],"note":"Angelo makes his will the limit of his power."},{"id":"334","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Become them with one half so good a grace As mercy does.","themes":["mercy","power"],"note":"Mercy suits the great better than any symbol of office."},{"id":"334","who":"ISABELLA","text":"You would have slipped like him, but he like you Would not have been so stern.","themes":["mercy","justice"],"note":"Isabella asks Angelo to imagine himself in Claudio's place."},{"id":"339","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Why, all the souls that were were forfeit once,","themes":["mercy","justice"],"note":"The Christian argument: God redeemed souls that deserved death."},{"id":"340","who":"ANGELO","text":"It is the law, not I, condemns your brother.","themes":["justice","power"],"note":"Angelo hides behind the law; Isabella will show that he is the law."},{"id":"343","who":"ANGELO","text":"The law hath not been dead, though it hath slept.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The law is personified as a sleeper who has woken."},{"id":"345","who":"ANGELO","text":"I show it most of all when I show justice;","themes":["justice","mercy"],"note":"Angelo claims severity is mercy to future victims."},{"id":"346","who":"ISABELLA","text":"O, it is excellent To have a giant's strength; but it is tyrannous To use it like a giant.","themes":["power","mercy"],"note":"Power itself is not wrong; using all of it is tyranny."},{"id":"348","who":"ISABELLA","text":"But man, proud man, Dressed in a little brief authority,","themes":["power","seeming"],"note":"Authority is a costume, small and short-lived."},{"id":"348","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Plays such fantastic tricks before high heaven As makes the angels weep;","themes":["power"],"note":"Proud man is an angry ape, and the angels weep at him."},{"id":"353","who":"ISABELLA","text":"That in the captain's but a choleric word Which in the soldier is flat blasphemy.","themes":["power","justice"],"note":"Rank decides how words and acts are judged."},{"id":"356","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Go to your bosom, Knock there, and ask your heart what it doth know That's like my brother's fault.","themes":["desire","mercy"],"note":"Isabella's appeal to conscience leads Angelo straight to temptation."},{"id":"357","who":"ANGELO","text":"She speaks, and 'tis such sense That my sense breeds with it.","themes":["desire","speech"],"note":"A pun: her good sense stirs his senses."},{"id":"368","who":"ANGELO","text":"For I am that way going to temptation, Where prayers cross.","themes":["desire"],"note":"Angelo knows exactly where he is heading."},{"id":"373","who":"ANGELO","text":"The tempter or the tempted, who sins most, ha?","themes":["desire"],"note":"Angelo's first question to himself is about blame."},{"id":"373","who":"ANGELO","text":"Do as the carrion does, not as the flower, Corrupt with virtuous season.","themes":["desire","seeming"],"note":"The same sun makes the violet bloom and the corpse rot."},{"id":"373","who":"ANGELO","text":"Dost thou desire her foully for those things That make her good?","themes":["desire"],"note":"Angelo is horrified that virtue itself attracts him."},{"id":"373","who":"ANGELO","text":"O cunning enemy, that, to catch a saint, With saints dost bait thy hook!","themes":["desire","seeming"],"note":"The devil uses a saint to catch a saint."},{"id":"393","who":"DUKE","text":"Then was your sin of heavier kind than his.","themes":["justice","desire"],"note":"The disguised Duke puts the heavier blame on the woman."},{"id":"404","who":"ANGELO","text":"Heaven hath my empty words,","themes":["desire","speech"],"note":"Angelo's prayers have become empty words, like Claudius's in Hamlet."},{"id":"404","who":"ANGELO","text":"Blood, thou art blood.","themes":["desire"],"note":"Angelo finally admits he is human."},{"id":"404","who":"ANGELO","text":"Let's write good angel on the devil's horn.","themes":["seeming"],"note":"A label can hide anything: a pun on his own name."},{"id":"424","who":"ISABELLA","text":"I had rather give my body than my soul.","themes":["desire","death"],"note":"Isabella means martyrdom; Angelo hears something else."},{"id":"438","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Th' impression of keen whips I'd wear as rubies,","themes":["death","desire"],"note":"Isabella's images of martyrdom are strikingly physical."},{"id":"440","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Better it were a brother died at once Than that a sister, by redeeming him, Should die for ever.","themes":["death","mercy"],"note":"Isabella sets eternal damnation against one death."},{"id":"442","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Lawful mercy Is nothing kin to foul redemption.","themes":["mercy","justice"],"note":"Mercy cannot be bought."},{"id":"448","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Women?—Help, heaven! Men their creation mar In profiting by them.","themes":["desire","power"],"note":"Isabella blames men for exploiting women's frailty."},{"id":"451","who":"ANGELO","text":"Plainly conceive, I love you.","themes":["desire","speech"],"note":"After all his hints, Angelo says it plainly."},{"id":"456","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Seeming, seeming! I will proclaim thee, Angelo, look for't.","themes":["seeming","speech"],"note":"Isabella names the play's word for hypocrisy."},{"id":"457","who":"ANGELO","text":"Who will believe thee, Isabel?","themes":["power","speech"],"note":"Reputation silences the truth."},{"id":"457","who":"ANGELO","text":"Say what you can, my false o'erweighs your true.","themes":["power","seeming","speech"],"note":"Power outweighs truth on the scales of justice."},{"id":"459","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Then, Isabel, live chaste, and, brother, die. More than our brother is our chastity.","themes":["death","desire"],"note":"Isabella's choice, made before she sees Claudio."},{"id":"465","who":"DUKE","text":"Be absolute for death.","themes":["death"],"note":"The disguised Duke's advice to Claudio."},{"id":"465","who":"DUKE","text":"Merely, thou art death's fool;","themes":["death"],"note":"Life is death's jester, running towards what it flees."},{"id":"489","who":"ISABELLA","text":"The sense of death is most in apprehension;","themes":["death"],"note":"The fear of death is worse than death itself."},{"id":"490","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"I will encounter darkness as a bride And hug it in mine arms.","themes":["death"],"note":"Claudio's brave answer, which soon gives way."},{"id":"491","who":"ISABELLA","text":"This outward-sainted deputy,","themes":["seeming"],"note":"Angelo is a saint only on the outside."},{"id":"504","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Death is a fearful thing.","themes":["death"],"note":"Claudio's courage cracks in four words."},{"id":"505","who":"ISABELLA","text":"And shamed life a hateful.","themes":["death"],"note":"Isabella answers with a matching half-line."},{"id":"506","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Ay, but to die, and go we know not where;","themes":["death"],"note":"Claudio's terror of the afterlife."},{"id":"506","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"The weariest and most loathed worldly life That age, ache, penury, and imprisonment Can lay on nature is a paradise To what we fear of death.","themes":["death"],"note":"Any life is better than death: the opposite of the Duke's sermon."},{"id":"508","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Sweet sister, let me live.","themes":["death","mercy"],"note":"The plea that breaks Isabella."},{"id":"509","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Is't not a kind of incest to take life From thine own sister's shame?","themes":["desire","death"],"note":"Isabella's harshest words are for her brother."},{"id":"511","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Mercy to thee would prove itself a bawd.","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Mercy herself would become a pimp."},{"id":"529","who":"DUKE","text":"The hand that hath made you fair hath made you good.","themes":["seeming"],"note":"The Duke praises Isabella's beauty and virtue together."},{"id":"537","who":"DUKE","text":"and he, a marble to her tears, is washed with them, but relents not.","themes":["marriage","mercy"],"note":"Angelo's hardness towards Mariana, pictured as stone in the rain."},{"id":"541","who":"DUKE","text":"the doubleness of the benefit defends the deceit from reproof.","themes":["seeming","marriage"],"note":"The Duke justifies the bed trick by its results."},{"id":"554","who":"DUKE","text":"From their abominable and beastly touches I drink, I eat, array myself, and live.","themes":["desire","justice"],"note":"The Duke's lecture to Pompey on living off vice."},{"id":"558","who":"DUKE","text":"That we were all, as some would seem to be, Free from our faults, as faults from seeming, free!","themes":["seeming"],"note":"A wish that people were what they seem."},{"id":"589","who":"LUCIO","text":"it is impossible to extirp it quite, friar, till eating and drinking be put down.","themes":["desire","justice"],"note":"Lucio's point: lust is as natural as hunger."},{"id":"617","who":"DUKE","text":"No might nor greatness in mortality Can censure 'scape.","themes":["speech","power"],"note":"The Duke learns how rulers are slandered."},{"id":"617","who":"DUKE","text":"What king so strong Can tie the gall up in the slanderous tongue?","themes":["speech","power"],"note":"Even a king cannot stop slander."},{"id":"643","who":"DUKE","text":"He who the sword of heaven will bear Should be as holy as severe,","themes":["justice","power"],"note":"The standard for a judge."},{"id":"643","who":"DUKE","text":"O, what may man within him hide, Though angel on the outward side!","themes":["seeming"],"note":"Another pun on Angelo's name."},{"id":"643","who":"DUKE","text":"Craft against vice I must apply.","themes":["seeming","justice"],"note":"The Duke will use cunning to defeat vice."},{"id":"647","who":"BOY","text":"Take, O take those lips away,","themes":["marriage","desire"],"note":"The song of broken vows, sung for Mariana."},{"id":"673","who":"DUKE","text":"O place and greatness, millions of false eyes Are stuck upon thee;","themes":["power","speech"],"note":"Great men are watched and talked about."},{"id":"680","who":"DUKE","text":"He is your husband on a pre-contract.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"The same kind of contract that condemned Claudio now excuses Mariana."},{"id":"743","who":"DUKE","text":"When vice makes mercy, mercy's so extended That for the fault's love is th' offender friended.","themes":["mercy","desire"],"note":"Mercy granted by a sinner is corrupt."},{"id":"754","who":"PROVOST","text":"insensible of mortality and desperately mortal.","themes":["death"],"note":"Barnardine has no sense of death, though he is about to die."},{"id":"797","who":"BARNARDINE","text":"I will not consent to die this day, that's certain.","themes":["death"],"note":"The prisoner who will not play his part."},{"id":"807","who":"DUKE","text":"A creature unprepared, unmeet for death;","themes":["death","mercy"],"note":"The Duke cannot let Barnardine die unready."},{"id":"839","who":"LUCIO","text":"the old fantastical duke of dark corners","themes":["speech","seeming"],"note":"Lucio's slander is accidentally true."},{"id":"862","who":"ANGELO","text":"This deed unshapes me quite,","themes":["desire","seeming"],"note":"Guilt has broken Angelo."},{"id":"862","who":"ANGELO","text":"Alack, when once our grace we have forgot, Nothing goes right; we would, and we would not.","themes":["desire"],"note":"Angelo is divided against himself."},{"id":"891","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Justice, O royal Duke!","themes":["justice","speech"],"note":"Isabella's public cry."},{"id":"899","who":"ISABELLA","text":"for truth is truth To th' end of reckoning.","themes":["speech","justice"],"note":"Truth does not change with power."},{"id":"901","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Make not impossible That which but seems unlike.","themes":["seeming","speech"],"note":"Isabella asks the Duke to see past appearances."},{"id":"947","who":"DUKE","text":"Why, you are nothing then, neither maid, widow, nor wife?","themes":["marriage"],"note":"A woman's identity depends on her relation to men."},{"id":"978","who":"LUCIO","text":"Cucullus non facit monachum,","themes":["seeming"],"note":"'The hood does not make the monk': true of the Duke as well as the friar."},{"id":"1002","who":"DUKE","text":"Where I have seen corruption boil and bubble Till it o'errun the stew.","themes":["justice","desire"],"note":"A 'stew' was a pot and a brothel."},{"id":"1020","who":"DUKE","text":"Thou art the first knave that e'er mad'st a duke.","themes":["seeming","power"],"note":"Lucio has unmasked the Duke."},{"id":"1023","who":"ANGELO","text":"When I perceive your Grace, like power divine, Hath looked upon my passes.","themes":["power","justice"],"note":"The Duke is compared to God, seeing everything."},{"id":"1023","who":"ANGELO","text":"Immediate sentence then, and sequent death Is all the grace I beg.","themes":["death","justice"],"note":"Angelo applies his own standard to himself."},{"id":"1034","who":"DUKE","text":"An Angelo for Claudio, death for death.","themes":["justice","death"],"note":"Strict justice, in Angelo's own terms."},{"id":"1034","who":"DUKE","text":"Like doth quit like, and measure still for measure.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The title line, from the Sermon on the Mount."},{"id":"1043","who":"MARIANA","text":"They say best men are moulded out of faults,","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Mariana's case for forgiving Angelo."},{"id":"1045","who":"ISABELLA","text":"My brother had but justice, In that he did the thing for which he died.","themes":["justice","mercy"],"note":"Isabella's plea for Angelo is legal as well as merciful."},{"id":"1045","who":"ISABELLA","text":"Thoughts are no subjects; Intents but merely thoughts.","themes":["justice","mercy"],"note":"The law punishes acts, not intentions."},{"id":"1058","who":"ANGELO","text":"That I crave death more willingly than mercy;","themes":["death","mercy"],"note":"Angelo would rather die than be forgiven."},{"id":"1065","who":"DUKE","text":"Give me your hand and say you will be mine.","themes":["marriage","speech"],"note":"The Duke's first proposal. Isabella does not answer."},{"id":"1070","who":"LUCIO","text":"Marrying a punk, my lord, is pressing to death, whipping, and hanging.","themes":["marriage","justice"],"note":"Lucio's punishment is marriage."},{"id":"1073","who":"DUKE","text":"What's mine is yours, and what is yours is mine.","themes":["marriage","speech"],"note":"The second proposal, and the play's last unanswered question."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Duke Vincentio of Vienna has let the city's strict laws sleep for years. He hands his power to the austere Angelo, pretends to leave, and stays in the city disguised as a friar to watch. Angelo revives an old law and condemns young Claudio to death for getting his fiancée Juliet pregnant.","Claudio's sister Isabella, about to become a nun, pleads with Angelo for mercy. Angelo desires her and offers Claudio's life in exchange for her body. She refuses. The disguised Duke arranges for Angelo's abandoned fiancée Mariana to take Isabella's place in the dark, and, when Angelo still orders Claudio's head, substitutes the head of a dead pirate.","In the last scene the Duke returns, lets Isabella and Mariana accuse Angelo, and is unmasked as the friar. Angelo is married to Mariana and condemned, then pardoned when Isabella kneels for him. Claudio is revealed alive, Lucio is made to marry, and the Duke asks Isabella to marry him. She does not reply."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Duke hands his power to Angelo","id":11},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Claudio is arrested for getting Juliet pregnant","id":95},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Claudio sends Lucio to fetch Isabella","id":109},{"s":"a1s3","t":"The Duke explains his plan to watch in disguise","id":123},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Lucio persuades Isabella to plead with Angelo","id":156},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Angelo refuses Escalus's plea and orders Claudio's death","id":167},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Escalus deals with Elbow, Froth and Pompey","id":270},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Isabella pleads for mercy","id":348},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Angelo discovers his desire for Isabella","id":373},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The disguised Duke questions Juliet","id":396},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Angelo demands Isabella's body for Claudio's life","id":453},{"s":"a2s4","t":"'Who will believe thee, Isabel?'","id":457},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The Duke tells Claudio to be ready for death","id":465},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Claudio begs Isabella to save him","id":508},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The Duke proposes the bed trick with Mariana","id":541},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Lucio slanders the Duke to the 'friar'","id":597},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The Duke's soliloquy against Angelo","id":643},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Mariana agrees to go to Angelo","id":676},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Angelo orders Claudio's head despite the bargain","id":746},{"s":"a4s2","t":"The Duke shows the Provost his seal","id":769},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Barnardine refuses to die","id":797},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Ragozine's head is sent to Angelo","id":808},{"s":"a4s3","t":"The Duke tells Isabella Claudio is dead","id":827},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Angelo is tormented by guilt","id":862},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Isabella accuses Angelo before the Duke","id":897},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Mariana unveils","id":962},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Lucio pulls off the friar's hood","id":1020},{"s":"a5s1","t":"'Measure still for measure': Angelo condemned","id":1034},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Isabella kneels for Angelo","id":1045},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Claudio is unmuffled; the Duke proposes","id":1065}],"irony":{"12":"Angelo asks for 'some more test' of his metal: he gets one, and fails it.","63":"Lucio says Claudio was 'ever precise in promise-keeping': it is broken promises that will drive the plot.","123":"The Duke calls Angelo 'precise' and a 'seemer' while he himself is about to put on a disguise.","167":"Angelo asks to be judged by his own standard if he ever offends: in Act 5 he is.","289":"Escalus says pardon breeds 'second woe', yet the play ends in pardons for everyone.","340":"'It is the law, not I': within a scene Angelo will break that law himself.","356":"Isabella tells Angelo to look into his heart for a fault like Claudio's. He finds one there, aimed at her.","362":"Isabella offers to 'bribe' Angelo with prayers. He will demand a different payment.","424":"'I had rather give my body than my soul': Isabella means martyrdom, but the words describe exactly what Angelo wants.","519":"The 'friar' assures Claudio that Angelo was only testing Isabella: a lie told by the man who is testing everyone.","726":"The Duke praises Angelo's justice to the Provost just before Angelo's broken promise arrives.","739":"'And here comes Claudio's pardon': it is an order to send his head.","839":"Lucio calls the Duke 'the old fantastical duke of dark corners' to the Duke's face.","929":"Lucio says he would have beaten the friar for slandering the Duke: the slanders were his own.","1018":"Lucio pulls off the hood to expose a 'lying rascal' and exposes himself."},"devices":{"coins":"Coins, stamps and weighing","disease":"Disease and corruption","harvest":"Seeds, harvest and nature","beasts":"Animals and beasts","heaven":"Heaven, angels and devils","clothes":"Clothes, robes and habits"},"dev":{"12":[["coins","Angelo is metal waiting to be stamped with authority, like a coin.","Let there be some more test made of my metal"]],"92":[["coins","Authority makes us pay for offences 'by weight', like gold on scales.","Make us pay down for our offence by weight"]],"95":[["beasts","Rats that swallow the poison set for them: desire as fatal thirst.","Like rats that ravin down their proper bane"]],"107":[["clothes","Old laws hang 'like unscoured armour' on the wall.","like unscoured armour"]],"121":[["beasts","The neglected law is 'an o'ergrown lion in a cave'.","an o'ergrown lion in a cave"]],"144":[["harvest","Juliet's pregnancy as a ploughed field brought to harvest.","Expresseth his full tilth and husbandry"]],"150":[["beasts","Liberty runs by the law 'As mice by lions'.","As mice by lions"]],"165":[["beasts","The law as a scarecrow that birds of prey learn to perch on.","make a scarecrow of the law"]],"339":[["heaven","Souls 'forfeit' and redeemed: the language of debt and salvation.","all the souls that were were forfeit once"]],"348":[["beasts","Proud man 'like an angry ape' before heaven.","like an angry ape"]],"356":[["disease","Authority has a 'medicine' that grows skin over its own sore.","That skins the vice o' th' top"]],"373":[["disease","Angelo rots like carrion in the sun beside the violet.","Do as the carrion does, not as the flower"],["heaven","The devil baits his hook with saints.","With saints dost bait thy hook"]],"404":[["heaven","'Good angel' written on 'the devil's horn': a pun on Angelo.","Let's write good angel on the devil's horn"]],"421":[["coins","Making a child outside marriage is like forging coins with heaven's image.","do coin heaven's image"]],"448":[["coins","Women take 'false prints' like soft wax or metal.","credulous to false prints"]],"457":[["beasts","Angelo gives his 'sensual race the rein' like a horse.","give my sensual race the rein"]],"465":[["beasts","The rich man is 'like an ass whose back with ingots bows'.","like an ass whose back with ingots bows"]],"491":[["beasts","Angelo drives follies away 'As falcon doth the fowl'.","As falcon doth the fowl"],["heaven","Angelo is a 'pond as deep as hell'.","A pond as deep as hell"]],"493":[["clothes","Hell's 'cunning livery' dresses the damned in precise trimmings.","'tis the cunning livery of hell"]],"550":[["clothes","Moneylending wears a 'furred gown' of fox on lambskin.","furred with fox on lambskins too"]],"565":[["disease","The 'tub' was the sweating cure for venereal disease.","she is herself in the tub"]],"591":[["beasts","Angelo spawned by a mermaid or 'two stockfishes'.","begot between two stockfishes"]],"680":[["harvest","'Our corn's to reap, for yet our tithe's to sow'.","Our corn's to reap, for yet our tithe's to sow"]],"763":[["clothes","'Death's a great disguiser': a shaved head hides Barnardine.","death's a great disguiser"]],"1002":[["disease","Corruption boils and bubbles over like a stew.","corruption boil and bubble"]]},"stage":{"palace":"An apartment in the Duke's palace","street":"A street in Vienna","cell":"A room in a monastery","abbey":"A convent of the nuns of Saint Clare","hall":"A room in Angelo's house","prison":"A room in the prison","grange":"Mariana's moated grange","field":"Fields outside the city","gate":"The city gate of Vienna"},"why":{"0":"Shows that the Duke thinks of power as both fear and love.","1":"Sets up the idea that virtue must be tested in action.","2":"Names the play's central pair: death and mercy.","3":"Angelo invites the test that will break him.","4":"Prepares the irony of the Duke's very public final scene.","5":"Shows authority acting like a god, even to its victim.","6":"Claudio accepts blame, which makes his sentence harder to justify.","7":"Links desire with poison and death.","8":"Establishes that Claudio and Juliet are married in all but ceremony.","9":"Suggests Angelo's motive may be ambition, not justice.","10":"Hints that Isabella's power over men lies partly in her silence and youth.","11":"The Duke admits the laws exist but have not been used.","12":"Shows the disorder the Duke has allowed.","13":"Reveals that the Duke wants someone else to take the blame.","14":"States the test the whole play carries out.","15":"Shows that Isabella shares Angelo's love of strictness.","16":"Shows how others see Isabella as a saint.","17":"The image of Angelo's coldness, soon proved wrong.","18":"Gives Isabella the push she needs.","19":"Angelo's view of law: it must be used or it becomes a joke.","20":"Escalus's moderate view of justice.","21":"The distinction Angelo will soon break.","22":"Angelo's own standard for his punishment.","23":"Shows that justice falls unevenly.","24":"The case for strictness: too much mercy breeds crime.","25":"Even the jailer finds the law absurd.","26":"Angelo refuses to separate the sin from the sinner.","27":"Shows Angelo making his will absolute.","28":"Isabella's case that mercy suits rulers best.","29":"Asks Angelo to imagine himself as the accused.","30":"The Christian argument for mercy.","31":"Angelo hides his choice behind the law.","32":"The law personified as a sleeper woken.","33":"Angelo's claim that severity protects future victims.","34":"A key line on the abuse of power.","35":"Authority is a costume, brief and small.","36":"Pride in power is grotesque and makes angels weep.","37":"Rank decides how acts are judged.","38":"Isabella's appeal to conscience starts Angelo's fall.","39":"The moment desire enters, through a pun.","40":"Angelo knows he is going towards sin.","41":"Angelo questions who is to blame.","42":"His key image of corruption.","43":"Virtue itself attracts him.","44":"The devil's trick, as Angelo sees it.","45":"The disguised Duke blames the woman more.","46":"Angelo's prayer has become empty.","47":"Angelo admits he is only human.","48":"Labels and titles can hide evil.","49":"Isabella's words carry a meaning she does not intend.","50":"Shows how physical Isabella's imagination is.","51":"Isabella values her soul above her brother's life.","52":"Mercy cannot be bought.","53":"Isabella blames men for exploiting women.","54":"Angelo's plain declaration.","55":"Isabella names hypocrisy.","56":"Reputation as a weapon.","57":"Power outweighs truth.","58":"Isabella's decision, much debated.","59":"The start of the Duke's consolation.","60":"Life is death's fool.","61":"Fear of death is worse than death.","62":"Claudio's brief courage.","63":"Isabella calls Angelo a saint only on the outside.","64":"Claudio's courage breaks.","65":"Isabella's answer: shame is worse.","66":"The opening of Claudio's speech on death.","67":"Claudio's view: any life beats death.","68":"The plea that turns Isabella against him.","69":"Isabella's most violent words.","70":"Mercy would become a pimp.","71":"The Duke links Isabella's beauty and goodness.","72":"Angelo's coldness to Mariana.","73":"The Duke excuses his trick by its results.","74":"The Duke attacks living off vice.","75":"Seeming against being.","76":"Lust is as natural as hunger.","77":"Rulers cannot escape gossip.","78":"Even kings cannot silence slander.","79":"The standard for a judge.","80":"A pun on Angelo's name.","81":"The Duke chooses cunning.","82":"The song of broken vows.","83":"Great men are watched and talked about.","84":"The contract that excuses the bed trick.","85":"Corrupt mercy.","86":"Barnardine has no sense of death.","87":"Barnardine refuses his part.","88":"The Duke cannot send a soul to death unready.","89":"Lucio's slander is accidentally true.","90":"Guilt has broken Angelo.","91":"Angelo divided against himself.","92":"Isabella's public cry for justice.","93":"Truth does not change.","94":"Look past appearances.","95":"A woman defined by her relation to men.","96":"The hood does not make the monk.","97":"The Duke's verdict on Vienna.","98":"Lucio unmasks the Duke.","99":"The Duke compared to God.","100":"Angelo asks for his own sentence.","101":"Strict justice in Angelo's terms.","102":"The title line.","103":"The case for forgiveness.","104":"Isabella's legal argument for mercy.","105":"Intentions are not crimes.","106":"Angelo prefers death to mercy.","107":"The first proposal, unanswered.","108":"Marriage as punishment.","109":"The final, unanswered proposal."},"arc":{"Strict justice":[6,7,8,3,9,9,6,9,10,7,9,10,8,5,4,3,6],"Mercy":[2,3,2,3,3,6,4,2,1,2,2,1,1,2,3,3,9]},"arcnote":"Justice starts high as the Duke hands over power and Angelo revives the law. It peaks in Act 2, when Angelo refuses both Escalus and Isabella and then turns the law into a bargain, and again in 4.2, when he orders Claudio's head. Mercy is low through the middle of the play: Isabella pleads for it, but it is twisted into blackmail, and she herself refuses it to Claudio. It rises only in the last scene, when Isabella kneels for Angelo and the Duke pardons almost everyone. Some readers think mercy wins; others notice that the Duke uses strict justice as a threat until the last moment.","context":[["Date","Probably written in 1603 or 1604. A play called 'Measure for Measure' by 'Shaxberd' was performed at court on 26 December 1604. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623."],["Sources","The story of a corrupt judge who demands a woman's body in exchange for her brother's life comes from Giraldi Cinthio's Italian tales (1565) and George Whetstone's play Promos and Cassandra (1578). In those versions the sister gives in. Shakespeare makes her a novice who refuses, and adds Mariana and the bed trick."],["James I","James became king of England in 1603. He had written a book on kingship, Basilikon Doron, and was known to dislike crowds. The Duke's wisdom, his dislike of public applause and his concern with law and mercy may have been designed to interest the new king."],["The disguised ruler","Several plays of the early 1600s show a ruler who goes among his people in disguise to see how they really live. The idea was fashionable, and audiences would recognise the device."],["Plague and the suburbs","In 1603 plague closed the theatres, and a proclamation ordered houses in the London suburbs, many of them brothels and taverns, to be pulled down. Pompey's news in 1.2 would have sounded very current."],["Marriage contracts","A spousal or pre-contract, a promise to marry made before witnesses, was binding in law, but couples were expected to wait for a church wedding before living together. Claudio and Juliet, and Angelo and Mariana, are both in this position, yet are treated very differently."],["Fornication and the law","Church courts punished sexual offences with public penance, not death. Puritan writers wanted harsher laws, and in 1650 England did briefly make adultery a capital crime. Angelo's law is extreme, but the debate was real."],["Puritans","Angelo is called 'precise', a common word for Puritans, who were mocked on stage as hypocrites hiding desire behind strict morals."],["Nuns and friars","England had been Protestant for decades, and there were no convents or friaries left. Vienna, a Catholic city, gives Shakespeare nuns, friars and confession, and the Duke's abuse of the confessional would have troubled some viewers."],["Problem plays","In 1896 the critic F. S. Boas grouped Measure for Measure with All's Well That Ends Well and Troilus and Cressida as 'problem plays': comedies whose dark themes and uneasy endings do not fit the usual pattern."],["The title","'Measure for measure' comes from the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 7): 'Judge not, that ye be not judged. For with what judgement ye judge, ye shall be judged: and with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you again.'"],["Revision","Some scholars think the Folio text was lightly revised around 1621, possibly by Thomas Middleton, who may have added the song and moved some short scenes. This could explain small confusions, like the 'nineteen' and 'fourteen' years."]],"critics":{"justice":[["Samuel Taylor Coleridge, Table Talk (1827)","Paraphrased: Coleridge called it the most painful of Shakespeare's plays, and felt the pardon of Angelo baffled the strong demand of justice in the audience."],["F. R. Leavis, 'The Greatness of Measure for Measure' (1942)","Paraphrased: Leavis defended the play as one of Shakespeare's greatest, and argued that the ending is right because it shows how far everyone in it falls short of the judgments they make."]],"mercy":[["G. Wilson Knight, The Wheel of Fire (1930)","Paraphrased: Knight read the play as a parable of the Gospels, with the Duke as a figure of divine providence who teaches each character the meaning of mercy."]],"power":[["Jonathan Dollimore, 'Transgression and Surveillance in Measure for Measure' (1985)","Paraphrased: Dollimore argued that the Duke's spying and staging use the sexual offences of the poor to strengthen state authority, and that the play's comic low-life is controlled rather than set free."]],"seeming":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, Shakespeare's Problem Plays (1950)","Paraphrased: Tillyard argued that the play breaks in two after Isabella and Claudio's scene: the first half is intense poetic drama, the second a mechanical plot of tricks."]],"speech":[["Philip McGuire, Speechless Dialect (1985)","Paraphrased: McGuire took his title from this play and showed how Isabella's silence at the end is 'open', so that each production must decide what it means."]],"desire":[["Kathleen McLuskie, 'The Patriarchal Bard' (1985)","Paraphrased: McLuskie argued that the play only lets women be maids, wives, widows or whores, and that its comic ending depends on that narrow choice."]]},"insults":[{"id":"42","f":"thou'rt a three-piled piece","who":"First Gentleman","n":"Insults about disease and baldness: the gentlemen mock each other as customers of the brothels."},{"id":"54","f":"Thy bones are hollow","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio jokes that syphilis has eaten his friend away."},{"id":"47","f":"Madam Mitigation","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio greets the brothel keeper with a mock title."},{"id":"197","f":"Prove it before these varlets here","who":"Elbow","n":"Elbow calls everyone a villain, including the judges."},{"id":"237","f":"O thou wicked Hannibal","who":"Elbow","n":"He means 'cannibal': Elbow's insults misfire like his law."},{"id":"260","f":"you are Pompey the great","who":"Escalus","n":"Escalus mocks Pompey's backside with a classical joke."},{"id":"324","f":"You are too cold","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio's repeated jibe pushes Isabella to plead harder."},{"id":"348","f":"like an angry ape","who":"Isabella","n":"Isabella insults authority itself, to Angelo's face."},{"id":"456","f":"Seeming, seeming!","who":"Isabella","n":"She attacks Angelo's hypocrisy in a single word."},{"id":"491","f":"is yet a devil","who":"Isabella","n":"The 'outward-sainted deputy' is really a devil."},{"id":"509","f":"O, you beast!","who":"Isabella","n":"Isabella turns on her brother with her fiercest words."},{"id":"509","f":"O faithless coward! O dishonest wretch!","who":"Isabella","n":"Coward, wretch: she denies he is even her father's son."},{"id":"554","f":"Fie, sirrah, a bawd, a wicked bawd","who":"Duke","n":"The disguised Duke lectures Pompey as a moralist."},{"id":"591","f":"begot between two stockfishes","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio mocks Angelo as a cold fish."},{"id":"603","f":"A very superficial, ignorant, unweighing fellow","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio insults the Duke to his face without knowing it."},{"id":"692","f":"he will discredit our mystery","who":"Abhorson","n":"Even the hangman looks down on a pimp."},{"id":"803","f":"O gravel heart!","who":"Duke","n":"The Duke despairs of Barnardine."},{"id":"1001","f":"Why, thou unreverend and unhallowed friar","who":"Escalus","n":"Escalus threatens the 'friar' with the rack."},{"id":"1018","f":"Why, you bald-pated lying rascal!","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio's last insult unmasks the Duke."},{"id":"1009","f":"was the Duke a fleshmonger, a fool, and a coward","who":"Lucio","n":"Lucio repeats his own slanders and pins them on the friar."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"314","f":"You are welcome. What's your will?","n":"Angelo starts with the formal 'you' to Isabella."},{"id":"457","f":"Who will believe thee, Isabel?","n":"Once he threatens her, Angelo shifts to 'thee' and her first name: contempt and intimacy at once."},{"id":"456","f":"I will proclaim thee, Angelo","n":"Isabella answers with 'thee': she no longer treats him as a superior."},{"id":"489","f":"O, I do fear thee, Claudio","n":"Isabella uses loving 'thee' to her brother before the quarrel."},{"id":"509","f":"Wilt thou be made a man out of my vice?","n":"The same 'thou' becomes an insult when she turns on him."},{"id":"1029","f":"Your friar is now your prince.","n":"The Duke speaks to Isabella with formal 'your' as he reveals himself."},{"id":"962","f":"This is that face, thou cruel Angelo,","n":"Mariana uses 'thou' to her husband: as his wife she can."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Angelo refuses the commission","Angelo begins to rule strictly"],"a1s2":["Claudio escapes from prison","Isabella is asked to help"],"a1s3":["The Duke goes to Poland","The Duke stays in disguise"],"a1s4":["Isabella refuses to leave the convent","Isabella goes to Angelo"],"a2s1":["Angelo pardons Claudio","Isabella comes to plead"],"a2s2":["Angelo makes a shameful offer","Angelo pardons Claudio at once"],"a2s3":["Juliet is executed","Angelo meets Isabella again"],"a2s4":["Isabella agrees to Angelo's demand","Isabella tells Claudio of the offer"],"a3s1":["Claudio refuses to let Isabella sacrifice herself","The friar's plan is put into action"],"a3s2":["Lucio is arrested","Mariana agrees to the plan"],"a4s1":["Angelo discovers the trick","Angelo breaks his promise"],"a4s2":["Barnardine is executed","Something goes wrong with the head"],"a4s3":["Isabella learns the truth","Angelo grows fearful"],"a4s4":["Angelo flees","The Duke prepares his return"],"a4s5":["Isabella confronts Angelo in public","The Duke cancels his return"],"a4s6":["The Duke takes Isabella's side at once","The Duke pretends to disbelieve her"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Isabella and Angelo","pts":["Both love strict rules: she wants a harsher convent, he revives a harsh law.","Both are tested by desire, but only Angelo falls.","In the last scene she shows him the mercy he refused her brother."],"ids":[129,165]},{"t":"Claudio and Angelo","pts":["Both slept with a woman they were contracted to marry.","Claudio is condemned to death; Angelo is married and pardoned.","Claudio's sin was love; Angelo's was lust and abuse of power."],"ids":[105,967]},{"t":"Angelo and Escalus","pts":["Angelo applies the law to the letter; Escalus weighs each case.","Escalus warns, Angelo punishes.","Both are deceived by the Duke in Act 5."],"ids":[167,174]},{"t":"The Duke and Lucio","pts":["Both talk too much about others behind their backs.","Lucio slanders the Duke; the Duke spies on everyone.","Lucio's punishment is the forced marriage the Duke offers more gently to Isabella."],"ids":[597,1067]},{"t":"Juliet and Mariana","pts":["Both were contracted to marry and slept with their partners before the ceremony.","Juliet is punished; Mariana's act is praised as 'no sin'.","Both love men who have treated them badly, and stay loyal."],"ids":[390,1043]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","Measure for Measure"],["Written","1603 to 1604; performed at court in December 1604; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Comedy (often called a 'problem play' or tragicomedy)"],["Setting","Vienna: the Duke's palace, Angelo's house, a monastery, a convent, the prison, the streets and the city gate"],["Protagonists","Isabella and the Duke"],["Antagonist","Angelo"],["Inciting incident","Angelo revives an old law and condemns Claudio to death for getting Juliet pregnant"],["Turning point","Angelo offers to spare Claudio if Isabella sleeps with him (2.4); the disguised Duke proposes the bed trick (3.1)"],["Climax","Lucio pulls off the friar's hood, revealing the Duke (5.1)"],["Point of view","Shared, with soliloquies by Angelo, Isabella and the Duke"],["Major conflicts","Justice against mercy; Isabella's chastity against Claudio's life; Angelo's public virtue against his private desire"],["Themes","Justice and mercy, power and authority, sex and desire, seeming and hypocrisy, death, marriage"]],"analysis":{"ISABELLA":["Isabella enters wanting a 'more strict restraint' on the nuns of Saint Clare. Like Angelo, she loves rules, and she begins her plea for Claudio by condemning his sin. Only Lucio's urging ('You are too cold') makes her fight.","Once roused, she is the finest speaker in the play. Her arguments for mercy, that the powerful should use their strength gently ('it is tyrannous To use it like a giant') and that God redeemed souls already forfeit, are some of the most famous in Shakespeare.","Her refusal of Angelo, 'More than our brother is our chastity', and her fury at Claudio ('O, you beast!') divide audiences. To some she is heroic in her integrity; to others she is cold. Her imagery of whips as rubies and death as a bed suggests a passion she cannot admit.","In Act 5 she kneels for the man she believes killed her brother, arguing that intentions are not crimes. Her silence when the Duke proposes twice is the play's great open question: productions have shown her accepting, refusing, or stunned."],"ANGELO":["Angelo is introduced as a man of 'stricture and firm abstinence', whose blood is 'very snow-broth'. He asks for a test of his metal before taking power, and sees the law as a scarecrow that must be used.","His confident distinction, 'Tis one thing to be tempted ... Another thing to fall', collapses within a scene. Isabella's virtue arouses him, and his soliloquies trace his horror: 'Dost thou desire her foully for those things That make her good?'","He becomes the play's tyrant, using his reputation as a weapon ('Who will believe thee, Isabel?') and breaking his promise by ordering Claudio's head. His earlier abandonment of Mariana, for her lost dowry, shows the coldness was there before.","Exposed, he asks only for death. Whether his repentance is real, and whether he deserves Mariana, is left open. The Duke's pardon saves him, but he says almost nothing more."],"DUKE":["The Duke is the play's most debated figure. He admits that Vienna's disorder is his fault, but rather than fix it he hands the harsh work to Angelo and watches in disguise, testing 'what our seemers be'.","As a friar he hears confessions, consoles Claudio with a sermon that never mentions heaven, overhears private talk, and devises the bed trick and the head trick. Some critics see him as a figure of providence, others as a meddler who plays with people's lives.","His plans keep going wrong: Angelo breaks his promise, Barnardine refuses to die. He depends on luck (Ragozine's death) and on others. He also lies, letting Isabella believe Claudio is dead to make her comfort greater.","Act 5 is his production, full of trumpets, veils and unmasking. He judges everyone, sentences Angelo by 'measure still for measure' and then pardons him, and proposes to Isabella. Lucio's slanders, though false, keep the audience asking how much we can trust him."],"CLAUDIO":["Claudio is a young gentleman who has slept with Juliet, his wife in all but the church ceremony. He accepts blame, calling his offence 'too much liberty', but thinks Angelo is punishing him to make a name.","He trusts his sister's 'speechless dialect' and skill in argument to save him, and when he hears Angelo's offer he first says 'Thou shalt not do't'.","His courage then collapses in one of the great speeches about death: 'Ay, but to die, and go we know not where'. His plea 'Sweet sister, let me live' is deeply human, and makes Isabella's harsh reply hard to watch.","After 3.1 he hardly speaks. In Act 5 he is brought in muffled and silent, and never speaks again. Audiences have to imagine his reunion with his sister."],"LUCIO":["Lucio is the play's comic voice: a 'fantastic' (a fashionable fop) who jokes about sex, disease and brothels and seems to know everyone.","He is a real friend to Claudio, and without his coaching Isabella would have given up. He tells her 'Our doubts are traitors'.","He lies constantly. He slanders the absent Duke to the disguised Duke, then denies it and blames the friar. His description of 'the old fantastical duke of dark corners' is false in detail but true in spirit: the Duke does lurk in corners.","He unmasks the Duke by pulling off the friar's hood, and is the only character the Duke cannot forgive. His punishment, marriage to Kate Keepdown, mirrors the marriages forced on others."],"ESCALUS":["Escalus is an old, experienced lord who shows a middle way between Angelo's strictness and the Duke's neglect. He argues that a judge should 'cut a little' rather than crush.","His handling of Elbow, Froth and Pompey is patient and humane: he warns and threatens but does not punish, and quietly finds a replacement for the foolish constable.","He grieves for Claudio but sees 'no remedy', and tells the friar Angelo is 'Justice' itself.","In Act 5 he too is fooled, and is ready to rack the 'friar'. Even the play's most reasonable judge can be misled by appearances."],"MARIANA":["Mariana appears late, in Act 4, but her story is central. Engaged to Angelo, she lost her brother and her dowry at sea, and Angelo abandoned her with a false accusation.","She lives alone at the moated grange, listening to songs of broken vows. Her love for Angelo has only grown stronger.","She agrees to the bed trick because the Duke assures her that her pre-contract makes it lawful. In Act 5 she unveils, reveals the trick, and calls herself 'neither maid, widow, nor wife'.","Once married, she pleads for Angelo's life and asks Isabella to kneel beside her: 'They say best men are moulded out of faults'. Her loyalty is touching, and also troubling."]},"motifs":[["Coins, stamps and weighing","Angelo's 'metal' stamped with authority, children as coins stamped with heaven's image, punishment paid 'by weight', and the title's measures.",[3,5,48,57,101,102]],["Disguise and seeming","The Duke's friar's habit, Mariana's veil, Claudio muffled, Ragozine's head, and Angelo's 'seeming' virtue.",[14,48,55,63,75,96]],["Sickness and corruption","Brothel jokes about disease, Angelo as carrion, and corruption boiling over in Vienna.",[7,42,90,97]],["Heaven and hell","Angels and devils, saints and souls, redemption and damnation, from Isabella's pleading to Angelo's soliloquies.",[30,36,44,47,80]],["Death and heads","Executions, the head of Ragozine, Barnardine's refusal, and the fear of what lies after death.",[59,64,66,67,87,101]],["Contracts and promises","Claudio's 'true contract', Mariana's pre-contract, Angelo's broken bargain and Lucio's broken promise to Kate Keepdown.",[8,72,84,95,108]]],"symbols2":[["The friar's habit","The Duke's disguise: holy authority used to watch, judge and plot. 'Cucullus non facit monachum'."],["The scarecrow","Angelo's image for a law that is never enforced, which birds learn to perch on."],["The keys and the garden","The two keys Angelo gives Isabella, to his vineyard and his walled garden: his secret desire behind locked doors."],["Ragozine's head","A dead pirate's head passed off as Claudio's: one body can stand in for another, as Mariana stands in for Isabella."],["The veil and the hood","Mariana unveils and the Duke is unhooded: the last scene is built on revelations."],["The scales","Never seen, but behind the title and every image of weighing: justice measured out, measure for measure."]],"bigq":[["Is the Duke a wise ruler or a manipulator?",["He admits his fault and wants Vienna reformed","He tests Angelo rather than simply trusting him","He spies, lies and lets Isabella grieve for a living brother","His plans rely on luck and others' help"],[13,14,81,98]],["Is Isabella right to refuse Angelo?",["She believes she would lose her soul for ever","Angelo breaks his bargain even when he thinks she has kept it, so giving in would have saved no one","Her refusal of Claudio is harsh and cruel","Her imagery suggests passion she will not admit"],[49,51,58,69]],["Does justice or mercy win at the end?",["Everyone is pardoned, even Barnardine","The Duke uses strict sentences as threats until the last moment","Lucio is punished harshly for slander","Isabella's plea for Angelo is the real act of mercy"],[101,102,104,105]],["Is Angelo a hypocrite or a man who discovers his own weakness?",["He sincerely believed he was above temptation","His soliloquies show real horror at himself","He abandoned Mariana for money long before","He breaks his promise and orders Claudio's head"],[21,41,43,57]],["Is Measure for Measure a comedy?",["It ends in marriages and pardons","No one dies except a pirate who was already dead","The marriages are forced or uncertain","Isabella's silence leaves the ending open"],[95,107,108,109]],["What does the play say about sex and the law?",["The brothels and the street show desire cannot be stamped out","Claudio and Mariana's similar contracts are judged differently","The poor suffer the law; the powerful escape it","Women are defined only as maid, wife, widow or whore"],[6,25,76,95]]],"symbols":{"Law":"\\blaws?\\b","Mercy":"\\bmerc(y|iful)\\b|\\bpardon","Justice":"\\bjustice\\b|\\bjust\\b","Death":"\\bdeath\\b|\\bdie\\b|\\bdies\\b","Head":"\\bheads?\\b","Seeming":"\\bseem(s|ing|ed|ers)?\\b"},"stagerules":[["palace","palace"],["monastery","cell"],["nunnery","abbey"],["mariana","grange"],["angelo","hall"],["street","street"],["prison","prison"],["fields","field"],["gate","gate"]],"solo":{"373":1,"404":1,"411":1,"459":1,"617":1,"643":1,"673":1,"862":1},"iago":[["a1s3","The Duke hands power to Angelo and borrows a friar's habit to watch him in secret."],["a2s3","As a friar, he enters the prison and hears Juliet's confession."],["a3s1","He tells Claudio to 'Be absolute for death' and listens in secret to Claudio and Isabella."],["a3s1","He lies to Claudio that Angelo was only testing Isabella."],["a3s1","He tells Isabella about Mariana and proposes the bed trick."],["a3s2","He collects evidence: Lucio's slanders and Escalus's account of Angelo."],["a4s1","He persuades Mariana that the meeting is lawful because of her pre-contract."],["a4s2","He expects a pardon, but Angelo orders Claudio's head instead."],["a4s2","He persuades the Provost to send Barnardine's head, showing the Duke's seal."],["a4s3","Barnardine refuses to die, and Ragozine's head is used instead."],["a4s3","He tells Isabella that Claudio is dead and directs her to accuse Angelo."],["a4s3","He writes to Angelo to meet him at the gates and invites public complaints."],["a4s5","Back in his own clothes, he sends Friar Peter with letters and gathers his friends."],["a5s1","He praises Angelo publicly and pretends to disbelieve Isabella."],["a5s1","He returns as Friar Lodowick and is unmasked by Lucio."],["a5s1","He marries Angelo to Mariana and condemns him, 'measure still for measure'."],["a5s1","He reveals Claudio, pardons Angelo and proposes to Isabella."]],"rel":{"1":[["DUKE","ANGELO","trust"],["DUKE","ESCALUS","trust"],["CLAUDIO","JULIET","love"],["CLAUDIO","LUCIO","trust"],["ANGELO","CLAUDIO","hate"],["DUKE","ANGELO","deceive"],["ISABELLA","CLAUDIO","love"]],"2":[["ANGELO","ISABELLA","love"],["ISABELLA","ANGELO","hate"],["ESCALUS","CLAUDIO","love"],["LUCIO","ISABELLA","trust"],["DUKE","JULIET","deceive"],["ANGELO","CLAUDIO","hate"],["ESCALUS","POMPEY","hate"]],"3":[["ISABELLA","CLAUDIO","hate"],["DUKE","CLAUDIO","deceive"],["ISABELLA","DUKE","trust"],["LUCIO","DUKE","hate"],["DUKE","ANGELO","hate"],["MARIANA","ANGELO","love"]],"4":[["MARIANA","DUKE","trust"],["DUKE","ANGELO","deceive"],["MARIANA","ANGELO","deceive"],["ANGELO","CLAUDIO","hate"],["PROVOST","DUKE","trust"],["DUKE","ISABELLA","deceive"],["LUCIO","DUKE","hate"]],"5":[["ISABELLA","ANGELO","hate"],["MARIANA","ANGELO","love"],["LUCIO","DUKE","deceive"],["DUKE","ISABELLA","love"],["ISABELLA","CLAUDIO","love"],["ESCALUS","DUKE","hate"],["ANGELO","MARIANA","trust"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Isabella?",[15,28,34,58,69,104]],["How does Shakespeare present Angelo's fall?",[3,21,42,43,57,90]],["How does Shakespeare present justice and mercy?",[19,26,30,34,101,105]],["How does Shakespeare present the Duke?",[4,13,14,81,98,107]],["How does Shakespeare present death?",[59,61,64,66,67,87]],["How does Shakespeare present hypocrisy and seeming?",[14,48,55,63,75,96]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, pun, irony, verse and prose, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of the ending","Referred to the whole play, including the comic scenes","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"place":{"a1s1":"The Duke's palace","a1s2":"Vienna","a1s3":"A monastery","a1s4":"The convent","a2s1":"Angelo's house","a2s2":"Angelo's house","a2s3":"The prison","a2s4":"Angelo's house","a3s1":"The prison","a3s2":"Vienna","a4s1":"The moated grange","a4s2":"The prison","a4s3":"The prison","a4s4":"Angelo's house","a4s5":"Outside Vienna","a4s6":"Vienna","a5s1":"The city gate"},"models":[[34,"Shakespeare presents Isabella's case against Angelo as an argument about the right use of power, not about power itself.","'Excellent' and 'tyrannous' are balanced across the line: the same strength is admired or condemned depending on how it is used.","The simile 'like a giant' makes Angelo a monster from folklore, crushing smaller people without noticing.","Shakespeare's audience, under a new king who wrote about wise rule, would recognise the question of how a ruler should use absolute power.","An alternative reading sees Isabella's sharpness here as close to Angelo's own: she too speaks with total certainty."],[42,"Shakespeare presents Angelo's discovery of desire as a moment of horrified self-knowledge.","The contrast of 'carrion' and 'flower' shows that the same sun, Isabella's virtue, produces opposite effects.","Angelo puts the blame on himself, not on her, which makes him more complex than a simple villain.","Jacobean audiences knew stage Puritans as hypocrites; Shakespeare makes this one aware of his own hypocrisy.","Yet within a scene he uses his knowledge as a weapon, so self-awareness does not lead to self-control."],[58,"Shakespeare presents Isabella's choice as absolute, in the formal sound of a rhyming couplet.","The balanced clauses 'live chaste' and 'brother, die' put the two values side by side, with no room for compromise.","The shift to the royal-sounding 'our' generalises her decision into a principle.","For a religious audience, damnation lasted for ever while death was a moment, so her logic was clear.","Modern audiences often find her cold, especially because she decides before speaking to Claudio."],[66,"Shakespeare presents Claudio's fear of death as raw and physical, in sharp contrast to the Duke's calm sermon.","The verbs 'rot', 'bathe', 'blown' and 'imprisoned' turn the soul into a body that suffers.","The run-on lines rush forward like his panic, broken by the dash before 'tis too horrible'.","Images of fire, ice and wind recall Catholic ideas of purgatory and Dante's hell.","The speech makes Claudio sympathetic, which makes Isabella's reply harder to accept."],[101,"Shakespeare presents the Duke's sentence on Angelo as strict justice, using Angelo's own logic.","The chiasmus 'death for death' and the pun on Angelo's name make the punishment fit exactly.","The rhyming 'measure still for measure' gives the line the force of a proverb or a law.","The title phrase comes from the Sermon on the Mount, which warns 'Judge not': the Duke uses it to judge.","Because the Duke knows Claudio is alive, the sentence is a performance designed to provoke mercy."],[109,"Shakespeare presents the Duke's final proposal as a promise of equality that Isabella does not answer.","The balanced rhyme 'mine is yours, and what is yours is mine' sounds like the words of a marriage vow.","Isabella's silence is the play's last 'speechless dialect', echoing Claudio's hope in Act 1.","She was about to become a nun; marriage to the ruler would change her whole life.","Productions have shown her accepting, refusing, or stunned, so the ending is open."]],"lang":{"un":[["343","The law hath not been dead, though it hath slept.","Though it has slept, the law has not been dead."],["459","More than our brother is our chastity.","Our chastity is worth more than our brother."],["330","Look, what I will not, that I cannot do.","Look, I cannot do what I will not do."],["1045","My brother had but justice, In that he did the thing for which he died.","My brother only got justice, because he did the thing he died for."]],"pun":[["357","She speaks, and 'tis such sense That my sense breeds with it.","sense","pun","Her good sense, her meaning, stirs his senses, his desire."],["12","Let there be some more test made of my metal,","metal","pun","Metal (as in a coin to be stamped) and mettle (his character)."],["404","Let's write good angel on the devil's horn.","angel","pun","A good angel, Angelo's own name, and a gold coin called an angel."],["1002","Till it o'errun the stew.","stew","pun","A cooking pot boiling over, and a stew or brothel."],["615","would eat mutton on Fridays","mutton","pun","Meat on a fast day, and loose women."],["1020","Thou art the first knave that e'er mad'st a duke.","mad'st","pun","Made a duke by unhooding him, and 'made' as in created one."]],"st":[["346","O, it is excellent To have a giant's strength; but it is tyrannous To use it like a giant.",[["excellent","Praise, as if power were a fine thing."],["tyrannous","The turn: use decides whether power is good."],["giant","A monster from folk tales, crushing smaller people."]]],["506","Ay, but to die, and go we know not where;",[["Ay, but","Claudio pushes back against his sister's courage."],["die","The plain, terrible word at the centre."],["know not where","The real fear is not pain but the unknown."]]],["1034","Like doth quit like, and measure still for measure.",[["Like","Repetition makes the punishment mirror the crime."],["quit","Repay, and also acquit: the word hints at pardon."],["measure","The title, from the Sermon on the Mount."]]]]},"dir":[["324","You are too cold.","How should Lucio say this, watching Isabella about to leave?",[["Urgently, in a whisper","He is a loyal friend desperate to save Claudio."],["Mockingly, enjoying her discomfort","He finds the novice's stiffness funny."],["Coaching, like a director","He knows how to work on a man and teaches her."]]],["451","Plainly conceive, I love you.","How should Angelo say this after all his hints?",[["Bluntly, almost angrily","His frustration at her innocence boils over."],["Tenderly, as a real confession","He believes he loves her and is shocked by it."],["Coldly, as a bargain","It is a business offer, not a feeling."]]],["508","Sweet sister, let me live.","How should Claudio say this line?",[["Weeping, on his knees","His fear has stripped him of all dignity."],["Quietly, ashamed","He knows what he is asking."],["Fiercely, holding her","He is angry that she values chastity over his life."]]],["797","I will not consent to die this day, that's certain.","How should Barnardine say this?",[["Drunk and comic","He is a clown who ruins the Duke's neat plan."],["Defiant, sober","He refuses to let the state decide his death."],["Sleepy and indifferent","He genuinely does not care."]]],["1045","Most bounteous sir, Look, if it please you, on this man condemned As if my brother lived.","How should Isabella kneel for Angelo?",[["Slowly, after a long silence","The audience feels the cost of her choice."],["Quickly, moved by Mariana","She acts on compassion before thinking."],["Coldly, as a legal argument","She pleads by reason, not forgiveness."]]],["1073","What's mine is yours, and what is yours is mine.","How should Isabella respond to the Duke's proposal?",[["Takes his hand with a smile","A happy ending: she accepts."],["Turns away in silence","She refuses, or cannot answer."],["Stands still, stunned","The audience is left to decide."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["92","1020"],["457","508","459"],["11","339","1002"],["15","123","558"],["79","82","121"],["105","680","947"],["92","105","1034"],["123","129","451"],["129","1002","1020"],["1034","792","947"],["1034","339","167"],["647","121"]],"cr":{"justice":[["1045","345","1034"],["1045","1023","1034"]],"mercy":[["1023","339","11"]],"power":[["348","457","79"]],"seeming":[["505","508","541"]],"speech":[["1034","891","456"]],"desire":[["947","948","448"]]}}};
